msgid "IDS_PASA104"
msgstr "Langley"

msgid "IDS_PASA104_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The successful use of aircraft carriers in the British fleet sparked great interest in the fleets of other countries, including the American fleet. In 1920–22, the fleet's coal transport USS Jupiter of the Proteus type was converted into an aircraft carrier, receiving the name USS Langley. The aircraft carrier was used for technology development and training of naval aviation pilots.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1936–37, the ship was converted into an aircraft transport, losing a third of its flight deck. During the Second World War, Langley took part in operations in the Pacific, transporting US Army Air Forces aircraft. The former aircraft carrier was heavily damaged by Japanese aircraft off Chila-cap on February 27, 1942, after which it was scuttled by its escort ships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA104_FULL"
msgstr "Langley"

msgid "IDS_PASA104_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PASA104_YEAR"
msgstr "1922"

msgid "IDS_PASA106"
msgstr "Ranger"

msgid "IDS_PASA106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>USS Ranger became the first American ship designed and built as an aircraft carrier. In 1926, a technical specification was formulated for an aircraft carrier with a flush deck and a range of 10,000 miles, with displacement not exceeding 18,000 tons.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The aircraft carrier was built at the shipyards in Newport News, Virginia. In terms of the number of aircraft in its air group, the Ranger was only slightly inferior to larger predecessor carriers. However, cramped conditions, overcrowding, and poor seaworthiness made the Ranger not very suitable for use as a full combat unit, so before the start of World War II, the ship was mainly used for training.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>For most of World War II, the Ranger served in the Atlantic, escorting Arctic convoys from America to Iceland and transporting army units for the landings in Africa. From 1944, the carrier served as a training center.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA106_FULL"
msgstr "Ranger"

msgid "IDS_PASA106_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PASA106_YEAR"
msgstr "1934"

msgid "IDS_PASA108"
msgstr "Lexington"

msgid "IDS_PASA108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>As a result of the Washington Naval Conference, the construction of Lexington-class battlecruisers was canceled, but it was allowed to convert two of them into aircraft carriers. Their flight deck and the outer sides of the hangar were designed as part of the hull structure and provided longitudinal strength for the entire ship, which made it possible to make a single-level hangar completely enclosed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The aircraft carrier USS Lexington was built in Quincy, Massachusetts, and served on the West Coast of the United States until 1940. Before the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor, Lexington put to sea to transport U.S. Marine Corps dive bombers to Midway, thus avoiding the attack. In early 1942, the aircraft carrier conducted raids on Japanese territories in the Pacific Ocean.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the Battle of the Coral Sea, Lexington sustained several bomb hits from Japanese carrier-based aircraft, after which a serious fire broke out, and the carrier had to be abandoned. The ship was finished off by torpedoes from the destroyer USS Phelps.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA108_FULL"
msgstr "Lexington"

msgid "IDS_PASA108_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PASA108_YEAR"
msgstr "1927"

msgid "IDS_PASA110"
msgstr "Midway"

msgid "IDS_PASA110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Taking into account the experience of battles in the Pacific Ocean in 1942, the US Navy began designing an aircraft carrier with an armored hangar and flight deck. Among the requirements for the new ships was protection against 203-mm shells from Japanese heavy cruisers and 610-mm torpedoes.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>USS Midway was built at the Newport News shipyard (Virginia) and was commissioned eight days after the end of World War II. Until 1955, this aircraft carrier was the largest ship in the world. In 1947–55, the carrier was engaged in training and patrol missions, and also took part in fleet maneuvers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1965–66, Midway participated in the Vietnam War, striking targets deep inside Vietnamese territory. In 1991, the aircraft carrier took part in Operation Desert Storm. In 2004, USS Midway took its place in San Diego, California, as a museum ship.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA110_FULL"
msgstr "Midway"

msgid "IDS_PASA110_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PASA110_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PASA111"
msgstr "United States"

msgid "IDS_PASA111_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>USS United States was to be the lead ship in a series of five new-type attack aircraft carriers. It was assumed that these carriers would operate jet bombers carrying nuclear weapons, for which the flight deck was integrated into the ship's structural strength. These ships were to operate as part of a task force under the air cover of traditional attack carriers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>USS United States was laid down in April 1949 at the Newport News shipyard (Virginia). Just five days after the keel laying, construction was canceled due to budget cuts and a shift in nuclear aviation priority to long-range land-based bombers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA111_FULL"
msgstr "United States"

msgid "IDS_PASA111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PASA111_YEAR"
msgstr "1952"

msgid "IDS_PASA206"
msgstr "Independence"

msgid "IDS_PASA206_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After Japan's attack on the U.S. in December 1941, the U.S. Navy leadership recognized the need for aircraft carriers. Given that Essex-class ships wouldn't be ready before 1944, it was decided to complete the hulls of Cleveland-class cruisers as light aircraft carriers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ships received a hangar for three dozen aircraft and a flight deck, and bulges were added to compensate for weight. The former cruisers lacked outstanding seakeeping but were advantageous over escort carriers in speed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>USS Independence was built in New York and was originally laid down as USS Amsterdam. During World War II, the ship participated in raids on Rabaul and Tarawa, attacks on the Philippines and Japan, and the Battle of Leyte Gulf. After the war, USS Independence transported American soldiers back home.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA206_FULL"
msgstr "Independence"

msgid "IDS_PASA206_NUMBER"
msgstr "9"

msgid "IDS_PASA206_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PASA208"
msgstr "Yorktown"

msgid "IDS_PASA208_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Yorktown-class aircraft carriers were superior to their predecessor, Ranger, with a larger air group, decent armor, and torpedo protection. The lead ship of the Yorktown class was built at the Newport News Shipyard.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the Battle of the Coral Sea in May 1942, aircraft from the Yorktown sank the light aircraft carrier Shōhō and damaged the Shōkaku. In the process, the Yorktown itself sustained serious damage. Later, the carrier participated in the Battle of Midway Atoll.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In June 1942, Japanese torpedo bombers hit Yorktown with two torpedoes, causing the carrier to lose power and list to port. The captain ordered the crew to abandon ship, but Yorktown did not sink. Towing of the aircraft carrier began, but the Japanese submarine I-168 hit it with a torpedo salvo. Salvage efforts were unsuccessful, and on June 7, the ship sank.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA208_FULL"
msgstr "Yorktown"

msgid "IDS_PASA208_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PASA208_YEAR"
msgstr "1937"

msgid "IDS_PASA210"
msgstr "Essex"

msgid "IDS_PASA210_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After Japan and Italy abandoned limitations on naval construction, the leadership of the US Navy began developing new warships of the main classes. In the late 1930s, a project for a large aircraft carrier with a large air group, enhanced armor, and numerous anti-aircraft armament was created.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>USS Essex was built in Newport News, Virginia, and in the spring of 1943 was transferred to the Pacific Ocean. During World War II, the aircraft carrier participated in strikes on Wake Atoll, Rabaul, the Gilbert Islands, and Kwajalein, and covered landing operations on Tarawa and the Marshall Islands. In 1944, as part of a fast carrier task group, Essex took part in strikes on Saipan, Tinian, and Guam. The aircraft carrier participated in attacks on Palau and Mindanao, strikes on Okinawa and Taiwan, and then fought in the Leyte Gulf.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1962, the aircraft carrier took part in the naval blockade of Cuba. In the 1960s, Essex served as a search and rescue ship for the Apollo space missions.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA210_FULL"
msgstr "Essex"

msgid "IDS_PASA210_NUMBER"
msgstr "24"

msgid "IDS_PASA210_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

