msgid "IDS_PASA510"
msgstr "F. D. Roosevelt"

msgid "IDS_PASA510_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>USS Franklin D. Roosevelt, the second Midway-class aircraft carrier, was built at the New York Naval Shipyard in Brooklyn. In July 1946, from the deck of this carrier, a jet aircraft performed a series of takeoffs and landings for the first time in the US Navy.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1946–53, the ship mainly participated in exercises and diplomatic missions. In 1954–56, the carrier was modernized, receiving an angled deck. In 1956–68, the ship operated in the Mediterranean Sea and Southeast Asia.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1968–69, Franklin D. Roosevelt underwent a second modernization. From 1969 to 1977, the carrier continued its service in the Mediterranean as part of the US Sixth Fleet.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA510_FULL"
msgstr "Franklin D. Roosevelt"

msgid "IDS_PASA510_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PASA510_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PASA518"
msgstr "Enterprise"

msgid "IDS_PASA518_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>USS Enterprise, the second Yorktown-class aircraft carrier, was built in Newport News, Virginia. From 1939, the ship served in the Pacific Ocean, based at Pearl Harbor. During the Japanese attack on the port, the carrier was transporting Marine Corps aircraft to Wake Island.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1942, Enterprise participated in raids on the Marshall Islands and then took part in the famous Doolittle Raid. In June, the ship fought at Midway — aircraft from Enterprise disabled the Japanese carriers Kaga and Akagi and sank the cruiser Mikuma. In September, Enterprise participated in the Battle of Santa Cruz, and then in the Battle of Guadalcanal, where it helped sink the battleship Hiei.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1943–44, the ship participated in most campaigns in the Pacific. In June 1944, the carrier took part in the Battle of the Philippine Sea, and in October — in the Battle of Leyte Gulf. In 1945, Enterprise covered the landings on Iwo Jima and Okinawa.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During its service, the aircraft carrier USS Enterprise received 20 battle stars, becoming the most decorated ship of the US Navy in World War II.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA518_FULL"
msgstr "Enterprise"

msgid "IDS_PASA518_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PASA518_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PASA528"
msgstr "Saipan"

msgid "IDS_PASA528_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1943, at the height of World War II, Admiral Ernest King demanded the construction of two light aircraft carriers per year to compensate for expected losses. The first two ships were to be commissioned in December 1945. To simplify design and construction, the new carriers were based on the hull of the Baltimore-class heavy cruiser.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>USS Saipan was built in Camden, New Jersey. Until 1957, the carrier, which was too small for jet aircraft, was used for training maneuvers, patrol and diplomatic missions, as well as for training Navy cadets.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the mid-1960s, Saipan became a communications relay ship and was renamed USS Arlington. In 1967, the ship was deployed to Vietnam, where it served until 1968. The ship participated in the Apollo program as a search and rescue vessel.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA528_FULL"
msgstr "Saipan"

msgid "IDS_PASA528_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PASA528_YEAR"
msgstr "1946"

msgid "IDS_PASA538"
msgstr "Hornet"

msgid "IDS_PASA538_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>USS Hornet, the third Yorktown-class aircraft carrier, was built in Newport News, Virginia. In 1942, the ship was transferred to the Pacific Ocean.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The aircraft carrier took part in the famous Doolittle Raid on Tokyo on April 18, 1942. In June, the carrier fought at Midway, where its aircraft inflicted heavy damage on the carrier Hiryū and took part in the sinking of the cruiser Mikuma. In the Solomon Islands campaign in August, Hornet participated in the capture and defense of Guadalcanal.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the Battle of the Santa Cruz Islands on October 26, 1942, the ship inflicted damage on the Japanese carrier Shōkaku, but itself received several hits from torpedo bombers and dive bombers. Hornet's power plant was disabled, and it was decided to abandon the ship. American destroyers fired nine torpedoes and several hundred 127-mm shells into it, but the carrier stayed afloat. USS Hornet was finally finished off by Japanese destroyers on October 27, 1942.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA538_FULL"
msgstr "Hornet"

msgid "IDS_PASA538_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PASA538_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PASA588"
msgstr "Hornet B"

msgid "IDS_PASA588_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>USS Hornet, the third Yorktown-class aircraft carrier, was built in Newport News, Virginia. In 1942, the ship was transferred to the Pacific Ocean.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The aircraft carrier took part in the famous Doolittle Raid on Tokyo on April 18, 1942. In June, the carrier fought at Midway, where its aircraft inflicted heavy damage on the carrier Hiryū and took part in the sinking of the cruiser Mikuma. In the Solomon Islands campaign in August, Hornet participated in the capture and defense of Guadalcanal.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the Battle of the Santa Cruz Islands on October 26, 1942, the ship inflicted damage on the Japanese carrier Shōkaku, but itself received several hits from torpedo bombers and dive bombers. Hornet's power plant was disabled, and it was decided to abandon the ship. American destroyers fired nine torpedoes and several hundred 127-mm shells into it, but the carrier stayed afloat. USS Hornet was finally finished off by Japanese destroyers on October 27, 1942.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PASA588_FULL"
msgstr "Hornet B"

msgid "IDS_PASA588_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PASA588_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

