msgid "IDS_PCQC006_YAMAMOTO_DESC"
msgstr "Yamamoto Isoroku was a Marshal Admiral and Commander-in-Chief of the Combined Fleet of the Empire of Japan during World War II. He graduated from the Imperial Japanese Naval Academy."

msgid "IDS_PCQC006_YAMAMOTO_NAME"
msgstr "Yamamoto Isoroku"

msgid "IDS_PCQC006_YAMAMOTO_NAME_QUOTES"
msgstr "\"Yamamoto Isoroku\""

msgid "IDS_PCQC010_HALSEY_DESC"
msgstr "Admiral William Frederick Halsey became one of the contributors to the Allied victory in the Pacific. It was he who managed to gain crucial success in the Battle of Guadalcanal and who led the fight for the Solomon Islands that eventually broke a path to Japan. His phrase \"Hit hard! Hit fast! Hit often!\" became the motto of the U.S. Navy in the Pacific theater."

msgid "IDS_PCQC010_HALSEY_NAME"
msgstr "\"Hit hard! Hit fast! Hit often!\""

msgid "IDS_PCQC010_HALSEY_NAME_QUOTES"
msgstr "\"Hit hard! Hit fast! Hit often!\""

msgid "IDS_PCQC014_EPOCHSOFSHIPS_DESC"
msgstr "<p>At all times, the image of a ship had an aesthetic value to it that is incomparable to any other man-made creation. The immense, mysterious, and even charming presence of majestic sailing ships has fascinated people for centuries. Then came the 19th century—a new era of engineering and automation known as the Industrial Revolution that spurred the development of human technology onwards at an unprecedented rate. Graceful carved wood and woven cloth functioning in harmony with nature's breath were replaced at sea by metal and billowing steam.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>At that time, and right up until the beginning of the space era, the ship embodied all outstanding scientific achievements that drove the development of our civilization. The 20th century brought with it unprecedented and ruthless world wars, and it was only natural that warships took center stage in shipbuilding at the time. Yet, people never ceased to admire the regularity and symmetry of their features, the complexity and deadly power of the weapons they carried.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQC014_EPOCHSOFSHIPS_NAME"
msgstr "Five Epochs of the Navy"

msgid "IDS_PCQC014_EPOCHSOFSHIPS_NAME_QUOTES"
msgstr "\"Five Epochs of the Navy\""

msgid "IDS_PCQC015_SHUMSHU_DESC"
msgstr "<p>Shumshu—the northernmost island of the Kuril Archipelago—was where Soviet forces began the Kuril Islands operation in August 1945.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Kurils hold great strategic importance along Eurasia's eastern coast: the islands form an arc around the Sea of Okhotsk and block access to the Pacific. The Russian Empire lost the Kurils to Japan under the 1875 Treaty of Saint Petersburg, receiving Sakhalin in exchange. At that time, Russian expansion focused on the Amur and Primorye regions, shifting south to Manchuria by the early 20th century. Only in the 1930s did the USSR achieve significant economic development and population growth in this region—the Soviet Far East project became one of the era's landmark achievements. Meanwhile, Japan remained influential in the Far East. During WWII, the Kurils served as Japan's rear: carrier strike groups assembled here for the Pearl Harbor attack, and fish processing plants supplying the military operated here.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>At the February 1945 Yalta Conference, it was agreed that the USSR would receive the Kuril Islands and southern Sakhalin (lost in 1905) in exchange for entering the war against Japan. On August 15 at 07:30, forces of the Kamchatka Defense District under Major General A.R. Gnechko received orders from the 2nd Far Eastern Front and Pacific Fleet commanders: occupy Shumshu, Paramushir, and Onekotan Islands.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQC015_SHUMSHU_NAME"
msgstr "Capture of Shumshu Island"

msgid "IDS_PCQC015_SHUMSHU_NAME_QUOTES"
msgstr "\"Capture of Shumshu Island\""

msgid "IDS_PCQC016_MAKAROV_DESC"
msgstr "The Russo-Japanese War broke out in early 1904 in the waters of the Yellow Sea when the Japanese fleet attacked the Russian squadron at Port Arthur. Japanese army units landed in ports along the coast, and transports carrying weapons, provisions, and reinforcements also arrived here. The priority task of the Japanese fleet was the naval blockade of Port Arthur on the Liaodong Peninsula and the destruction of Russian ships threatening to disrupt Japanese maritime communications."

msgid "IDS_PCQC016_MAKAROV_NAME"
msgstr "Naval Theaters of the Russo-Japanese War"

msgid "IDS_PCQC016_MAKAROV_NAME_QUOTES"
msgstr "\"Naval Theaters of the Russo-Japanese War\""

