msgid "IDS_PCQM051_DESC"
msgstr "<p>Isoroku Yamamoto was a Fleet Admiral and the Commander-in-Chief of the Imperial Japanese Navy during World War II, and a graduate of the Naval Academy.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The future naval commander was born into a samurai family. The discipline and warrior spirit of Takano Isoroku, who later became famous under the name Yamamoto, were forged in the Spartan atmosphere of the Naval Academy in Etajima. His first combat experience, while still an unknown officer, was the Battle of Tsushima in May 1905, the decisive naval engagement of the war with Russia. In the hours-long artillery duel between battleships and cruisers of Japan’s young navy and the fleet of one of the world’s leading powers, the cruiser Nisshin—on which Isoroku served—fought alongside battleships and sustained the heaviest damage. An explosion in a main battery turret riddled Takano Isoroku with shrapnel, yet he did not leave his post until the battle was over. The young officer’s bravery was acknowledged by the Commander-in-Chief of the Combined Fleet—Admiral Tōgō.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM052_DESC"
msgstr "After winning the war against Russia, Japan became one of the naval powers. The bright officer was improving his knowledge. He took a training course in the Artillery School and then entered the Naval War College. He was adopted into the Yamamoto family, took their name and became the heir to the well known and influential clan. Through his interest in warfare innovations, studying the latest technical novelties during business trips abroad, and working as the representative of the Japanese Navy in the U.S.A., Yamamoto realized that the future of naval warfare belonged to carrier-based aircraft. He was appointed a head of the first Japanese naval aviation school where new aircraft for the Navy were tested."

msgid "IDS_PCQM053_DESC"
msgstr "In the late 1920s, Yamamoto was appointed Commander of Akagi, the most powerful aircraft carrier of the Japanese Navy. Due to his extensive knowledge, he participated as Rear and Vice Admiral in two international naval conferences to protect the interests of his country. For a while, Yamamoto commanded the First Carrier Division, then, for a few years, he managed key units of the Navy Ministry, which were responsible for carrier-based aircraft development, and initiated the development of several promising aircraft models. In 1939, Yamamoto left his post of Deputy Navy Minister to become Commander-in-Chief of the Combined Fleet, the largest naval force of Japan. Though he was against the aggressive intentions of the Army command, which gained a major influence in the country, the Admiral had to prepare the fleet for its upcoming confrontation with the U.S.A. on the Pacific Ocean. Under his command, a famous tactic of the Japanese Navy was honed: attack the enemy at night using the firepower of cruiser squadrons and destroyer-launched torpedoes."

msgid "IDS_PCQM054_DESC"
msgstr "Admiral Yamamoto was among the naval commanders who foresaw the decisive role of aircraft carriers in the impending naval battles. However, while opposing the construction of super battleships, he recognized the significance of battleships as a symbol and pillar of power for any maritime nation. Among the top brass of the Japanese Navy, the idea of a decisive battle between battleship fleets as the only path to victory in the ocean theater was extremely popular — a kind of new Tsushima. Yamamoto, who had studied the United States well during his service, understood that in a naval confrontation with this industrial giant, Japan's only chance was a surprise air strike on US ships. Thus, the idea of attacking Pearl Harbor, the main base of the US Pacific Fleet, was born. Brilliantly planned by Yamamoto and executed by his brainchild, the 1st Carrier Strike Force, the operation went down in history. Japanese torpedo bombers and bombers achieved impressive results, repeating Admiral Togo's success — seven enemy battleships were disabled, some of them permanently."

msgid "IDS_PCQM055_DESC"
msgstr "After the attack on Pearl Harbor, Admiral Yamamoto commanded a series of brilliant naval operations and took control of vast territories in the Pacific region. He provided guidance from the most massive Japanese battleships: Nagato, Yamato, and Musashi. His thoroughly planned landing operations included victorious naval battles, in which the forces of the U.S.A., U.K., Australia, and Netherlands were utterly smashed. Progressive tactics and effective use of torpedoes were the strengths of Yamamoto's fleet. Cruiser divisions and destroyer flotillas paved the way, aircraft carriers were the main striking force, and battleships waited for a general engagement. However, Yamamoto's concerns that people, warship, and aircraft losses after a confrontation with the U.S.A. couldn't be compensated, had been justified. The victories were followed by defeats: Midway, Guadalcanal, and many others. In April 1943, Admiral Yamamoto was inspecting the key points on the long frontline. U.S.A. surveillance deciphered information about the flights of the Commander-in-Chief. They organized an extensive operation to intercept the aircraft with Yamamoto on board and managed to shoot it down over the jungles of an island in the Pacific Ocean. The prominent Admiral died clutching his samurai sword."

msgid "IDS_PCQM070_DESC"
msgstr "<p>Admiral William Halsey was one of those who ensured the Allied victory in the Pacific War. It was he who achieved the decisive success in the battle for Guadalcanal and led the campaign for the Solomon Islands, paving the way to Japan. The admiral’s phrase “Hit hard! Hit fast! Hit often!” became the motto of the U.S. Navy in the Pacific campaign.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>William Frederick Halsey Jr. was a born sailor. He was born in the small port town of Elizabeth, New Jersey, near New York, into a family of naval officers. Among his ancestors were pirates and whalers—so his path in life, the path to the sea, was predetermined. In 1899, failing to secure admission to the Naval Academy in Annapolis, Halsey Jr. enrolled at the University of Virginia, hoping to join the Navy at least as a physician. From his time at the university, he mainly took away a passion for American football.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1900, Annapolis finally opened its doors to the man who would become one of its most renowned graduates. However, at the Academy, the stubborn and headstrong “Shorty” Halsey did not excel academically—he graduated 42nd out of 62 in the class of 1904. The first ship of his naval career was the battleship Missouri. The significance of this assignment would only become clear more than forty years later.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM071_DESC"
msgstr "<p>Commissioned as an officer in 1906, Halsey participated in the legendary 'Great White Fleet' voyage — a round-the-world cruise of 16 of America's newest battleships, intended to showcase the United States' growing naval power. During the voyage, when visiting Japan, he had the opportunity to board the famous flagship of the Combined Fleet, Mikasa. The battle-honored Japanese battleship did not impress Halsey... After returning from the cruise, the young lieutenant left 'big gun ships' for service on small and fast torpedo boats.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War I, Halsey finally became a ship commander, commanding the destroyer USS Shaw, a Sampson-class destroyer. Finding himself once in command of a detachment of two American destroyers and two British sloops, he later recalled that he was 'as proud as a dog with two tails'. After the war, Halsey's chest was adorned with the Navy Cross.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM072_DESC"
msgstr "<p>In the early 1920s, Halsey, who hardly ever disembarked from a ship during his service, was suddenly appointed as a U.S. naval attaché in Germany and Scandinavia. In 1930, he commanded a division of destroyers. Then, to pave the way to the rank of admiral, Halsey studied at the U.S. Naval War College for senior officers. In 1935, he was assigned as commander of USS Saratoga, one of the most powerful U.S. Navy aircraft carriers of that time. However, this position required completion of an air observer course. Halsey didn't like this perspective, as he thought it would be much better to control the airplane himself rather than being the \"second person in the cockpit\".</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1935, the 52-years-old Halsey completed the full course of a naval pilot, and became the oldest man in the history of the U.S. Navy to earn the Naval Aviator's Wings. Being one of the few at that time, Halsey, same as his future opponent Yamamoto Isoroku, became a big advocate for naval aviation, foreseeing that aircraft carriers will be the greatest power in the future war in the ocean.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM073_DESC"
msgstr "<p>On the day of the Pearl Harbor attack, Vice Admiral Halsey, who was in charge of the task force headed by USS Enterprise, was far from the Hawaii base, as the storm had delayed their arrival. After learning of the Japanese attack, the admiral, who had an explosive temper, let the hatred for the new enemy grow in his heart. Chester W. Nimitz, the newly appointed Commander-in-Chief of the U.S. Pacific Fleet, realized that Halsey's aggression and restlessness was just the right thing for the American seamen depressed by the Pearl Harbor disaster. For this reason, in early 1942, it was Halsey who, with the help of his carrier task force, held a series of lightning-like \"hit-and-run\" raids on the Japanese strongholds. After James Doolittle's bombers took off for the Tokyo Raid from the deck of Halsey's aircraft carriers, the name of William Halsey became a personification of the belief in victory over Japan for every American.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In October of 1942, after taking the command over the South Pacific forces, Halsey led the persistent fight for Guadalcanal, one of the Solomon Islands, that was a key to the entire Pacific Campaign.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM074_DESC"
msgstr "<p>In the Battle of Guadalcanal, Admiral Halsey again proved to be the right man for the job at the right time and place for the United States. With his overflowing energy and belligerence, he instilled confidence in all his subordinates, down to the last sailor or marine. He struck the Japanese repeatedly, sending the latest battleships and aircraft carriers into the narrow straits of the Solomon Islands when the situation warranted it.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>When Guadalcanal finally remained in American hands in February 1943, Halsey turned his attention to the rest of the islands of the archipelago, being the first in the Pacific to apply the strategy of 'leapfrogging' the strongest Japanese defensive points. 'As long as I have at least one pilot and at least one plane, I will advance': step by step, the admiral advanced, took risks, made mistakes, suffered losses, some of which were far from justified. The culminating moment in the career of the naval commander came on September 2, 1945. On that day, aboard the USS Missouri — the flagship of Admiral William Halsey, commander of the Third Fleet — the most powerful formation of the US Navy, the surrender of Japan was signed.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM091_SHIPS_DESC"
msgstr "<p>Throughout history, the image of a ship has carried an aesthetic value beyond compare with any other work of human hands. For centuries, people were captivated by the boundless and mysterious allure of majestic sailing ships. But then came the “nineteenth, iron century...” and the industrial revolution accelerated humanity’s pace of life—replacing wood and wind at sea, nature’s gifts, with metal and steam.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>From then on, right up to the dawn of the space age, it was ships that embodied all the scientific and technological achievements of our civilization. And since the coming twentieth century would bring worldwide wars on an unprecedented scale, warships inevitably led the way in shipbuilding. Yet people continued to admire their precision and symmetry, their complexity and deadly power.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>When the twentieth century arrived, the world was entering it to the thunder of naval guns: the Sino-Japanese, Spanish-American, and Russo-Japanese Wars were largely decided at sea. Learning from those conflicts, the great powers’ navies developed new technical requirements for building warships of the main classes.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In battleship construction, they moved on to building “dreadnought” types armed with eight to twelve 305 mm or larger guns, outfitted with a new armor layout, higher speeds (thanks to turbine engines), and improved survivability. The idea of such ships was discussed in many countries, but with its highly developed economy and shipbuilding, Britain pulled ahead—launching the revolutionary HMS Dreadnought, whose name became synonymous with this new type. Older battleships were rendered obsolete overnight, and all powers effectively started again in the naval arms race, with economic strength alone determining who would pull ahead.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Cruiser design also became more specialized, producing large series of high-speed cruisers intended for operations within fleets. Speed became one of the main tactical and technical parameters. To achieve the necessary speed without enlarging displacement too much, cruisers moved from coal-fired piston engines to steam turbines with oil-fired boilers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>This process was even more pronounced in destroyer design. Requirements for maneuverability and speed were drastically increased, allowing them to choose the best position and timing for a strike. Based on experience from recent naval conflicts, the role of destroyers grew significantly. They not only took part in battles but also carried out essential day-to-day duties—reconnaissance, patrols, and laying minefields. As a result, destroyers began to be armed with 102–120 mm guns, replaced fixed underwater torpedo tubes with deck-mounted rotating multi-tube launchers, and were equipped to deploy and recover large numbers of mines directly from the upper deck. Destroyers thus became a formidable opponent for surface ships of all classes.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>All these many innovations in warship design would be put to the test very soon—World War I erupted in 1914.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM092_SHIPS_DESC"
msgstr "<p>During World War I, the evolution of warships of the major types revolved around the simple core idea of making them larger and stronger. The increasingly large caliber of their guns was complemented by their ever-stronger armor protection, which resulted in more significant water displacement and bigger proportions. For battleships, the race was initiated by the British when they designed a series of Queen Elizabeth-class warships with a sharp increase in their main gun caliber (up to 381 mm). Germany promptly responded with its Bayern-class super-dreadnoughts, which carried the same super-heavy artillery. The German ships, however, outmatched their rivals in terms of armor and combat durability. Their aim was to achieve superiority in quality, as it was impossible to compete with or match the sheer number of ships in the British fleet. Perhaps the only really important novelty in this race for world shipbuilding supremacy emerged when the Americans adopted \"all or nothing\" armor, not typical of warships of the pre-dreadnought era, on their Nevada-class battleships. The new scheme involved heavily armoring the areas most important to a ship, while the remainder received significantly less armor to emphasize protection optimized for long-range engagement. By a twist of fate, the strongest battleships of that period were eventually built by Japan, a country that had, until that time, stayed away from the main theaters of military activity. Hardly any rival ship was on par with their Nagato-class warships in terms of speed, armor and armament combined.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the war, only two European states could afford to build large series of new cruisers—Germany and Great Britain. The trends were the same: when the main caliber of the German cruisers reached 150 mm, the British designed ships of the Hawkins class with 190 mm guns, which became the prototype of a totally new subclass of ships—the heavy cruiser. The Americans and Japanese, in turn, carefully considered their military experience in relation to their own objectives. Built to scout for a fleet of U.S. battleships, the Omaha-class light cruisers featured high speed for cooperation with allied destroyers, and 152 mm guns to fend off any enemy destroyers that might be sent their way. The Japanese, in turn, reinforced their fleet of warships with several series of Kuma- and Nagara-class 5,500-ton light cruisers. Any of the them could be used as both a reconnaissance ship, and also as a command vessel for destroyer flotillas during massively overwhelming torpedo attacks.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Destroyers were undoubtedly the most active fighting ships during World War I, which is why they would generally be considered the navy's expendable assets. In addition to raiding operations, patrolling and mine laying, this type of ship assumed another military role—anti-submarine warfare—which resulted in corresponding changes being made to their design and armament. The general trend, however, was the same: destroyers received ever more weapons, and the caliber of their guns grew, along with their displacement and speed. During the war, for the first time in the Royal Navy, a new sub-type of ships appeared: destroyer flotilla leaders, the role of which approached light cruisers in terms their characteristics and objectives.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>World War I demonstrated clearly that, during a global conflict, only a few economically powerful countries that had steered clear of direct combat were capable of building the major ship types on a massive scale. But it was clear even to them that the uncontrolled naval arms race was accelerating beyond their capabilities.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM093_SHIPS_DESC"
msgstr "<p>The economic consequences of World War I were so devastating that even the greatest winning states had to commit to something they once thought impossible—conclude a treaty on the limitation of naval armament. From 1922, for the first time in history, naval officials and shipbuilders had to be guided not by operational or strategic considerations, or optimal technical solutions, but primarily by the articles of the Washington Naval Treaty. This treaty contained restrictions on the number, displacement, and armament for the major types of the vessels of war. In addition, Germany, Russia, and Austria-Hungary ceased to rank among the naval powers at that time. It is not surprising, therefore, that the \"Washington Treaty\" period in the history of military shipbuilding turned out to be a rather difficult one.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Firstly, no capital ships were allowed to be built for a period of ten years. At the signing of the treaty, the contracting powers retained their most advanced capital ships in their respective navies. The only exception was the Nelson class of British battleships built during that period. To comply with the limitations of the Washington Treaty, these ships were of an unusual design with all three main battery turrets carried forward of the ship's bridge, an arrangement unlikely to be used under normal circumstances.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Washington Treaty had a more profound effect on cruiser building. Cruisers were limited in terms of quality, but not numbers. Since battleships were no longer allowed to be built, the major naval powers began a competition to build \"treaty cruisers\" using the maximum values for technical specifications allowed by the treaty. To obey the limits on total cruiser displacement established for each navy, designers were forced to make compromises which normally affected armor protection. As a result, during the second half of the 1920s, Great Britain, France, Italy, the U.S.A., and Japan possessed ships boasting a high speed, a 203 mm main battery, and good seaworthiness, all at the cost of very weak armor. Warships of this new subtype, the need for which was never dictated by any objective naval warfare needs, were collectively known as the \"Washington Treaty Cruisers\".</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The treaty for the limitation of naval armament likewise had an effect on the brand new type of warship—aircraft carriers. In 1922, the number of capital ships employed on fleet duties was sharply reduced. The hulls of the newest battleships and battle cruisers, which were being outfitted at shipyards, could either be scrapped, or converted into aircraft carriers. This way, by the end of the 1920s, the U.S.A., Japan, and Great Britain came to own large, high-speed aircraft carriers that were used to work through basic design solutions and tactics that were later adopted on this type of massively produced, purpose-built ship.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The trends in the development of destroyers in the post-war years did not change—their size grew, and their armament became deadlier. France and Italy competed in creating ever larger and faster warships, equipped with increasingly more powerful artillery, eventually blurring the line between these ships and light cruisers. During the years of \"forced enthusiasm\" for the construction of \"Washington Treaty\" warships, only a few ships of this subtype were built. However, the real breakthrough came with the appearance of the Japanese destroyers of the Fubuki class and their successors—the Hatsuharu class. Powerful artillery, and most importantly, the crippling long-range 610 mm torpedoes coupled with a unique system of fast reload right at sea, propelled the destroyer forces of the Imperial Japanese Navy to a leading position in the world.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Meanwhile, as the shock from the world war passed, Germany and the U.S.S.R., the heir to the Russian Empire, again sought to join the ranks of the world's leading powers on land and at sea. For many signatories of the Washington Naval Treaty, naval arms limitations became increasingly difficult to put up with.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM094_SHIPS_DESC"
msgstr "<p>In the first half of the 1930s, Great Britain made enormous efforts to maintain and strengthen the system of international naval armament limitation treaties. The reason was not pacifism among the British but that the weakening British Empire could no longer confidently hold the trident of Neptune. Two conferences were held in London in 1930 and 1936, resulting in the tightening of existing limits on the number, size, and armament of warships under the Washington Agreement of 1922. In addition, several new restrictions were introduced, which from then on applied to aircraft carriers, destroyers, submarines, and all cruisers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The agreements reached were largely devalued: Italy refused to sign them, and Japan withdrew from the naval agreement system altogether. Attempts to involve Germany and the USSR in bilateral treaties (under the Washington-London system) also failed, despite these countries' desire to enter the open seas from the mid-1930s.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Nevertheless, the London naval agreements significantly influenced military shipbuilding. Since Japan and Italy refused to join the 1936 treaty, the United States, France, and Great Britain amended it, increasing the allowed displacement of capital ships. They also increased the main caliber from 356 to 406 mm. However, the British themselves designed and laid down their new King George V-class battleships with the previous displacement restriction and 14-inch guns. In turn, the Americans, consistently advancing toward the concept of fast battleships, managed to install 16-inch main guns on the new North Carolina and South Dakota-class battleships. The most balanced ships of this class, considering treaty limitations, were built in France — these were the Richelieu-class ships with 380 mm guns. This was a symmetrical response to the new Italian Littorio-class battleships. Finally, in the late 1930s, Germany rapidly entered the closed club of owners of full-fledged modern battleships with the excellently protected Bismarck-class ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The London treaties had the most significant impact on cruiser construction. Subclasses of heavy and light cruisers were defined, with the construction of the former initially restricted and then banned altogether. This allowed Great Britain to focus on creating a new generation of light cruisers with 152 mm main guns — the British Empire needed many such ships. However, the emergence of powerful Japanese Mogami and Tone-class cruisers with 155 mm guns, which could easily be replaced with 203 mm guns, led to the need to enhance the combat qualities of light cruisers of the British Navy and the US Navy. Perhaps the most advanced heavy cruiser of the 1930s was the French Algérie, built as a single unit, while the best-protected were the Italian Zara-class cruisers. Among the light cruisers, the French La Galissonnière-class ships were considered the best in terms of cost-effectiveness and combat efficiency.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>As for destroyers, in the second half of the 1930s, powerful Japanese destroyers and fast French leaders were joined by relatively large, well-armed new German destroyers. Due to treaty limitations, the United States and Great Britain responded by creating lighter ships. They did not have outstanding characteristics or armament, but the powerful industry allowed them to be built in incomparably larger numbers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>By the late 1930s, treaty limitations were virtually ignored. What held back a full-scale naval arms race among the great powers was only the diplomatic framework of propriety and peacetime budgetary constraints, but even these were abandoned with the onset of World War II.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM095_SHIPS_DESC"
msgstr "<p>World War II overturned the entire system of limitations on naval armament that existed in the 1920s and 30s, bringing international shipbuilding back to its natural state. From that moment on, every decision was dominated by the need for warships, production capacities, technology, and time. It is not surprising then that the United States was the only country that expressed a wish to build a series of battleships during these years. The Iowa-class battleships fit perfectly into the new format of naval warfare, changing their inherent role from hunting for high-speed Japanese battlecruisers to ensuring the stability of fast aircraft carrier strike formations. It is generally accepted that with all of their performance characteristics combined, they were the best battleships of World War II, but this honorary title is contested by the Japanese giants of the Yamato class. In contrast to them, the last battleship of the Royal Navy—Vanguard—looked much weaker, despite her excellent seaworthiness.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>A similar situation unfolded during World War II in cruiser building. It was possible in this period to create well-balanced and powerful ships, but only the United States and Great Britain had the resources to do so. As a result, the U.S. Navy received a large series of Baltimore-class heavy cruisers, the largest of their type, as well as light cruisers of the Cleveland class. The former ones were the strongest wartime heavy cruisers, balanced so well in terms of armament, armor protection, and seaworthiness that they remained in service for many years. Their enhanced versions served as the basis for the design of the Des Moines-class heavy cruisers equipped with auto-loading main battery guns, and these ships embodied all the experience gained during World War II. Armed with 203 mm main guns and representing the pinnacle of cruiser building under the Washington Naval Treaty, they were the best representatives of the latest generation of heavy artillery ships. In the light cruiser type, they were matched by the ships of the Worcester class, which also entered service after the war and carried a battery of 152 mm automatic dual-purpose guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The most massively produced surface ships of the World War II period were destroyers, as they were involved in all impactful battles in all theaters of military activity. But despite mass production, the loss rate of this type of ship was the highest. Unlike heavy artillery ships, new classes of destroyers entered service during the war, not only in huge series joining the U.S. Navy (Fletcher, Gearing) but also in large numbers in the Royal Navy, as well as in noticeable numbers in Japan (Yūgumo, Akizuki) and Germany (various series of the 1936 class). After the war, new artillery destroyers were designed by those countries that could not afford to have larger ships in sufficient numbers. Fortunately, in terms of displacement and armament, the destroyers of that time often matched pre-war light cruisers: the Battle and Daring classes of Great Britain, the Halland class of Sweden, and the Friesland class of the Netherlands.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The era when steam turbine-driven warships dominated the seas extended over a short period by historical measures—only about half a century, almost coinciding in time with the first half of the 20th century. However, despite being short, it encompassed as many as several different epochs.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM096_SHUMSHU_DESC"
msgstr "<p>Shumshu is the northernmost island of the Kuril archipelago, the capture of which by Soviet troops in August 1945 marked the beginning of the Kuril operation.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Kuril Islands hold great strategic significance on the eastern coast of Eurasia: the islands form an arc enclosing the Sea of Okhotsk and block the exit to the Pacific Ocean. The Russian Empire lost the Kuril Islands to Japan under the terms of the Treaty of Saint Petersburg in 1875, receiving ownership of Sakhalin in exchange. At that time, Russian development of the Far East extended to the Amur and Primorye regions, and by the beginning of the 20th century, it shifted southward to Manchuria. It was only in the 1930s that the USSR achieved truly rapid economic development and population growth in this region—the Soviet Far Eastern project became one of the landmark achievements of the era. At the same time, Japan remained an influential power in the Far East. During World War II, the Kuril Islands served as a Japanese rear area: the carrier strike force for the attack on Pearl Harbor assembled here, and fish processing plants supplying the army were also located here.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>At the Yalta Conference in February 1945, an agreement was reached that the USSR would receive the Kuril Islands and southern Sakhalin, which had been seized in 1905, in exchange for entering the war against Japan. On August 15 at 07:30, troops of the Kamchatka Defense District under the command of Major General A. R. Gnechko received orders from the commanders of the 2nd Far Eastern Front and the Pacific Fleet: to occupy the islands of Shumshu, Paramushir, and Onekotan.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In total, 8,824 personnel with 205 guns and mortars were concentrated for the operation, transported by 17 transport and 16 landing vessels. The largest combat ships were the border patrol ships \"Kirov\" and \"Dzerzhinsky,\" and the minelayer \"Okhotsk.\" The landing force was divided into an advance detachment and main forces consisting of two echelons. The first wave included a composite marine battalion—791 personnel from the best units of the naval base, a company of submachine gunners and sappers from the 302nd Rifle Regiment of the 101st Division, and a company of border guards. The first echelon of the main forces consisted of the 138th Rifle Regiment, an anti-tank battalion, an artillery battalion, and a howitzer battalion. The second echelon included the 373rd Rifle Regiment and two battalions of the artillery regiment.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The advance detachment was to capture the coastline in the northeast of Shumshu, between Capes Kokutan and Kotomari, and then seize the heights near point 171 in the interior of the island. The first echelon would land next and advance to capture the Kataoka naval base in the southwest of Shumshu. The second echelon would exploit its success.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM097_SHUMSHU_DESC"
msgstr "<p>The core of the Japanese force on the Northern Kuril Islands was the 91st Infantry Division under Lieutenant General Fusaki Tsutsumi. It consisted of the 73rd and 74th Infantry Brigades on Shumshu and Paramushir islands, the 11th Independent Tank Regiment (64 medium and light tanks), three artillery battalions, and other units. By August 18, 1945, the division numbered 16,733 personnel, of which 8,480 servicemen and 98 guns were on Shumshu. The Japanese command developed a new island defense tactic, incorporating lessons from battles against the Americans in the Pacific. In areas of potential enemy landings, only isolated company strongpoints were built on the shore, from which infantry, in coordination with artillery, would destroy the enemy landing force. The main defensive line, however, was positioned several kilometers inland. The enemy was to be worn down on pre-prepared lines up to this defense line, with mobile reserves counterattacking when opportune.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>On Shumshu, the defensive line was shifted from the coast to the center of the island and to the southwest, towards the Kataoka naval base. Strongpoints were established in all key areas, designed for autonomous combat operations in any situation. For example, at Cape Kokutan and Cape Kotomari, 75-mm guns and companies of a separate infantry battalion were stationed in concrete permanent defensive positions.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Despite the fact that the Emperor of Japan, in his radio address to the nation on August 15, essentially announced the surrender, two days later an order arrived at the commander of the 91st Division from the front headquarters, permitting \"to resort to self-defense if necessary\" against the forces of the anti-Hitler coalition. Then, on the night of August 17-18, when a Japanese observation post at Cape Kokutan detected a large number of ships off the eastern coast of Kamchatka, an order was issued from the division headquarters to unit commanders in the north of the island at midnight: \"In the event of a Soviet landing, destroy them.\"</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM098_SHUMSHU_DESC"
msgstr "<p>At 5:00 on August 17, ships and landing craft departed Avacha Bay near Petropavlovsk-Kamchatsky. The southward movement to the First Kuril Strait proceeded through dense fog, taking nearly 24 hours. The fog prevented air support from the 128th Aviation Division.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>At 04:22 on August 18, the 1,363-strong advance detachment began landing from 4 LCIs under enemy artillery fire. Within 35 minutes, the first wave deployed almost without losses, though three LCIs couldn't beach, forcing troops to wade 150-200m through water. Marines and border troops secured the coastal area and advanced inland toward strategic Heights 171 and 165.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM099_SHUMSHU_DESC"
msgstr "<p>After receiving a report about the Soviet landing force at around five o'clock in the morning on August 18th, the command of the Japanese 91st Division ordered the 11th Tank Regiment to advance towards Cape Kokutan, and the commander of the 73rd Infantry Brigade to concentrate all available forces, attack, and destroy the enemy. Two battalions from the 74th Brigade and a company of amphibious tanks were transferred from the neighboring island of Paramushir to Shumshu.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Major Shutov's advance detachment, destroying small groups of Japanese along the way, reached the starting position for the assault on heights 171 and 165 at 05:40. The attack began half an hour later. The marines, armed only with rifles, slowly advanced under Japanese artillery, mortar, and machine gun fire, systematically eliminating firing points. By the beginning of the tenth hour, the landing force managed to capture height 165. Here, Chief Petty Officer Nikolai Aleksandrovich Vilkov performed a heroic deed, silencing the embrasure of an enemy pillbox by covering it with his own body after failing to destroy it with a grenade. However, at 09:30, the Japanese launched a determined counterattack supported by a company of light tanks. They managed to push the marines back about 300 meters down the slope of the height, losing 7 out of 11 vehicles.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>By 11 o'clock, the 138th Rifle Regiment began to reach the line of the advance detachment, and two hours later, the 373rd Regiment did as well. But they needed time to reorganize after the difficult landing. Colonel P. A. Artyushin, deputy commander of the 101st Division, assumed command of all landed troops. At 14:00, the arriving main forces of the 11th Tank Regiment attacked the Soviet positions without infantry support. Through decisive actions by anti-tank rifle crews and grenades, the attack was repelled, 17 Japanese tanks were destroyed, and the regimental commander was killed. After 15:00, Major Shutov, wounded for the third time, transferred command of the units at marker 171 to Marine Battalion Commander Major T. A. Pochtaryov. At 17:00, the marines and riflemen again launched an assault on the heights, drove the Japanese out by nine in the evening, and consolidated their positions.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM100_SHUMSHU_DESC"
msgstr "<p>After landing, a marine company attempted to neutralize the Kotomari Cape coastal battery but couldn't overcome its fortifications. With most radios inoperative during landing, stable communications weren't established until afternoon on August 18.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>On August 19, assault groups from the 119th Engineer Battalion destroyed the Kokutan and Kotomari strongpoints by blowing up fortifications with their garrisons. By 11:00, the first artillery pieces were manually transported to the front lines.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>At 21:40 on August 21, Japanese forces officially surrendered: 'Japanese troops in the northern Kuril Islands cease all combat, lay down arms and surrender.' On Shumshu, 3 generals, 525 officers and 11,700 soldiers capitulated. Soviet losses totaled 516 killed, 329 missing and 716 wounded—the final act of WWII in the Far East.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM101_MAKAROV_DESC"
msgstr "<p>The Russo-Japanese War broke out in early 1904 in the waters of the Yellow Sea when the Japanese fleet attacked the Russian squadron at Port Arthur roadstead. Japanese army units landed in ports along the coast, and transports with weapons, provisions, and reinforcements also arrived here. The priority task of the Japanese fleet was the naval blockade of Port Arthur on the Liaodong Peninsula and the destruction of Russian ships threatening Japanese sea lines of communication.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Port Arthur was the only Russian naval base and maritime fortress in the Yellow Sea. The Russian and Japanese fleets, operating near Port Arthur and the nearby bays and archipelagos, engaged in patrol and reconnaissance duties, laid mines, and provided fire support for their ground forces with naval artillery.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the summer of 1904, an attempt was made to break out of the First Pacific Squadron to Vladivostok. The naval battle in the Yellow Sea in 1904 was its last major engagement. There were no ship losses on either side, but the Russian squadron was divided and largely lost its combat capability.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>At the end of 1904, the remaining Russian ships in Port Arthur were destroyed either by Japanese siege artillery fire or by their own crews. The subsequent capture of Port Arthur ensured the Combined Fleet of Japan under Admiral Tōgō Heihachirō complete dominance at sea, which the Second Pacific Squadron attempted to challenge.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM102_MAKAROV_DESC"
msgstr "<p>During the war in the summer of 1904, Russian auxiliary cruisers were sent to the shores of Africa to intercept ships carrying military contraband to Japan. The choice of African waters for cruising was not accidental. In the Strait of Gibraltar, the Suez Canal, the Red Sea, near the island of Madagascar, and at the Cape of Good Hope, international sea routes converge.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The \"Ural\" and \"Don\" left the Baltic Sea, passed through the North Sea, and circled England from the north to begin cruising near the Strait of Gibraltar and the northwestern coast of Africa. Later, the auxiliary cruiser \"Terek\" replaced them in the same area of the Atlantic Ocean.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the waters of the Indian Ocean, the auxiliary cruisers \"Petersburg\" and \"Smolensk\" operated. They left Sevastopol, passed through the Bosporus and Dardanelles straits and the Suez Canal, after which they mounted artillery and began cruising in the Red Sea. \"Petersburg\" inspected eight ships, one of which carried contraband military cargo for Japan. This steamer, along with several others, was detained and sent to Libau. In mid-July, \"Petersburg\" and \"Smolensk\" entered the Indian Ocean and began cruising off the eastern coast of Africa and the island of Madagascar.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The British government saw the actions of the Russian cruisers as a violation of maritime prize law and protested—threatening to sever relations and start a war with the Russian Empire. Therefore, at the end of August 1904, \"Petersburg\" and \"Smolensk\" were ordered to cease cruising and return to Russia.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM103_MAKAROV_DESC"
msgstr "<p>On the eve of the war with Japan, the Russian command recognized the need to strengthen the fleet in the Pacific Ocean. For this purpose, in 1903, a detachment of ships under the command of Rear Admiral A. A. Virenius was sent from the Baltic Sea to the Far East. Already in the Red Sea, the detachment received orders to return to the Baltic due to the outbreak of the Russo-Japanese War. Its ships became part of the Second Pacific Squadron—which was to join the First Pacific Squadron and gain dominance at sea.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The voyage to the Far East was a complex task. There was not a single Russian naval base along the way, and foreign ports imposed neutrality restrictions. In connection with this, the Second Pacific Squadron included tugboats, hospital ships, a floating workshop, and transports that supplied the squadron with coal, provisions, and fresh water.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In October 1904, the Second Pacific Squadron under the command of Vice Admiral Z. P. Rozhestvensky left the Baltic Sea, passed through the North Sea, and exited into the Atlantic Ocean via the English Channel. Near Gibraltar, ships with a draft allowing passage through the Suez Canal separated from the squadron. Under the command of Rear Admiral D. G. Felkerzam, they passed through the Mediterranean and Red Seas, rejoining the main forces near the island of Madagascar, which had circumnavigated Africa from the west and south.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The fall of Port Arthur and the destruction of the First Pacific Squadron delayed the Russian sailors off the coast of Madagascar for two months while awaiting reinforcements from the Baltic Sea. In March 1905, Z. P. Rozhestvensky led the Second Pacific Squadron through the Indian Ocean without waiting for the arrival of the Third Pacific Squadron. The two squadrons later united in the South China Sea.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>To break through to Vladivostok, the shortest and safest route—through the Korean Strait—was chosen. There, near the island of Tsushima, the decisive naval battle of the Russo-Japanese War took place in May 1905.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM104_MAKAROV_DESC"
msgstr "<p>As an island nation, the Japanese Empire heavily depended on maritime supplies from other countries. In July 1904, the Vladivostok Cruiser Detachment set out into the Pacific Ocean—toward the busiest sea routes to Japan. While passing through the Tsugaru Strait, the Russian cruisers were spotted, and Japanese ships were warned.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The loss of stealth did not hinder the operation's success. The Vladivostok Detachment intercepted and inspected 12 vessels near the approaches to Tokyo Bay and the Tsugaru Strait, sinking 7 of them. Considering the remaining coal reserves and calculating consumption, the detachment commander, Rear Admiral K. P. Jessen, decided to break through the Tsugaru Strait again. The operation concluded successfully in Vladivostok without battles or crew losses.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After the battle in the Yellow Sea, the 2nd-class cruiser \"Novik\" docked in the German port of Qingdao to replenish coal supplies. The cruiser's commander, Captain 2nd Rank M. F. von Schultz, led \"Novik\" through the Pacific waters around Japan's eastern shores to Vladivostok, but the cruiser was spotted and intercepted near the Korsakov post on Sakhalin Island while loading coal. After a battle with the Japanese cruiser \"Tsushima,\" the Russian cruiser was scuttled by its crew.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In August 1904, the auxiliary cruiser \"Lena\" was sent into the Pacific Ocean, having sunk two Japanese ships in June during a raid on the Korean port of Gensan. However, cruising on the San Francisco–Yokohama line was aborted due to mechanical failures, and \"Lena\" was interned in San Francisco until the end of the war. The last time Russian cruisers appeared in the Pacific was in May 1905, when \"Terek\" and \"Kuban\" sank two steamers carrying military cargo for Japan.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PCQM105_MAKAROV_DESC"
msgstr "<p>The Vladivostok Cruiser Detachment operated in the Sea of Japan. From the very beginning of the war, they posed a threat to Japanese maritime communications and ports along the coasts of Japan and Korea. Russian cruisers and destroyers managed to sink several vessels and troop transports. Daring operations diverted part of the Japanese fleet's forces from Port Arthur, but their area of operations was limited to the Sea of Japan, with only one sortie into the ocean through the Tsugaru Strait.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Japanese fleet took retaliatory actions: Vladivostok was shelled from the sea, mines were laid on its approaches, and attempts were made to intercept the Vladivostok Detachment. The Japanese fleet succeeded in doing so in the summer of 1904, when the Russian cruisers were rushing to the Korean Strait to join the First Pacific Squadron. During the ensuing battle, the cruiser \"Rurik\" was lost, while the cruisers \"Rossiya\" and \"Gromoboy\" sustained significant damage and retreated to Vladivostok.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>On the eve of the Tsushima Battle in the spring of 1905, the cruisers \"Rossiya\" and \"Gromoboy\" made their last combat sortie to the Tsugaru Strait, and destroyers—to the island of Hokkaido. Despite sinking several vessels, the Japanese took no retaliatory action, focusing all their forces on preparing for the battle with the Second Pacific Squadron. The Tsushima Battle, which took place in the waters of the Sea of Japan in May 1905, became the decisive engagement of the war. The Russian squadron suffered a heavy defeat—only the cruiser \"Almaz\" and the destroyers \"Bravyy\" and \"Groznyy\" reached Vladivostok.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the summer of 1905, the Japanese fleet conducted an amphibious operation that resulted in the capture of Sakhalin. However, until the very end of the war, the Japanese never took active offensive actions at sea against Vladivostok and Nikolaevsk, fearing Russian submarines, mines, surface ships, and coastal batteries above all.</p>"

