msgid "IDS_PGSA104"
msgstr "Rhein"

msgid "IDS_PGSA104_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the late 1930s and early 1940s, Germany developed several draft designs for aircraft-carrying ships for the war against Great Britain. One such sketch was a project for a small aircraft carrier intended for serial construction during wartime. The main idea was economy and the possibility of mass production at civilian shipyards.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The project itself dates to mid-1942, but under wartime conditions, the construction of such a ship could not have been completed before 1944. According to the understanding of German specialists, the flight deck length had to be at least 170 m, with take-offs still expected to require catapults.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSA104_FULL"
msgstr "Rhein"

msgid "IDS_PGSA104_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSA104_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PGSA106"
msgstr "Weser"

msgid "IDS_PGSA106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The cruiser Seydlitz, of the Admiral Hipper class, was built in Bremen, but with the start of World War II, work slowed down. In June 1942, at 95% readiness, construction was halted: after the attack on Pearl Harbor, the need to build aircraft-carrying ships became clear. The nearly completed cruiser was designated for conversion into an aircraft carrier and renamed Weser.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Work began in December 1942, but ceased after six months, and the ship was towed to Königsberg. In January 1945, the hull of the aircraft carrier was scuttled.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSA106_FULL"
msgstr "Weser"

msgid "IDS_PGSA106_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSA106_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PGSA108"
msgstr "A. Parseval"

msgid "IDS_PGSA108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Graf Zeppelin-class ships used a classic steam turbine power plant, with the lead aircraft carrier featuring turbine-gear units and boilers of Swiss manufacture.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>German shipbuilders believed that diesel-type main power plants were necessary for raider ships. Such a plant could provide high range at an acceptable speed. It is highly probable that subsequent raider aircraft carriers would have been equipped with diesel power plants.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSA108_FULL"
msgstr "August von Parseval"

msgid "IDS_PGSA108_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSA108_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PGSA110"
msgstr "M. Richthofen"

msgid "IDS_PGSA110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A hypothetical variant of a heavy aircraft carrier based on the hull of the unfinished German battleship of the H-39 class. It is assumed that by the time battleship construction was halted, the hull was fully formed, and the machinery of the main power plant and power station had been installed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In this form, the ship would have been one of the largest on the planet, second only to the Japanese Shinano.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSA110_FULL"
msgstr "Manfred von Richthofen"

msgid "IDS_PGSA110_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSA110_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSA506"
msgstr "E. Loewenhardt"

msgid "IDS_PGSA506_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The second ship of the Graf Zeppelin class, Aircraft Carrier B, was laid down in Kiel in 1938 after the launch of the first. There was no particular rush with its construction, planning to account for all the 'teething troubles' of the first ship in the series.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>By the start of World War II, the aircraft carrier's hull had been assembled up to the armored deck level, but the German leadership decided to complete only ships in a high state of readiness. As a result, in September 1939, the order for the second aircraft carrier was canceled, and the ship itself was dismantled on the slipway in early 1940.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSA506_FULL"
msgstr "Erich Loewenhardt"

msgid "IDS_PGSA506_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSA506_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PGSA508"
msgstr "Graf Zeppelin (old)"

msgid "IDS_PGSA508_DESCR"
msgstr "This ship was available before the release of Update 0.8.0."

msgid "IDS_PGSA508_FULL"
msgstr "Graf Zeppelin (< 23.01.2019)"

msgid "IDS_PGSA508_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PGSA508_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PGSA510"
msgstr "W. Voss"

msgid "IDS_PGSA510_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>One of the possible variants of an aircraft carrier based on the unfinished hull of a battleship. The project assumes that when battleship construction was halted, the hull was fully formed, and the main power plant and power station had been installed on the ship.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Such a ship would have become the largest in the world, possessing good armor for its class and powerful AA armament.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSA510_FULL"
msgstr "Werner Voss"

msgid "IDS_PGSA510_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSA510_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSA518"
msgstr "Graf Zeppelin"

msgid "IDS_PGSA518_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Realizing the necessity of building aircraft carriers for the upcoming war with Great Britain, Germany sent a group of experts to Japan to study the operational experience of ships of this class.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In December 1936, the first aircraft carrier was laid down in Kiel, receiving the name Graf Zeppelin upon launch. However, after the adoption of the German fleet modernization program, the ship's construction was suspended. In 1940, the aircraft carrier's stem was changed to an 'Atlantic' bow, and in 1942, external bulges were added to the original design. Sea trials were scheduled for April 1943, but in February of that year, construction was halted again.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Until 1945, the aircraft carrier was located in various Baltic ports. In April 1945, the ship was scuttled in Stettin due to the city's occupation by the Red Army. After the war, Graf Zeppelin was raised and subjected to comprehensive study in the USSR. There were plans to complete the ship, but they were ultimately abandoned, and the aircraft carrier was used as a target and sunk in 1947.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSA518_FULL"
msgstr "Graf Zeppelin"

msgid "IDS_PGSA518_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PGSA518_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PGSA598"
msgstr "Graf Zeppelin B"

msgid "IDS_PGSA598_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Realizing the necessity of building aircraft carriers for the upcoming war with Great Britain, Germany sent a group of experts to Japan to study the operational experience of ships of this class.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In December 1936, the first aircraft carrier was laid down in Kiel, receiving the name Graf Zeppelin upon launch. However, after the adoption of the German fleet modernization program, the ship's construction was suspended. In 1940, the aircraft carrier's stem was changed to an 'Atlantic' bow, and in 1942, external bulges were added to the original design. Sea trials were scheduled for April 1943, but in February of that year, construction was halted again.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Until 1945, the aircraft carrier was located in various Baltic ports. In April 1945, the ship was scuttled in Stettin due to the city's occupation by the Red Army. After the war, Graf Zeppelin was raised and subjected to comprehensive study in the USSR. There were plans to complete the ship, but they were ultimately abandoned, and the aircraft carrier was used as a target and sunk in 1947.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSA598_FULL"
msgstr "Graf Zeppelin B"

msgid "IDS_PGSA598_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PGSA598_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PGSA610"
msgstr "M. Immelmann"

msgid "IDS_PGSA610_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The project is one of the possible variants for completing an H-39 class battleship as an aircraft carrier.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>At the time construction ceased as a battleship, the hull should have been fully formed, with the main power plant machinery and power stations installed. Such an aircraft carrier would have become the largest ship in Europe.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSA610_FULL"
msgstr "Max Immelmann"

msgid "IDS_PGSA610_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSA610_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSA990"
msgstr "M. Richthofen Test"

msgid "IDS_PGSA990_DESCR"
msgstr "A hypothetical variation of a heavy German aircraft carrier based on the unfinished H-39 class battleship hull. It is assumed that by the time the construction of the battleship was halted, her hull would have been fully shaped, and the main propulsion plant and electrical plant machinery would also have been installed. With such a configuration, she would have been one of the largest ships in the world, second only to Japanese Shinano."

msgid "IDS_PGSA990_FULL"
msgstr "Manfred von Richthofen Test"

msgid "IDS_PGSA990_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSA990_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSB001"
msgstr "Bismarck"

msgid "IDS_PGSB001_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1932, the German navy began studies for a new battleship, based on a battlecruiser from World War I. During development, the displacement exceeded Washington Treaty limits, which was decided to be concealed. When it became known that France and Italy were building battleships with 380 mm guns, the Germans also increased the main caliber of their battleship.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Bismarck was built at the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg. In May 1941, the battleship put to sea with the cruiser Prinz Eugen on its first and last combat operation. Learning of the enemy's movement, the British ships Prince of Wales and Hood attempted to intercept the German ships on May 24. After several hits, Hood exploded and sank. The German raiders headed into the Atlantic, and the British fleet gave chase. As a result of an attack by torpedo bombers from the aircraft carrier Ark Royal on May 26, Bismarck suffered rudder damage and became unmaneuverable. In the ensuing battle with the British squadron, the battleship was sunk.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB001_FULL"
msgstr "Bismarck"

msgid "IDS_PGSB001_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PGSB001_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSB002"
msgstr "Tirpitz"

msgid "IDS_PGSB002_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Tirpitz, the second Bismarck-class battleship, was built in Wilhelmshaven. After sea trials, the battleship became the flagship of a temporary Baltic squadron covering Operation Barbarossa from the sea.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In January 1942, Tirpitz moved to Trondheim, Norway, to attack northern convoys. However, due to fuel shortages and fear of losing another battleship after Bismarck's loss, the ship's activity remained low for the rest of the war. In March 1942, Tirpitz attempted to attack convoy PQ-12 but was itself attacked by British carrier aircraft and withdrew. In June, the battleship sortied from Altafjord to intercept convoy PQ-17 but was detected and attacked by the Soviet submarine K-21.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In September 1943, the battleship participated in the landing on Spitsbergen, and upon return was attacked by British mini-submarines, sustaining serious damage. The ship was repeatedly attacked by British aircraft and was sunk by Lancaster bombers on November 12, 1944.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB002_FULL"
msgstr "Tirpitz"

msgid "IDS_PGSB002_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PGSB002_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PGSB103"
msgstr "Nassau"

msgid "IDS_PGSB103_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1903, at the suggestion of Kaiser Wilhelm II, studies began for a new armored ship with 283 mm guns. The design requirements changed several times, particularly due to emerging information about new British battleships of the Lord Nelson class. In April 1906, the final version was approved, and battleships were ordered immediately.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>SMS Nassau was built at the Wilhelmshaven shipyard and became Germany's first dreadnought. After the start of World War I, Nassau covered Franz von Hipper's raid on Scarborough and in August 1915 participated in an attempt to break into the Gulf of Riga. During the Battle of Jutland, Nassau, together with Thuringen, sank the British cruiser Black Prince. After World War I, the battleship was transferred to Japan as part of reparations.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB103_FULL"
msgstr "Nassau"

msgid "IDS_PGSB103_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PGSB103_YEAR"
msgstr "1909"

msgid "IDS_PGSB104"
msgstr "Kaiser"

msgid "IDS_PGSB104_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>As a result of changes in the German naval law, it was decided to build battleships to replace old armored ships. The new battleship project represented a step forward: steam turbines were used, and the main battery turrets were arranged more rationally. The ship lost one turret compared to its predecessors, but the two aft turrets were now in a superfiring arrangement, and the two wing turrets were placed diagonally.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>SMS Kaiser, the lead ship of its class, was built in Kiel and launched on the Kaiser's birthday, March 22, 1911. After the start of World War I, the ship covered the raid on Scarborough and Hartlepool and conducted patrol operations. During the Battle of Jutland, the battleship sank the destroyer HMS Nomad and the cruiser HMS Defence, and inflicted heavy damage on the battleship Warspite. In September 1917, the battleship participated in Operation Albion in the Baltic, disabling the destroyer Grom. After Germany's capitulation, Kaiser moved to Scapa Flow, where it was scuttled by its crew on June 21, 1919.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB104_FULL"
msgstr "Kaiser"

msgid "IDS_PGSB104_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PGSB104_YEAR"
msgstr "1912"

msgid "IDS_PGSB105"
msgstr "König"

msgid "IDS_PGSB105_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>According to amendments to the naval law reducing the maximum service life of capital ships, four old armored ships were due for replacement. Development of the new project began in 1910, with the previous battleship type—Kaiser—chosen as the basis. The two wing turrets were replaced by one, located on the ship's centerline.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>SMS König was built at the Wilhelmshaven shipyards and entered service just after the start of World War I. In 1914–16, the battleship participated in raid and patrol operations. During the Battle of Jutland, König, being in the vanguard of the German fleet, sustained heavy damage and took on 1,600 tons of water. In September 1917, the battleship participated in Operation Albion in the Baltic, where it engaged in a duel with the Russian battleship Slava. After Germany's capitulation, König moved to Scapa Flow, where it was scuttled by its crew on June 21, 1919.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB105_FULL"
msgstr "König"

msgid "IDS_PGSB105_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PGSB105_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PGSB106"
msgstr "Bayern"

msgid "IDS_PGSB106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1910, it became clear that future battleships of England, the USA, and other countries would have armament of more than 305 mm caliber. After a period of studies and discussions, a project for a ship armed with eight 380 mm guns in four turrets was approved.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>SMS Bayern, the lead ship of the series, was built in Kiel and entered service shortly after the Battle of Jutland. During World War I, the battleship participated in patrol and raid operations, but its only combat action was Operation Albion—the breakthrough of the Moonsund positions held by the Russian army and fleet. In October 1917, during Operation Albion, Bayern hit a mine, sustaining light damage. After Germany's capitulation, Bayern, along with the rest of the High Seas Fleet, moved to Scapa Flow, where it was scuttled by its crew on June 21, 1919.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB106_FULL"
msgstr "Bayern"

msgid "IDS_PGSB106_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PGSB106_YEAR"
msgstr "1916"

msgid "IDS_PGSB107"
msgstr "Gneisenau"

msgid "IDS_PGSB107_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1933, German engineers began designing ships that exceeded Versailles restrictions. Initially, the new ships were an enlarged version of the Deutschland-class 'pocket battleships,' but the project was revised due to the characteristics of the French Dunkerque-class battlecruisers under construction. To counter 330 mm guns, the new project received enhanced armor, and for adequate firepower, a third turret was added to the ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Gneisenau was built in Kiel. During World War II, the ship participated in the invasion of Denmark and Norway, and in Operations Juno and Berlin. In February 1942, Gneisenau broke through the English Channel and returned to Kiel.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>As a result of an attack by British aircraft, the ship was hit by a bomb in the forecastle, causing an explosion in the forward turret. The guns removed from the battleship were used for coastal defense in Norway. Gneisenau was scuttled on March 27, 1945, due to the advance of the Red Army.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB107_FULL"
msgstr "Gneisenau"

msgid "IDS_PGSB107_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PGSB107_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PGSB108"
msgstr "Bismarck"

msgid "IDS_PGSB108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1932, the German navy began studies for a new battleship, based on a battlecruiser from World War I. During development, the displacement exceeded Washington Treaty limits, which was decided to be concealed. When it became known that France and Italy were building battleships with 380 mm guns, the Germans also increased the main caliber of their battleship.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Bismarck was built at the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg. In May 1941, the battleship put to sea with the cruiser Prinz Eugen on its first and last combat operation. Learning of the enemy's movement, the British ships Prince of Wales and Hood attempted to intercept the German ships on May 24. After several hits, Hood exploded and sank. The German raiders headed into the Atlantic, and the British fleet gave chase. As a result of an attack by torpedo bombers from the aircraft carrier Ark Royal on May 26, Bismarck suffered rudder damage and became unmaneuverable. In the ensuing battle with the British squadron, the battleship was sunk.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB108_FULL"
msgstr "Bismarck"

msgid "IDS_PGSB108_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PGSB108_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSB109"
msgstr "F. der Grosse"

msgid "IDS_PGSB109_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1935, the Anglo-German Naval Agreement was signed, which freed Germany's hands in terms of fleet construction. In 1938–39, Admiral Erich Raeder developed a plan for modernizing and expanding the fleet, with the ultimate goal of defeating the British fleet in a future war.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The core of the fleet was to be six new battleships, representing a modernized version of the Bismarck-class ships with a diesel power plant and increased gun caliber. In 1939, the first preliminary draft variants of ships armed with 406 mm guns were presented. The project, named H-39 after the lead ship's index and the year of development.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In April 1939, the construction of two ships of this type was ordered, and in May—four more. In July, the first ship was laid down at the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg, and in September, construction began on the second in Bremen. With the start of World War II, construction was suspended, and by the end of 1941, it was canceled altogether.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB109_FULL"
msgstr "Friedrich der Grosse"

msgid "IDS_PGSB109_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PGSB109_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PGSB110"
msgstr "G. Kurfürst"

msgid "IDS_PGSB110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After studying the loss of the battleship Bismarck and the damage to the battleship Scharnhorst in July 1941, the German naval leadership conducted a review of the under-construction H and J ships. One requirement was to strengthen horizontal armor while maintaining a draft of 11.5 m for operations in the shallow North Sea.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During development, the project acquired a triple bottom and an increased number of watertight compartments for protection against underwater explosions. Additionally, it was planned to increase the main battery caliber to give the new battleships a significant advantage over British and American battleships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The changes introduced into the project were significant, and the construction of the two already laid-down ships was discontinued. It was assumed that ships according to the new project would be laid down in 1942. However, soon thereafter, construction for the German navy was reoriented towards submarines, and no new battleships were laid down.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB110_FULL"
msgstr "Grosser Kurfürst"

msgid "IDS_PGSB110_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PGSB110_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PGSB111"
msgstr "Hannover"

msgid "IDS_PGSB111_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Further development of the H project with enhanced main battery artillery. The next step after 457 mm guns would have been to increase the caliber to nineteen inches, which is 483 mm. Such a caliber would have made German battleships the most heavily armed ships in the history of navies.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>By the time of design, it was planned to completely abandon non-universal secondary artillery, replacing it with universal artillery.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB111_FULL"
msgstr "Hannover"

msgid "IDS_PGSB111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB111_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSB203"
msgstr "Von der Tann"

msgid "IDS_PGSB203_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The German navy required a qualitative leap to fight British battlecruisers. The new ship was armed with four turrets with 283 mm guns, as on the Nassau-class battleships. Steam engines were replaced by turbines, significantly increasing speed, and the armor already significantly surpassed the British.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>SMS Von der Tann was built at the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg. With the start of World War I, the ship participated in raids on Yarmouth, Scarborough, and Hartlepool in attempts to lure the Grand Fleet from its bases. In the summer of 1915, Von der Tann participated in skirmishes with Russian cruisers in the Baltic. During the Battle of Jutland, the ship sank the battlecruiser Indefatigable but itself received several hits from British ships, resulting in all of Von der Tann's artillery being disabled.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The battlecruiser conducted several more unsuccessful raids into the North Sea. After Germany's capitulation, Von der Tann moved to Scapa Flow, where it was scuttled by its crew on June 21, 1919.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB203_FULL"
msgstr "Von der Tann"

msgid "IDS_PGSB203_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB203_YEAR"
msgstr "1910"

msgid "IDS_PGSB204"
msgstr "Moltke"

msgid "IDS_PGSB204_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After developing the Von der Tann battlecruiser, it was decided to build the next ship according to an improved design. At the insistence of Grand Admiral Alfred von Tirpitz, the number of main battery turrets was increased to five. The additional turret was located superfiring in the stern—following the example of the Kaiser-class battleships. Armor was also strengthened, leading to a significant increase in displacement.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>SMS Moltke was built at the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg. During World War I, the ship participated in raids on Yarmouth, Lowestoft, Scarborough, and Hartlepool, the Battle of the Heligoland Bight, the Battle of Dogger Bank, the attempt to break into the Gulf of Riga, the Battle of Jutland, and Operation Albion. After Germany's capitulation, Moltke moved to Scapa Flow, where it was scuttled by its crew on June 21, 1919.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB204_FULL"
msgstr "Moltke"

msgid "IDS_PGSB204_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PGSB204_YEAR"
msgstr "1911"

msgid "IDS_PGSB205"
msgstr "Derfflinger"

msgid "IDS_PGSB205_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the Agadir Crisis of 1911, funds were allocated for the construction of new ships for the German navy. Due to the strengthening of armor on British ships, new projects envisaged an increase in main caliber: eight 305 mm guns in four turrets were arranged longitudinally in a superfiring scheme.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>SMS Derfflinger was built at the Blohm & Voss shipyard. During World War I, the ship participated in the raid on Scarborough, the Battle of Dogger Bank, and the raid on Yarmouth and Lowestoft.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>At the Battle of Jutland, Derfflinger suffered the heaviest losses among the unsunk German ships. For its resilience, the ship earned the nickname 'Iron Dog' from the British. After Germany's capitulation, Derfflinger was scuttled by its crew at Scapa Flow on June 21, 1919.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1939, the ship's hull was raised and anchored upside down off the island of Risa-Little. The ship remained there until 1948. Thus, Derfflinger spent more time upside down than in its natural position.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB205_FULL"
msgstr "Derfflinger"

msgid "IDS_PGSB205_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PGSB205_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PGSB206"
msgstr "Mackensen"

msgid "IDS_PGSB206_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1912, Germany began designing a battlecruiser for laying down in 1914. The issue of artillery armament was particularly acute: increasing the caliber to 305 mm on previous projects did not provide a significant advantage in firepower over British ships. At the same time, ship sizes were also limited: few docks in Germany could accommodate ships wider than thirty meters.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Two armament options were considered for the project: Kaiser Wilhelm insisted on six 380 mm guns in three turrets, while the commander of the High Seas Fleet, Admiral Friedrich von Ingenohl, wanted to install ten 305 mm guns on the ships. As a compromise, it was decided to arm the battlecruisers with eight 350 mm guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>SMS Mackensen was built at the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg. Approximately 15 months remained until the ship's completion when work stopped due to the end of hostilities in World War I.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB206_FULL"
msgstr "Mackensen"

msgid "IDS_PGSB206_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PGSB206_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PGSB207"
msgstr "P. Heinrich"

msgid "IDS_PGSB207_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1915, it became clear that the war was dragging on, and Germany needed to build new ships to replace losses. Kaiser Wilhelm insisted that the new ships be armed with 380 mm guns and combine the speed of a battlecruiser with the armor of a battleship.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the series was laid down in July 1916, and in August, the designs were presented. The final version was a Mackensen enlarged by 2,500 tons with eight 380 mm guns. The end of the war changed German naval commanders' views on strategy, and all efforts were directed towards the construction and repair of submarines. The laid-down ship was dismantled on the slipway after the war.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB207_FULL"
msgstr "Prinz Heinrich"

msgid "IDS_PGSB207_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB207_YEAR"
msgstr "1916"

msgid "IDS_PGSB208"
msgstr "Zieten"

msgid "IDS_PGSB208_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1918, the design department of the German Naval Construction Administration conducted a series of studies, resulting in several dozen preliminary draft designs for the so-called Grosskampfschiff—a fast battleship.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The designs were based on the battlecruiser laid down in July 1916 and differed in displacement and armament composition. All these variants significantly exceeded the size of existing docks in Germany. One project envisaged a ship of 45,000 tons displacement, armed with three main battery turrets.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB208_FULL"
msgstr "Zieten"

msgid "IDS_PGSB208_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB208_YEAR"
msgstr "1918"

msgid "IDS_PGSB209"
msgstr "P. Rupprecht"

msgid "IDS_PGSB209_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>One of the projects studied in 1918 by the designers of the Naval Construction Administration of the German Naval Staff. The ship corresponded to the concept of a fast battleship, although it was initially designated as a battlecruiser on the drawing.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The project envisaged armament of four main battery turrets and a displacement of 45,000 tons. The designers returned to the half-bow ship scheme, which had been abandoned during the design of the Derfflinger-class battlecruisers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB209_FULL"
msgstr "Prinz Rupprecht"

msgid "IDS_PGSB209_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB209_YEAR"
msgstr "1918"

msgid "IDS_PGSB210"
msgstr "Schlieffen"

msgid "IDS_PGSB210_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A project for a fast battleship with four main battery turrets and a displacement of 50,000 tons. It differs from its predecessor by an increased length of 30 m and is the largest of the preliminary drafts developed by the designers of the Naval Construction Administration of the German Naval Staff in 1918.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>This is the project for the largest and most heavily armed of all German battleships until the studies for the H battleship conducted in the early 1940s.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB210_FULL"
msgstr "Schlieffen"

msgid "IDS_PGSB210_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB210_YEAR"
msgstr "1918"

msgid "IDS_PGSB211"
msgstr "Seydlitz"

msgid "IDS_PGSB211_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1918, German designers presented several dozen preliminary design proposals for a high-speed battleship—Grosskampfschiffe. The largest of the developed variants was designated GK-5041 and featured an armament of four main battery turrets with a displacement of 50,000 tons.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In a hypothetical version of this project, the battleship is armed with 450 mm main battery guns.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB211_FULL"
msgstr "Seydlitz"

msgid "IDS_PGSB211_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB211_YEAR"
msgstr "1918"

msgid "IDS_PGSB310"
msgstr "Preussen"

msgid "IDS_PGSB310_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>During World War II, the German naval leadership studied the experience gained, particularly the loss of the battleship Bismarck and the damage to the battleship Scharnhorst in July 1941. The conclusions drawn led to a revision of the characteristics of the under-construction H and J ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>It was required to strengthen horizontal armor while maintaining a draft of 11.5 m for operations in the shallow North Sea. The project acquired a triple bottom and more watertight compartments for protection against underwater explosions. The main caliber of future ships was also increased.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Subsequently, construction for the fleet was reoriented towards submarines—no new battleships were laid down.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB310_FULL"
msgstr "Preussen"

msgid "IDS_PGSB310_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB310_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSB503"
msgstr "K. Albert"

msgid "IDS_PGSB503_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>SMS König Albert, a Kaiser-class battleship, was built at the Schichau shipyards in Danzig. After commissioning, the battleship went on a tour of South America and German Africa together with the sister ship Kaiser.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Before World War I, König Albert joined the third battle squadron of the High Seas Fleet. In 1914–16, the battleship participated in covering raid operations and mine-laying. Two days before the Battle of Jutland, König Albert went to Wilhelmshaven for repairs, becoming the only German battleship to miss the famous battle.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In September 1917, König Albert participated in Operation Albion in the Gulf of Riga. After the war, the battleship, along with other ships of the High Seas Fleet, moved to Scapa Flow, where it was scuttled by its crew on June 21, 1919.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB503_FULL"
msgstr "König Albert"

msgid "IDS_PGSB503_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PGSB503_YEAR"
msgstr "1913"

msgid "IDS_PGSB506"
msgstr "P. E. Friedrich"

msgid "IDS_PGSB506_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>SMS Prinz Eitel Friedrich, a Mackensen-class battlecruiser, was built at the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg. The ship was named after one of the sons of Kaiser Wilhelm II.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The battleship was not launched before the end of World War I because priority in shipbuilding was given to submarines. The ship was launched only in March 1920. It was proposed to convert the ship into a grain carrier—in which case it could have been completed as a battleship in the 1930s. However, the proposal was not accepted, and the ship's hull was dismantled in 1921.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB506_FULL"
msgstr "Prinz Eitel Friedrich"

msgid "IDS_PGSB506_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PGSB506_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PGSB507"
msgstr "Scharnhorst"

msgid "IDS_PGSB507_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Scharnhorst, the lead ship of its class, was built at the Wilhelmshaven shipyards. Almost immediately after entering service, the ship returned to the shipyard, where the straight stem was replaced by an 'Atlantic' bow.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>With the start of World War II, the battleship, together with the sister ship Gneisenau, began active participation in raid operations. In February 1940, the battleship raided the Shetland Islands; in April, it covered the invasion of Denmark and Norway. In June, as a result of Operation Juno, the battleships sank the British aircraft carrier Glorious. In early 1941, Scharnhorst participated in Operation Berlin—a raid into the Atlantic, during which several British merchant ships were sunk.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After repairs, the ship moved to Norway to attack Arctic convoys supplying the USSR. During an attack on a convoy on December 26, 1943, Scharnhorst sustained heavy damage from the British battleship Duke of York and was subsequently sunk by torpedoes from the cruisers Jamaica and Belfast.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB507_FULL"
msgstr "Scharnhorst"

msgid "IDS_PGSB507_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PGSB507_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PGSB508"
msgstr "Odin"

msgid "IDS_PGSB508_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1932–34, as part of the further development of the Deutschland-class ships, more than a dozen projects were considered. In July 1934, it was decided to add a third main battery turret to the ships, leading to a significant increase in displacement. This was deemed justified, as the likely opponents of the new ships were to be the French Dunkerque-class battlecruisers. In early 1935, the characteristics of the French battlecruiser Strasbourg became known, and the project was adjusted once more.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Options with 330 mm and 380 mm guns were considered, but the variant with 305 mm main battery guns was considered the most balanced. Moreover, this was the caliber Great Britain wanted to propose as the main one at the future naval conference. However, redesigning the already under-construction ships would have taken about a year and a half, and the changes were abandoned. As a result, the ships were completed as Scharnhorst and Gneisenau.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB508_FULL"
msgstr "Odin"

msgid "IDS_PGSB508_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB508_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PGSB509"
msgstr "Pommern"

msgid "IDS_PGSB509_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>When designing the H battleship, the main requirement was firepower superior to all existing analogues in the world. The leadership insisted on a 420 mm caliber, which would surpass the main caliber of battleships of any other country (the true caliber of the Yamato's guns became known to the world only after the end of World War II). However, installing guns of such caliber on a ship would violate the Washington Naval Treaty, which Germany had pledged to adhere to.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>A possible solution would have been to switch to a 406 mm caliber, but such guns did not exist in Germany, and their development would have taken a long time. Another option envisaged increasing the number of 380 mm guns to twelve—in four triple turrets. In the end, the naval leadership settled on 406 mm guns, and the variant with twelve 380 mm guns was not realized.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB509_FULL"
msgstr "Pommern"

msgid "IDS_PGSB509_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB509_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSB510"
msgstr "Lothringen"

msgid "IDS_PGSB510_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>German naval doctrine envisaged raider actions by large ships in the Atlantic against British trade and supply. For this purpose, from the late 1920s–early 1930s, ships were developed, including aircraft-carrying ones with cruiser and battleship artillery armament. Among the assumed targets for such ships were British aircraft carriers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Atlantik-Flugzeugkreuzer A-III project envisaged the installation of two triple turrets with large-caliber guns, and in terms of armor, such a ship was to correspond to battleships. Further development of the hybrid aircraft carrier/battleship concept could have been based on the H-39 class battleships laid down in 1939, whose dimensions approximated the A-III project.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB510_FULL"
msgstr "Lothringen"

msgid "IDS_PGSB510_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB510_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PGSB517"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PGSB517_DESCR"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PGSB517_FULL"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PGSB517_NUMBER"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PGSB517_YEAR"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PGSB518"
msgstr "Brandenburg"

msgid "IDS_PGSB518_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1932, the German navy began conceptual studies for a 35,000-ton battleship project. The basis for the future ship was a World War I-era battlecruiser project laid down in July 1916. To select optimal armament, protection, and speed, variants with different armament and armor were studied.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Simultaneously, the German naval leadership ascertained Great Britain's position regarding Germany's entry into the system of international naval treaties. At that time, Britain was inclined to reduce the maximum battleship caliber to 305 mm, so such guns were considered as the main ones for the future German ship. Moreover, such a caliber suggested maximally balanced armor for the planned displacement. However, France laid down the Dunkerque battlecruiser with 330 mm guns, so Germany abandoned the idea of a battleship with 305 mm artillery.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB518_FULL"
msgstr "Brandenburg"

msgid "IDS_PGSB518_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB518_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSB528"
msgstr "Anhalt"

msgid "IDS_PGSB528_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1916, Germany returned to designing a new type of battleship. The main task was to increase the ships' firepower. In April, three preliminary draft projects based on the Bayern battleship were presented to Kaiser Wilhelm II.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>One of the projects differed from the others by having five main battery turrets, three of which were located in the stern. However, the additional turret was positioned too high, making the entire ship unstable due to a high center of gravity. The problem could be solved by increasing the ship's length and lowering two turrets to a lower deck.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship's armament in one variant was envisaged as five triple turrets with 350 mm guns—such turrets had been proposed for the future Bayern by Grand Admiral Alfred von Tirpitz in 1912. As a result of Germany's defeat in World War I, ships according to these designs were not laid down.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB528_FULL"
msgstr "Anhalt"

msgid "IDS_PGSB528_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB528_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PGSB537"
msgstr "Pfalz"

msgid "IDS_PGSB537_DESCR"
msgstr "A hypothetical project for the modernization of a Bayern-class battleship. This variant suggests replacing the twin turrets with 380 mm guns with triple turrets with 283 mm guns."

msgid "IDS_PGSB537_FULL"
msgstr "Pfalz"

msgid "IDS_PGSB537_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB537_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PGSB538"
msgstr "Brandenburg B"

msgid "IDS_PGSB538_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1932, the German navy began conceptual studies for a 35,000-ton battleship project. The basis for the future ship was a World War I-era battlecruiser project laid down in July 1916. To select optimal armament, protection, and speed, variants with different armament and armor were studied.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Simultaneously, the German naval leadership ascertained Great Britain's position regarding Germany's entry into the system of international naval treaties. At that time, Britain was inclined to reduce the maximum battleship caliber to 305 mm, so such guns were considered as the main ones for the future German ship. Moreover, such a caliber suggested maximally balanced armor for the planned displacement. However, France laid down the Dunkerque battlecruiser with 330 mm guns, so Germany abandoned the idea of a battleship with 305 mm artillery.</p>"

