msgid "IDS_PGSB991_YEAR"
msgstr "1918"

msgid "IDS_PGSB999"
msgstr "Hannover (old)"

msgid "IDS_PGSB999_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Further development of the H project with enhanced main battery artillery. The next step after 457 mm guns would have been to increase the caliber to nineteen inches, which is 483 mm. Such a caliber would have made German battleships the most heavily armed ships in the history of navies.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>By the time of design, it was planned to completely abandon non-universal secondary artillery, replacing it with universal artillery.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSB999_FULL"
msgstr "Hannover (< 22.09.2021)"

msgid "IDS_PGSB999_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSB999_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSC001"
msgstr "Hermelin"

msgid "IDS_PGSC001_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>German engineers developed a project for a 'colonial gunboat' on order from the Netherlands. The Dutch planned to build a series of seven ships and laid down three of them according to a simplified design. When the Netherlands was occupied, these ships fell into German hands and were completed for the German navy.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The original project surpassed the Dutch ships in size and armament. There is an assumption that the German Admiralty planned to build ships according to the original project for post-war service at future German remote bases.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC001_FULL"
msgstr "Hermelin"

msgid "IDS_PGSC001_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PGSC001_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PGSC002"
msgstr "Dresden"

msgid "IDS_PGSC002_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>At the turn of the 20th century, the German naval command considered combating enemy torpedo boats the main task of light cruisers. Accordingly, ships of this class were armed with a large number of rapid-fire small-caliber guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After three series of small ships with steam engines, Germany proceeded to build ships with turbines. The Dresden type became transitional: the lead ship was equipped with the latest turbines, while the cruiser Emden carried proven triple-expansion steam engines.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>SMS Dresden, the lead ship of the type, was built at the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg. During World War I, Dresden participated in the Battle of Coronel, and in the Battle of the Falkland Islands became the only surviving German ship. In March 1915, the ship was intercepted by British cruisers while attempting internment on the Chilean island of Más a Tierra. In the ensuing battle, the ship was sunk, and the crew was interned in Chile.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC002_FULL"
msgstr "Dresden"

msgid "IDS_PGSC002_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PGSC002_YEAR"
msgstr "1908"

msgid "IDS_PGSC103"
msgstr "Kolberg"

msgid "IDS_PGSC103_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Kolberg type became the first series of German cruisers in which all ships were equipped with turbines. These cruisers were larger than their predecessors and had higher speed and seaworthiness, carrying more guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>SMS Kolberg was built in Danzig at the Schichau shipyard. Before World War I, the cruiser participated in reviews and exercises, often escorting Kaiser Wilhelm's yacht. With the start of the war, the ship participated in raids on Yarmouth and Scarborough, fought in the Battle of Dogger Bank in January 1915, and in August of the same year, together with the main fleet forces, attempted to break into the Gulf of Riga. In 1917, Kolberg repeated the breakthrough attempt in Operation Albion, this time successfully.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After World War I, the ship was transferred to France, where it entered service in 1922 under the name Colmar. In French service, the ship participated mainly in colonial campaigns.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC103_FULL"
msgstr "Kolberg"

msgid "IDS_PGSC103_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PGSC103_YEAR"
msgstr "1910"

msgid "IDS_PGSC104"
msgstr "Karlsruhe"

msgid "IDS_PGSC104_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The next stage in the development of German light cruisers was the appearance of an armor belt. It first appeared on the Magdeburg-class cruisers and was subsequently installed on all German ships of this class.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The pinnacle of development for German light cruisers at the start of World War I was the Königsberg class. Structurally, it repeated previous types but had increased displacement and improved seaworthiness. The new ships received names in honor of cruisers already lost in the war.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>SMS Karlsruhe was built in Kiel. After mandatory crew training, the cruiser joined the 2nd Scouting Squadron, which patrolled the German Bight. The ship performed reconnaissance duties and escorted minesweepers. In the autumn of 1917, Karlsruhe participated in Operation Albion—an attempt to break into the Gulf of Riga. After Germany's capitulation, the cruiser was transferred to Scapa Flow, where it was scuttled by its own crew on June 21, 1919.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC104_FULL"
msgstr "Karlsruhe"

msgid "IDS_PGSC104_NUMBER"
msgstr "14"

msgid "IDS_PGSC104_YEAR"
msgstr "1916"

msgid "IDS_PGSC105"
msgstr "Königsberg"

msgid "IDS_PGSC105_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Versailles Treaty allowed the German navy to have six light cruisers, each with a displacement not exceeding 6,000 tons. In 1921–25, Germany built the first post-war cruiser, whose design was based on wartime projects.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1926, three ships of an improved design were laid down. The armament was placed in three triple turrets, two of which were located in the stern. Both aft turrets were offset from the centerline to facilitate firing at sharp angles. A combined diesel-steam turbine power plant was used.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Königsberg, the lead ship of the type, was built in Wilhelmshaven. At the start of World War II, the cruiser laid minefields in the North Sea and then participated in the invasion of Norway. In April 1940, during the naval landing in Bergen, Königsberg was damaged by Norwegian coastal batteries and sank. The next day, British bombers destroyed the cruiser.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC105_FULL"
msgstr "Königsberg"

msgid "IDS_PGSC105_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PGSC105_YEAR"
msgstr "1929"

msgid "IDS_PGSC106"
msgstr "Nürnberg"

msgid "IDS_PGSC106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A ship built to an improved design based on the K-class cruisers. Nürnberg differed with more developed superstructures and some changes in armor.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The cruiser Nürnberg was built in Kiel. At the start of World War II, the cruiser laid minefields in the North Sea. In December 1939, during one such operation, it was torpedoed by a British submarine. After repairs, the cruiser served in Norway, and from April 1943, it was in the Baltic as a training ship. After the war, Nürnberg was transferred to the Soviet Union, in whose fleet it served until 1959 under the name Admiral Makarov.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC106_FULL"
msgstr "Nürnberg"

msgid "IDS_PGSC106_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC106_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PGSC107"
msgstr "Yorck"

msgid "IDS_PGSC107_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>As a result of World War I, Germany signed the Treaty of Versailles, significantly limiting its armed forces. According to the treaty, Germany was allowed to have six old battleships of the Deutschland and Braunschweig types in its fleet, which upon reaching their maximum age could be replaced by ships with a displacement of no more than 10,000 tons each.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the 1920s, the naval leadership initiated a series of studies on future ships to replace the old battleships. Several options were proposed, including Entwurf I/10, which had armament of eight 8-inch guns in four turrets and was close to 'Washington' cruisers. The project was not realized, and its further development led to the appearance of 'pocket battleships'.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC107_FULL"
msgstr "Yorck"

msgid "IDS_PGSC107_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PGSC107_YEAR"
msgstr "1923"

msgid "IDS_PGSC108"
msgstr "Hipper"

msgid "IDS_PGSC108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In early 1934, technical specifications were developed for a heavy cruiser project, whose intended opponents were to be French and British ships of this class. The first draft designs appeared in the summer. Engineers abandoned the diesel power plant in favor of turbines and boilers with high steam parameters.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Admiral Hipper, the lead cruiser of the type, was built in Kiel. At the start of World War II, an 'Atlantic' bow was installed on the cruiser. During the invasion of Denmark and Norway, Hipper was the flagship of the 2nd group of ships conducting the landing at Trondheim. During the operation, Admiral Hipper was rammed by the British destroyer Glowworm.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1940–42, the ship conducted raids into the Atlantic and attacks on convoys. In December 1942, Hipper was damaged during an attack on convoy JW51B in the Barents Sea. The cruiser was placed in reserve, where it remained until the end of the war.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC108_FULL"
msgstr "Admiral Hipper"

msgid "IDS_PGSC108_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PGSC108_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PGSC109"
msgstr "Roon"

msgid "IDS_PGSC109_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the denunciation of the Versailles Treaty and the signing of the Anglo-German Naval Agreement, the German Naval Staff decided to further develop the Deutschland-type ships. In 1937, several preliminary draft projects designated 'Cruiser P' were considered, and in 1938, the construction of such ships was included in the German fleet modernization program. The raider cruisers were to cut off Great Britain from its sources of supply and raw materials.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>More than twenty variants of preliminary draft projects were considered. The ship's speed remained one of the most difficult issues. To achieve the required characteristics, it was possible to reduce the main gun caliber to the 'Washington' 203 mm. The optimal option would have been the placement of three triple turrets. Of the twelve ordered ships, not one was built due to the start of World War II.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC109_FULL"
msgstr "Roon"

msgid "IDS_PGSC109_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PGSC109_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PGSC110"
msgstr "Hindenburg"

msgid "IDS_PGSC110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The further development of the Admiral Hipper-class cruisers, unconstrained by 'Washington' limitations, could have been ships armed with four triple turrets with 203 mm guns. Such gun mounts had already been developed for possible installation on raider ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Twelve 203 mm guns would have made the new German cruisers the strongest in their class until the appearance of the American Des Moines-class ships with rapid-fire mounts. However, priorities in shipbuilding were given to submarines, and such a ship was not built in Germany.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC110_FULL"
msgstr "Hindenburg"

msgid "IDS_PGSC110_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PGSC110_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSC111"
msgstr "Clausewitz"

msgid "IDS_PGSC111_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The next step in the development of German raider cruisers, based on the Admiral Hipper type, would have been the use of the latest artillery. 210 mm guns could have been used as the main caliber, and the ship could also have received 128 mm universal mounts, 55 mm twin AA mounts, and 30 mm quadruple and twin mounts.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In this case, the ship's displacement could have exceeded 20,000 tons. A combined diesel-steam turbine electric plant, improved compared to those used in the 1930s, would have allowed combining high speed with long range.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC111_FULL"
msgstr "Clausewitz"

msgid "IDS_PGSC111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC111_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSC207"
msgstr "Vineta"

msgid "IDS_PGSC207_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The German naval development program of the late 1930s envisaged the construction of six light cruisers—squadron scouts for raider groups. The last two ships of this type were to be built to an improved design: increased size and heavy anti-aircraft armament, as well as improved AA fire control.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>A radical option was the replacement of all main battery turrets with twin mounts of universal 128 mm guns. The turrets could have been installed in the ends in groups of three: in a triangle with the vertex facing the stem.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC207_FULL"
msgstr "Vineta"

msgid "IDS_PGSC207_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC207_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PGSC208"
msgstr "Hertha"

msgid "IDS_PGSC208_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Under the German fleet development plan adopted in 1939, light cruisers were planned for construction, intended for use as squadron scouts for raider groups.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Two projects differed from the others in increased size and heavy anti-aircraft armament with improved fire control. Thus, all main battery guns could have been replaced with twin mounts of universal 128 mm guns. Six turrets could have been installed in groups of three in the ends, 'stepped' along the ship's centerline.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC208_FULL"
msgstr "Hertha"

msgid "IDS_PGSC208_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC208_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PGSC209"
msgstr "Freya"

msgid "IDS_PGSC209_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Further development of the AA cruiser concept designed for the German fleet. The project envisages armament of universal main battery artillery.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship of this project is based on the hull of a Prinz Eugen-class cruiser and is armed with seven twin 128 mm gun mounts. Three such mounts are located in the ends on the centerline, and another four are placed amidships on the beams.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC209_FULL"
msgstr "Freya"

msgid "IDS_PGSC209_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC209_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSC210"
msgstr "Hansa"

msgid "IDS_PGSC210_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Hypothetical development of a German light cruiser armed with universal main battery artillery.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>This project is based on the hull of a Prinz Eugen-class cruiser, and its armament consists of eight twin 128 mm gun mounts. Four main battery turrets are located in the ends on the ship's centerline, and another four are placed amidships on the beams.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC210_FULL"
msgstr "Hansa"

msgid "IDS_PGSC210_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC210_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSC502"
msgstr "Emden"

msgid "IDS_PGSC502_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>SMS Emden, the second cruiser of the Dresden type, was built in Danzig. It spent almost its entire pre-war career in the Far East, where it met the start of World War I as part of Maximilian von Spee's squadron. In September 1914, the ship was sent to the Indian Ocean under the command of Captain Karl von Müller. After capturing two dozen ships in two months, Emden, in a surprise attack in the port of Penang, sank the Russian cruiser Zhemchug and the French destroyer Mousquet.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In November 1914, during a raid on the Cocos Islands, Emden was intercepted by the Australian cruiser Sydney. After a short battle, Emden sustained heavy damage and ran aground on a coastal reef. The remains of the cruiser were located near the coast of the Cocos Islands until the 1950s.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC502_FULL"
msgstr "Emden"

msgid "IDS_PGSC502_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PGSC502_YEAR"
msgstr "1909"

msgid "IDS_PGSC506"
msgstr "Graf Spee"

msgid "IDS_PGSC506_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The third 'pocket battleship' of the Deutschland class was built in Wilhelmshaven. Admiral Graf Spee differed from its two predecessors in larger size and improved armor.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Immediately after entering service, the ship became the flagship of the German fleet. At the start of World War II, Admiral Graf Spee set off on its first and last raid into the Atlantic. From September to December, it managed to capture and sink several British merchant ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In December 1939, the ship encountered the British cruisers Exeter, Ajax, and Achilles in the Battle of the River Plate, heavily damaging the first and inflicting damage on the others. The 'pocket battleship' itself was also damaged and headed to Montevideo for repairs. Britain transmitted disinformation by radio: it was reported that Admiral Graf Spee would be met by British battlecruisers and aircraft carriers upon exiting the port. After consulting with Berlin, the 'pocket battleship's' commander, Hans Langsdorff, ordered the ship scuttled, which was done on December 17, 1939.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC506_FULL"
msgstr "Admiral Graf Spee"

msgid "IDS_PGSC506_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PGSC506_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PGSC507"
msgstr "München"

msgid "IDS_PGSC507_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The German fleet modernization plan envisaged thirteen light cruisers in service, six of which had already been built by the start of 1939. Six cruisers built to an improved design were to be added to them: they were to differ from previous types in improved range and higher speed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1938–39, six ships were ordered, two of which were laid down in Kiel and Wilhelmshaven in 1938. In September 1939, the construction of the ships was canceled due to the start of World War II, and the laid-down cruisers were dismantled on the slipway.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC507_FULL"
msgstr "München"

msgid "IDS_PGSC507_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC507_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PGSC508"
msgstr "Prinz Eugen"

msgid "IDS_PGSC508_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Prinz Eugen, the third cruiser of the Admiral Hipper class, was built to a slightly modified design in Kiel. In May 1941, together with the battleship Bismarck, the ship sailed into the North Sea. The cruiser participated in the Battle of the Denmark Strait, where the British battleship HMS Hood was lost. Prinz Eugen broke away from the British pursuit and managed to reach Brest.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In February 1942, the cruiser, together with the battleships Scharnhorst and Gneisenau, broke through the English Channel, after which it was hit by a torpedo from the British submarine HMS Trident. After repairs, the cruiser was used as a training ship in the Baltic and then returned to service, supporting German troops in the Baltic states.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In May 1945, in Copenhagen, Prinz Eugen surrendered to the British fleet. After World War II, the ship was transferred to the USA and then participated in nuclear tests at Bikini Atoll. In August 1946, the former cruiser was decommissioned, and in December, it sank near Kwajalein Atoll.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC508_FULL"
msgstr "Prinz Eugen"

msgid "IDS_PGSC508_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PGSC508_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSC509"
msgstr "Siegfried"

msgid "IDS_PGSC509_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>According to the initial plan for the war against Great Britain, the main striking force of the raider formations was to be twelve P-class cruisers. However, in early 1938, the German command decided that it was necessary to support its raiders with full-fledged battlecruisers with heavy guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The hull of the new project ship was enlarged to accommodate three gun mounts, and the gun caliber was increased to 380 mm. The fully diesel power plant was replaced by a combined one. In August 1939, the first ship of this type out of three planned was ordered from the Germaniawerft shipyard. However, the laying down of none of the battlecruisers took place due to an acute shortage of materials, which with the start of World War II went primarily to the construction of submarines.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC509_FULL"
msgstr "Siegfried"

msgid "IDS_PGSC509_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC509_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSC516"
msgstr "Leipzig"

msgid "IDS_PGSC516_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The three Königsberg-class cruisers, built in the late 1920s, proved to be ships with a finicky power plant and a weak hull. On the next cruiser type, the power plant was largely redesigned, adding a central shaft that used only diesel engines. The turrets returned to the centerline.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Leipzig, the lead ship of the type, was built in Wilhelmshaven. At the start of World War II, the ship participated in mine-laying and patrols. In December 1939, Leipzig was torpedoed by the British submarine HMS Salmon, and after repairs were completed in late 1940, it became a training ship.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1941–42, Leipzig supported the offensive of German armies in the Baltic states. In October 1944, the ship collided with the cruiser Prinz Eugen and sustained serious damage. Due to the impracticality of repairs, the ship was used as a barracks hulk, and in 1946, the former cruiser was scuttled in the Baltic Sea.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC516_FULL"
msgstr "Leipzig"

msgid "IDS_PGSC516_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC516_YEAR"
msgstr "1931"

msgid "IDS_PGSC517"
msgstr "Weimar"

msgid "IDS_PGSC517_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>When developing 10,000-ton ships to replace old battleships, German engineers considered several armament options. The 'lightest' considered was project I/10 with eight 210 mm guns in four turrets.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>At the same time, the first modern German light cruisers received armament of 150 mm guns in triple turrets. The size and arrangement of project I/10 вполне allowed for the placement of four such turrets, replacing the 210 mm mounts.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC517_FULL"
msgstr "Weimar"

msgid "IDS_PGSC517_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC517_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PGSC518"
msgstr "Mainz"

msgid "IDS_PGSC518_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>During the design of the Admiral Hipper-class heavy cruisers, German engineers encountered the fact that the proposed project did not fit within the 'Washington' limitations for standard displacement. The armor was not to be inferior to the French cruiser Algérie, which, combined with the required speed and range, led to the total tonnage significantly exceeding the limits. One way to reduce displacement was to reduce the caliber of the main guns: a transition to 190 mm or 170 mm was discussed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The issue of caliber reduction was revisited in 1936 when Germany ordered two cruisers with armament of twelve 150 mm guns in four turrets. However, these cruisers were designed such that their barbettes were identical to the barbettes of cruisers with 203 mm guns, which could easily be installed instead of the 150 mm. Nevertheless, neither of these ships was completed in Germany as a cruiser.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC518_FULL"
msgstr "Mainz"

msgid "IDS_PGSC518_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC518_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PGSC519"
msgstr "Ägir"

msgid "IDS_PGSC519_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The raider battlecruisers were to carry six 380 mm guns in three turrets. However, there was an opinion that such a caliber was excessive for a raider, as the number of shells was limited, and the shells themselves were clearly excessive for destroying merchant ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>A compromise between sufficient firepower and shell power would have been the use of triple 305 mm mounts. Three such mounts would provide firepower comparable to three twin 380 mm caliber mounts.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC519_FULL"
msgstr "Ägir"

msgid "IDS_PGSC519_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC519_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSC520"
msgstr "Falkenhayn"

msgid "IDS_PGSC520_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Hypothetical development of the German navy's heavy cruisers of the late 1930s–early 1940s. Considering the development of the 'Washington' heavy cruiser class in other countries, a symmetrical response would have been a cruiser project with heavier armament.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The main difference from previous projects is the presence of a universal caliber, unified in mounts with the H-42 project battleships. Such a solution would have provided better interaction in formation when repelling enemy air attacks.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC520_FULL"
msgstr "Falkenhayn"

msgid "IDS_PGSC520_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC520_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PGSC528"
msgstr "Schill"

msgid "IDS_PGSC528_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the late 1930s, the German naval leadership developed a project for a naval blockade of Great Britain by raider formations. The main striking force in this plan was to be 12 P-class ships, created based on the development of the Deutschland-class armored ship project.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1939, contracts were signed with shipyards for the construction of these ships, but the order was subsequently canceled in favor of the O-class battlecruisers. In the original documentation, the ship type is indicated as Handelszerstörer—'raider'.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC528_FULL"
msgstr "Schill"

msgid "IDS_PGSC528_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC528_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PGSC529"
msgstr "Schröder"

msgid "IDS_PGSC529_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the late 1920s, as Germany reflected on its experiences during World War I, the concept of a large surface raider emerged. Several designs were developed for such a ship, taking into account Britain’s desire at future naval conferences to limit battleship armament to a 305mm caliber.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Depending on the design, the ship’s standard displacement ranged from 17,500 to 25,000 tons, and its armament typically consisted of eight 305mm guns in four turrets along with a robust secondary battery of 150mm guns. The armor and anti-torpedo protection were considered too light for the vessel to be classified as a true battleship.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC529_FULL"
msgstr "Admiral Schröder"

msgid "IDS_PGSC529_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC529_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSC530"
msgstr "Wotan"

msgid "IDS_PGSC530_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In early 1938, the command of the German Navy decided that the German fleet needed full-fledged battlecruisers with heavy guns. This led to the development of the O-class battlecruiser-raider project. The ship was planned to be equipped with a combined power plant, and its armament was to consist of three mounts with 380 mm caliber guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The hypothetical development of this concept includes increasing the number of main battery guns and strengthening the anti-aircraft armament.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC530_FULL"
msgstr "Wotan"

msgid "IDS_PGSC530_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC530_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PGSC538"
msgstr "Sabie"

msgid "IDS_PGSC538_DESCR"
msgstr "A warship embodying Nokmar's ideals: unyielding Will, discipline, and faith in the boundless power of the human spirit. Commanded by Keira—the Northern Naval Academy's top graduate. Like its mistress, Sabie attacks swiftly and precisely, like a sharp saber, eliminating any opponents in its path."

msgid "IDS_PGSC538_FULL"
msgstr "Sabie"

msgid "IDS_PGSC538_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC538_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PGSC539"
msgstr "Danzig"

msgid "IDS_PGSC539_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Hypothetical development of German light cruisers. The ship is based on the Leichter Kreuzer 1938 project and is armed with three triple mounts of semi-automatic universal Gerät 60 guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Designed for escorting and protecting raider groups and aircraft carriers, equipped with a diesel power plant.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC539_FULL"
msgstr "Danzig"

msgid "IDS_PGSC539_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC539_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PGSC549"
msgstr "Nibelung"

msgid "IDS_PGSC549_DESCR"
msgstr "A ship for testing."

msgid "IDS_PGSC549_FULL"
msgstr "Nibelung"

msgid "IDS_PGSC549_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC549_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PGSC588"
msgstr "Prinz Eugen B"

msgid "IDS_PGSC588_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Prinz Eugen, the third cruiser of the Admiral Hipper class, was built to a slightly modified design in Kiel. In May 1941, together with the battleship Bismarck, the ship sailed into the North Sea. The cruiser participated in the Battle of the Denmark Strait, where the British battleship HMS Hood was lost. Prinz Eugen broke away from the British pursuit and managed to reach Brest.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In February 1942, the cruiser, together with the battleships Scharnhorst and Gneisenau, broke through the English Channel, after which it was hit by a torpedo from the British submarine HMS Trident. After repairs, the cruiser was used as a training ship in the Baltic and then returned to service, supporting German troops in the Baltic states.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In May 1945, in Copenhagen, Prinz Eugen surrendered to the British fleet. After World War II, the ship was transferred to the USA and then participated in nuclear tests at Bikini Atoll. In August 1946, the former cruiser was decommissioned, and in December, it sank near Kwajalein Atoll.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC588_FULL"
msgstr "Prinz Eugen B"

msgid "IDS_PGSC588_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PGSC588_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSC598"
msgstr "Mainz B"

msgid "IDS_PGSC598_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>During the design of the Admiral Hipper-class heavy cruisers, German engineers encountered the fact that the proposed project did not fit within the 'Washington' limitations for standard displacement. The armor was not to be inferior to the French cruiser Algérie, which, combined with the required speed and range, led to the total tonnage significantly exceeding the limits. One way to reduce displacement was to reduce the caliber of the main guns: a transition to 190 mm or 170 mm was discussed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The issue of caliber reduction was revisited in 1936 when Germany ordered two cruisers with armament of twelve 150 mm guns in four turrets. However, these cruisers were designed such that their barbettes were identical to the barbettes of cruisers with 203 mm guns, which could easily be installed instead of the 150 mm. Nevertheless, neither of these ships was completed in Germany as a cruiser.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC598_FULL"
msgstr "Mainz B"

msgid "IDS_PGSC598_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSC598_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PGSC706"
msgstr "HSF Graf Spee"

msgid "IDS_PGSC706_DESCR"
msgstr "Deutschland-class training ship of the Wilhelmshaven Marine High School. In the anime, the commander of the ship is Thea Kreutzer, and the vice commander is Wilhelmina. She has 28 cm guns mounted on a hull with a displacement equivalent to that of a training cruiser. Her diesel propulsion gives her an extensive cruising range, and this makes her a valuable asset for operations in far-flung locations. In the High School Fleet anime series, she is often used for long-distance voyages."

msgid "IDS_PGSC706_FULL"
msgstr "HSF Admiral Graf Spee"

msgid "IDS_PGSC706_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PGSC706_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PGSC718"
msgstr "Cross of Dorn"

msgid "IDS_PGSC718_DESCR"
msgstr "This warship is one of the most closely guarded secrets of the Black Templars. Only a few chosen Brothers know of its very existence, as it lays dormant deep in the Reclusiam hangars of the Eternal Crusader, flagship of the Templars’ fleets and Chapter’s Fortress-Monastery. This ship was the gift, reward, and blessing, given by Rogal Dorn to the newborn Chapter and its first High Marshal Sigismund. It is ancient like the time itself, dating back to the early days of the Dark Age of Technology. Looking deceptively small, compared to the gargantuan vessels of Chapter’s fleets, it holds the mysterious and now forbidden powers of that long-forgotten era."

msgid "IDS_PGSC718_FULL"
msgstr "Cross of Dorn"

msgid "IDS_PGSC718_NUMBER"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PGSC718_YEAR"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PGSC809"
msgstr "Roon CLR"

msgid "IDS_PGSC809_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the denunciation of the Versailles Treaty and the signing of the Anglo-German Naval Agreement, the German Naval Staff decided to further develop the Deutschland-type ships. In 1937, several preliminary draft projects designated 'Cruiser P' were considered, and in 1938, the construction of such ships was included in the German fleet modernization program. The raider cruisers were to cut off Great Britain from its sources of supply and raw materials.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>More than twenty variants of preliminary draft projects were considered. The ship's speed remained one of the most difficult issues. To achieve the required characteristics, it was possible to reduce the main gun caliber to the 'Washington' 203 mm. The optimal option would have been the placement of three triple turrets. Of the twelve ordered ships, not one was built due to the start of World War II.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSC809_FULL"
msgstr "Roon CLR"

msgid "IDS_PGSC809_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PGSC809_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PGSC910"
msgstr "[Hindenburg]"

msgid "IDS_PGSC910_DESCR"
msgstr "This ship is a copy of Hindenburg and is suitable for Clan Battles only"

msgid "IDS_PGSC910_FULL"
msgstr "[Hindenburg]"

msgid "IDS_PGSC910_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PGSC910_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSD102"
msgstr "V-25"

msgid "IDS_PGSD102_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A series of six 985-ton destroyers was ordered by China from Germany in 1913. Their predecessors, the V-1 type torpedo boats, showed unsatisfactory seaworthiness, so the new project received increased displacement and enhanced armament. Due to the start of World War I, the destroyers were requisitioned and enlisted in the German fleet.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>V-25 was built at the AG Vulcan Stettin shipyard. With the start of hostilities, the ship was assigned to the Baltic Coastal Defense Division. In October 1914, the ship was part of the 17th Half-Flotilla of the 9th Flotilla of the High Seas Fleet. In February 1915, five destroyers of the 9th Flotilla set out on a combat mission to cover minesweeping off the North Sea coast. V-25 did not return from the sortie. Later, its wreckage was found near Heligoland: presumably, the destroyer hit a British mine.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD102_FULL"
msgstr "V-25"

msgid "IDS_PGSD102_NUMBER"
msgstr "71"

msgid "IDS_PGSD102_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PGSD103"
msgstr "G-101"

msgid "IDS_PGSD103_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1910–12, Argentina ordered the construction of modern destroyers from several foreign firms, including the German shipyards Germaniawerft and Schichau. The start of World War I led to the ships under construction in Germany being requisitioned and assigned to the 2nd Destroyer Flotilla of the German navy.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>SMS G-101 was laid down as ARA Santiago in early 1914 and launched in August of the same year. In 1916, the destroyer participated in the Battle of Jutland. After World War I, the ship was interned at Scapa Flow and then scuttled by its crew on June 21, 1919.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD103_FULL"
msgstr "G-101"

msgid "IDS_PGSD103_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PGSD103_YEAR"
msgstr "1915"

msgid "IDS_PGSD104"
msgstr "V-170"

msgid "IDS_PGSD104_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>To compensate for losses in destroyers during World War I, construction of the 1916 type—'mobilization'—ships began in the autumn of 1916. The next project was the 1917 type, construction of which began in 1918 at the Vulcan Stettin, Schichau, and Howaldtswerke shipyards.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In terms of design, this type was an enlarged variant of the previous one, with one additional main battery gun. Ships of this project were the first German destroyers to receive turbine-gear units as the main engine. No ship was launched due to the end of World War I.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD104_FULL"
msgstr "V-170"

msgid "IDS_PGSD104_NUMBER"
msgstr "17"

msgid "IDS_PGSD104_YEAR"
msgstr "1917"

msgid "IDS_PGSD105"
msgstr "T-22"

msgid "IDS_PGSD105_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the 1920s, several series of torpedo boats were built for the Reichsmarine to replace obsolete World War I ships. The first series were improved versions of World War I torpedo boats, with displacement not exceeding 1,000 tons. The development of these ships was the Type 39, which was radically enhanced compared to its predecessors.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the type, T-22, was built at the Schichau shipyard in Elbing in March 1939. After training completion, the ship was transferred to France, where in 1942–43 it participated in patrol operations in the Bay of Biscay. During a battle in the English Channel in October 1943, T-22 sank the British escort destroyer HMS Limbourne. In December of the same year, the destroyer participated in the Battle of the Bay of Biscay against the British cruisers Glasgow and Enterprise.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In June 1944, T-22 was transferred to the Baltic. On the night of August 17–18, the destroyer hit mines in the Narva Bay and sank.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD105_FULL"
msgstr "T-22"

msgid "IDS_PGSD105_NUMBER"
msgstr "15"

msgid "IDS_PGSD105_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PGSD106"
msgstr "Gaede"

msgid "IDS_PGSD106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In February 1933, the Kriegsmarine command issued a task for designing full-fledged destroyers, planned for laying down in early 1934. According to requirements, they were to surpass ships of the same class in the fleets of other countries in speed and firepower.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In March, Admiral Erich Raeder was presented with a project by the Vulcan Stettin firm, developed in 1932, which was a destroyer with a displacement of 1,500 tons. The project did not meet approval and was revised first to 1,850 tons, and then the displacement exceeded 2,000 tons. It partially repeated the design of the 1916 type destroyer from World War I. However, the Kriegsmarine leadership preferred the project from Deutsche Werke, on the basis of which the Type 1934 was subsequently developed.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD106_FULL"
msgstr "Ernst Gaede"

msgid "IDS_PGSD106_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PGSD106_YEAR"
msgstr "1933"

msgid "IDS_PGSD107"
msgstr "Maass"

msgid "IDS_PGSD107_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In September 1934, a competition was announced for the design of a new destroyer for the German navy. The best was recognized as the Deutsche Werke project, which formed the basis for the Type 1934 destroyers. The displacement significantly exceeded 2,000 tons, although officially stated as 1,625 tons.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead destroyer, Leberecht Maas, was built at the Deutsche Werke shipyard in Kiel. At the start of World War II, the destroyer participated in the blockade of the Polish coast and mine-laying in the North Sea. In February 1940, during Operation Wikinger, Leberecht Maas was mistakenly attacked by Luftwaffe bombers and sank.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD107_FULL"
msgstr "Leberecht Maass"

msgid "IDS_PGSD107_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PGSD107_YEAR"
msgstr "1937"

msgid "IDS_PGSD108"
msgstr "Z-23"

msgid "IDS_PGSD108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Type 1936 destroyers differed from their predecessors in having a strengthened structure and a more reliable power plant. Following the Type 1936 came eight destroyers of the 1936A project, which were laid down in 1938–40.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Z-23, the lead ship of the Type 1936A, was built at the Deschimag shipyard in Bremen. At the start of World War II, the destroyer conducted convoys in Norwegian waters and then in the Bay of Biscay. In June 1941, Z-23 returned to Norway, where it hunted Arctic convoys and laid minefields.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In early 1943, the ship was again transferred to France. In December, during the Battle of the Bay of Biscay, the destroyer was heavily damaged by British bombers. The German command decided not to repair the ship, and Z-23 was struck from the fleet list. After the war, the French navy used the destroyer as a source of spare parts for captured ships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD108_FULL"
msgstr "Z-23"

msgid "IDS_PGSD108_NUMBER"
msgstr "15"

msgid "IDS_PGSD108_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSD109"
msgstr "Z-46"

msgid "IDS_PGSD109_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Type 1936C repeated the hull of the Type 1936A destroyer but used more powerful turbines from scout cruisers. The main armament was also changed: it now consisted of six 128 mm universal guns in three turret mounts.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>A total of six ships of this type were ordered, but none were launched. Z-46 was laid down in 1943 at the Deschimag shipyard, but due to a lack of materials and Allied air raids, construction proceeded extremely slowly. In 1945, both hulls were blown up on the slipways.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD109_FULL"
msgstr "Z-46"

msgid "IDS_PGSD109_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PGSD109_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PGSD110"
msgstr "Z-52"

msgid "IDS_PGSD110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the early 1940s, as part of the plan to modernize the German navy, options for building destroyers with diesel propulsion were considered. The first was the Type 1942, the sole representative of which was laid down in 1943. The development of this project was the Type 1944—also with diesel engines but with increased firepower.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Six Type 1944 destroyers, with names from Z-52 to Z-56, were laid down in 1943 at the Deschimag shipyard in Bremen, but Allied bombings, a shortage of construction materials, and the need to clear slipways for submarines led to the construction of these destroyers being canceled in July 1944.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD110_FULL"
msgstr "Z-52"

msgid "IDS_PGSD110_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PGSD110_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PGSD111"
msgstr "Otto Weddigen"

msgid "IDS_PGSD111_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1945, based on the design of the Type 1936D destroyers, the Type 1945 was designed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Its design differed from previous projects, primarily by returning to the use of steam turbines instead of diesels. Such a ship would have been significantly more heavily armed: eight 128 mm universal guns in four turret mounts. Despite the heavier armament, turbine-gear units would have provided higher power plant output compared to diesel engines.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Due to shipyards being occupied with submarine repair and construction, and also due to the end of World War II, the project was not realized.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD111_FULL"
msgstr "Otto Weddigen"

msgid "IDS_PGSD111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSD111_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSD207"
msgstr "Z-31"

msgid "IDS_PGSD207_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In June-July 1939, the construction of twelve Type 1936B destroyers was ordered as part of the German fleet modernization program. With the start of World War II, ten ships of this type were first canceled, and in 1940–41, seven of them were re-ordered as Type 1936A (Mob) destroyers. The destroyers received 150 mm gun mounts that were previously intended for battlecruiser-raiders.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the type, Z-31, was built at the Deschimag shipyard in Bremen. After crew training, the ship was transferred to Norway. In December 1942, the destroyer participated in the Battle of the Barents Sea during the hunt for convoy JW-51B. In September 1943, the ship participated in the raid on Spitsbergen; in October 1944—in the transport of German troops from Finland to Norway. In January 1945, the destroyer sustained heavy damage in a battle with British cruisers. After the war, Z-31 was transferred to France as reparations and renamed Marceau.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD207_FULL"
msgstr "Z-31"

msgid "IDS_PGSD207_NUMBER"
msgstr "15"

msgid "IDS_PGSD207_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PGSD208"
msgstr "G. J. Maerker"

msgid "IDS_PGSD208_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Type 1937 destroyer was developed as a ship with an ocean range. It was planned that these ships would be able to protect or attack convoys in the Atlantic and operate in tropical climates. In terms of combat power, they were to be not inferior to modern French destroyers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The 1937J variant was chosen as the basis for the project. However, in April 1938, Admiral Erich Raeder ordered the design to be abandoned, as it was not possible to combine the necessary combat power, range, speed, and reliability in one hull.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD208_FULL"
msgstr "Gustav-Julius Maerker"

msgid "IDS_PGSD208_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSD208_YEAR"
msgstr "1937"

msgid "IDS_PGSD209"
msgstr "F. Schultz"

msgid "IDS_PGSD209_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The development of the ocean destroyer concept led to the appearance of the 1938 project. The final variant in its size and armament was essentially a light cruiser, with a displacement approaching 5,000 tons. It was armed with six 150 mm guns in three turret mounts.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Under the German fleet modernization program, 24 ships of this type were planned, but none were laid down. Further development led to the appearance of the scout cruiser project and to the development of the Type 1938B—a destroyer for operations in the Baltic and coastal seas but with an increased range. Twelve such ships were ordered in 1939, but the order was canceled due to the start of World War II.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD209_FULL"
msgstr "Felix Schultz"

msgid "IDS_PGSD209_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSD209_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PGSD210"
msgstr "Elbing"

msgid "IDS_PGSD210_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Type 1938 served as the basis for the scout cruiser project. The ship increased in size and received stronger armament, allowing it to compare with German interwar cruisers. It was assumed that the scout cruiser would be able to escape from a superior ship and sink a weaker opponent.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In February 1941, three cruisers of this type were ordered, and in August, the lead ship was laid down, but in March 1942, the construction program was suspended.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD210_FULL"
msgstr "Elbing"

msgid "IDS_PGSD210_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSD210_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSD506"
msgstr "T-61"

msgid "IDS_PGSD506_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the capture of the Netherlands, the shipbuilding capacities of that country fell to Germany. At the Wilton-Fijenoord, Koninklijke Maatschappij De Schelde, and Rotterdamsche Droogdok Maatschappij shipyards, a series of twelve fleet torpedo boats was ordered, designed taking into account Dutch power plants.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ships laid down in 1942 were built slowly due to the Dutch reluctance to work for the occupiers, and by the time of the Allied landing in Normandy, not a single destroyer of the series had been launched. The three ships closest to completion were urgently launched and towed to Germany, but one of them was sunk by British bombers, and the others were not completed due to a lack of resources.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD506_FULL"
msgstr "T-61"

msgid "IDS_PGSD506_NUMBER"
msgstr "8"

msgid "IDS_PGSD506_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSD508"
msgstr "Z-39"

msgid "IDS_PGSD508_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Z-39, a Type 1936A destroyer, was built at the Germaniawerft shipyard in Kiel. The ship entered service in August 1943 but was ready for combat only in January 1944 due to errors during construction. In March 1944, the destroyer was transferred to Tallinn as part of the 6th Destroyer Flotilla, where it participated in mine-laying and shelling Soviet troops. In June, the ship was damaged by Soviet aviation and was under repair until February 1945. After returning to service, the destroyer escorted convoys evacuating troops and civilians from East Prussia and Courland.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After World War II, Z-39 was transferred to the US Navy, where the ship received the name DD-939 and was used for research on the power plant with high-pressure boilers. In 1948, the ship passed to France, where it was renamed Q-128. The former German destroyer was used as a donor for spare parts and as a pontoon for minesweepers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD508_FULL"
msgstr "Z-39"

msgid "IDS_PGSD508_NUMBER"
msgstr "15"

msgid "IDS_PGSD508_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PGSD516"
msgstr "K. Schönberg"

msgid "IDS_PGSD516_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1933–34, the SK22 project was proposed, based on the Type 1916 ships from World War I.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In one variant of the project, the ship's armament was represented by four 150 mm guns. The operational experience of such guns remained controversial: the ship was too unstable a platform for artillery fire, even if its displacement exceeded 2,000 tons. Moreover, handling heavy six-inch shells significantly exhausted the crew. Largely for this reason, the German naval leadership preferred a project with smaller caliber artillery.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD516_FULL"
msgstr "Karl von Schönberg"

msgid "IDS_PGSD516_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSD516_YEAR"
msgstr "1933"

msgid "IDS_PGSD518"
msgstr "Z-35"

msgid "IDS_PGSD518_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the summer of 1939, twelve new type destroyers—1936B—were ordered. The ships were armed with four 128 mm guns and were to become part of the German fleet modernization program. However, with the start of World War II, ten ships of this type were canceled, and then seven of them were re-laid as Type 1936A (Mob) destroyers. In 1941, a ship was laid down according to the original 1936B project, with the number of main battery guns increased to five during construction.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Z-35, the lead ship of the series, was built at the Deschimag shipyard. In February 1944, the ship was transferred to Tallinn, where it participated in mine-laying and raid operations. In December 1944, during another mine-laying operation in the Gulf of Finland, Z-35 hit mines and sank.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD518_FULL"
msgstr "Z-35"

msgid "IDS_PGSD518_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PGSD518_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PGSD519"
msgstr "Z-44"

msgid "IDS_PGSD519_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Z-44, a Type 1936B destroyer, was built at the Deschimag shipyard in Bremen. The ship's construction proceeded slowly, as the main efforts of German shipbuilders were directed towards the construction and repair of submarines.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In July 1944, work was suspended, and five days later, Z-44 was sunk at the fitting-out pier as a result of a British bomber raid on Bremen. The destroyer was raised and partially dismantled for spare parts to repair Z-39. The completion of the ship was canceled in September 1944 when the destroyer's stern broke off.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD519_FULL"
msgstr "Z-44"

msgid "IDS_PGSD519_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PGSD519_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PGSD529"
msgstr "ZF-6"

msgid "IDS_PGSD529_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1940, Germany gained access to a large number of unfinished French ships in the occupied territory. Among them were four Le Hardi-class destroyers. Two of them had been launched, and two more were in the initial stage of construction.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The German command planned to complete such ships for its own needs, installing German armament, and the changes made could have been very significant. However, by the end of the war, the Germans had not even managed to launch the destroyers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD529_FULL"
msgstr "ZF-6"

msgid "IDS_PGSD529_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSD529_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PGSD538"
msgstr "Z-32"

msgid "IDS_PGSD538_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Type 1938B destroyers, intended for operations in the Baltic, were ordered for construction in the summer of 1939; however, with the start of World War II, some ships were re-ordered as Type 1936A (Mob).</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Z-32 was built at the Deschimag shipyard in Bremen. The ship joined the 8th Destroyer Flotilla, which in March 1943 was transferred to Bordeaux via the English Channel during Operation Karin. Z-32 participated in covering Italian and German blockade runners. In January 1944, the ship collided with the destroyer Z-37 and was under repair until summer.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the Allied landing in Normandy in June 1944, Z-32 took part in the Battle of Ushant. During the battle, the destroyer sustained serious damage and ran aground off the island of Île de Batz. The crew blew up the irreparable ship.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD538_FULL"
msgstr "Z-32"

msgid "IDS_PGSD538_NUMBER"
msgstr "7"

msgid "IDS_PGSD538_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PGSD548"
msgstr "Z-33"

msgid "IDS_PGSD548_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Originally ordered as part of the Type 1938B, this ship was re-ordered as a Type 1936A (Mob) destroyer. Z-33 was built at the Deschimag shipyard in Bremen. After crew training, the ship was sent to Norway, where in September 1943 it participated in the raid on Spitsbergen.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>As part of the battleship Scharnhorst's escort, the destroyer participated in attacks on Allied Arctic convoys, and from October 1944, it itself covered convoys during the evacuation of Germans from northern Norway. In January 1946, the ship was transferred to the Soviet Union as reparations and received the name Provorny.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD548_FULL"
msgstr "Z-33"

msgid "IDS_PGSD548_NUMBER"
msgstr "7"

msgid "IDS_PGSD548_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PGSD558"
msgstr "Z-38"

msgid "IDS_PGSD558_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A Type 1936A (Mob) destroyer, built at the Germaniawerft shipyard in Kiel. After crew training, Z-38 was sent to Norway. In 1944, the destroyer participated in patrol missions, covering the retreat of German troops from the Arctic.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1945, the ship was transferred to the Baltic, where it participated in combat operations in East Prussia, shelling Soviet positions along the coast and escorting military transports. In July 1945, the destroyer was transferred to the British Navy, where it received the name HMS Nonsuch.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD558_FULL"
msgstr "Z-38"

msgid "IDS_PGSD558_NUMBER"
msgstr "7"

msgid "IDS_PGSD558_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PGSD610"
msgstr "Z-42"

msgid "IDS_PGSD610_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A destroyer ordered in July 1939 as a Type 1936B ship, and in October re-ordered under the Type 1936A (Mob). In June 1940, due to a shortage of materials, construction was canceled, but in December, it was resumed. In February 1941, the ship's construction was finally canceled.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>If this ship had been completed as a fleet AA destroyer, twin 105 mm universal gun mounts could have been used as the main caliber. Such mounts were planned for the ship under the Flottentorpedoboot 1944 project.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSD610_FULL"
msgstr "Z-42"

msgid "IDS_PGSD610_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSD610_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PGSD990"
msgstr "Elbing Test"

msgid "IDS_PGSD990_DESCR"
msgstr "Ship for test."

msgid "IDS_PGSD990_FULL"
msgstr "Elbing Test"

msgid "IDS_PGSD990_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSD990_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSS106"
msgstr "U-69 (old)"

msgid "IDS_PGSS106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Type VII submarine project was based on German UB-III and UG submarines, as well as the Finnish Vetehinen type and the Spanish Type E. The latter three types were developed by the German-Dutch firm NV Ingenieurskantoor voor Scheepsbouw to circumvent Versailles restrictions, which prohibited Germany from developing submarines.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Type VII became the most mass-produced type of submarine in history: a total of 703 boats were built in various modifications. The most numerous of these was the VIIC series, built in 1940–45.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead boat of the series, U-69, was built in Kiel. In 1939–43, the boat conducted ten war patrols and participated in seven 'wolf packs'. In February 1943, during the hunt for convoy ONS-165, the submarine was sunk by the British destroyer HMS Fame in the middle of the North Atlantic.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSS106_FULL"
msgstr "U-69 (< 04.10.2022)"

msgid "IDS_PGSS106_NUMBER"
msgstr "568"

msgid "IDS_PGSS106_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSS108"
msgstr "U-190 (old)"

msgid "IDS_PGSS108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The project for ocean-going Type IX submarines appeared as a development of the Type I, built in 1936. The Type IX was developed in 1935–36 for long-range raids, particularly to the Caribbean Sea and the US coast. Submarines of this type proved to be powerful and reliable, with the largest series being the IXC/40.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead submarine of the series, U-190, was built in Bremen. The boat made six war patrols in 1942–45, sinking two Allied ships. In May 1945, the boat surrendered to a Canadian frigate and was officially enlisted in the Canadian navy as a prize. In July 1947, U-190 was scuttled during exercises.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSS108_FULL"
msgstr "U-190 (< 04.10.2022)"

msgid "IDS_PGSS108_NUMBER"
msgstr "90"

msgid "IDS_PGSS108_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PGSS110"
msgstr "U-2501 (old)"

msgid "IDS_PGSS110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The main feature of World War II submarines was that they spent most of their time on the surface. The first truly 'submarine' was the German Type XXI project. These submarines were specifically designed to spend most of their time submerged. They were equipped with improved battery capacity and a snorkel—a device for underwater diesel operation. The boats were also equipped with quiet electric motors for stealthy approach to targets.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>U-2501, the lead submarine of the series, was built in Hamburg. During its short service, the boat was in Kiel and Danzig. U-2501 did not participate in war patrols and was scuttled in May 1945 in Hamburg.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSS110_FULL"
msgstr "U-2501 (< 04.10.2022)"

msgid "IDS_PGSS110_NUMBER"
msgstr "267"

msgid "IDS_PGSS110_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PGSS111"
msgstr "U-796"

msgid "IDS_PGSS111_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Project for a large ocean-going boat with a Walter engine. The ship was to have a streamlined hull, with hydrogen peroxide tanks for the Walter turbine located inside the pressure hull.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In December 1943, two submarines, U-796 and U-797, were laid down in Kiel, but in March 1944, their construction was canceled due to fuel shortages. As a result of replacing fuel tanks with capacious batteries and the Walter turbines with electric motors, the Type XXI submarine project was developed.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSS111_FULL"
msgstr "U-796"

msgid "IDS_PGSS111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PGSS111_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PGSS206"
msgstr "U-69"

msgid "IDS_PGSS206_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Type VII submarine project was based on German UB-III and UG submarines, as well as the Finnish Vetehinen type and the Spanish Type E. The latter three types were developed by the German-Dutch firm NV Ingenieurskantoor voor Scheepsbouw to circumvent Versailles restrictions, which prohibited Germany from developing submarines.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Type VII became the most mass-produced type of submarine in history: a total of 703 boats were built in various modifications. The most numerous of these was the VIIC series, built in 1940–45.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead boat of the series, U-69, was built in Kiel. In 1939–43, the boat conducted ten war patrols and participated in seven 'wolf packs'. In February 1943, during the hunt for convoy ONS-165, the submarine was sunk by the British destroyer HMS Fame in the middle of the North Atlantic.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSS206_FULL"
msgstr "U-69"

msgid "IDS_PGSS206_NUMBER"
msgstr "568"

msgid "IDS_PGSS206_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PGSS208"
msgstr "U-190"

msgid "IDS_PGSS208_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The project for ocean-going Type IX submarines appeared as a development of the Type I, built in 1936. The Type IX was developed in 1935–36 for long-range raids, particularly to the Caribbean Sea and the US coast. Submarines of this type proved to be powerful and reliable, with the largest series being the IXC/40.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead submarine of the series, U-190, was built in Bremen. The boat made six war patrols in 1942–45, sinking two Allied ships. In May 1945, the boat surrendered to a Canadian frigate and was officially enlisted in the Canadian navy as a prize. In July 1947, U-190 was scuttled during exercises.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSS208_FULL"
msgstr "U-190"

msgid "IDS_PGSS208_NUMBER"
msgstr "90"

msgid "IDS_PGSS208_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PGSS210"
msgstr "U-2501"

msgid "IDS_PGSS210_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The main feature of World War II submarines was that they spent most of their time on the surface. The first truly 'submarine' was the German Type XXI project. These submarines were specifically designed to spend most of their time submerged. They were equipped with improved battery capacity and a snorkel—a device for underwater diesel operation. The boats were also equipped with quiet electric motors for stealthy approach to targets.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>U-2501, the lead submarine of the series, was built in Hamburg. During its short service, the boat was in Kiel and Danzig. U-2501 did not participate in war patrols and was scuttled in May 1945 in Hamburg.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSS210_FULL"
msgstr "U-2501"

msgid "IDS_PGSS210_NUMBER"
msgstr "267"

msgid "IDS_PGSS210_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PGSS510"
msgstr "U-4501"

msgid "IDS_PGSS510_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In October 1943, Walter-Werke proposed to the German navy a project for a submarine with a Walter closed-cycle engine running on hydrogen peroxide. Such an engine worked equally well both surfaced and submerged.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In May 1944, the Kriegsmarine Main Committee for Shipbuilding presented a production plan providing for the construction of submarines, which were assigned the index XXVIW. Individual modules for the hulls were to be manufactured at different shipyards. By the end of the war, sections for four submarines had been produced: from U-4501 to U-4504.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PGSS510_FULL"
msgstr "U-4501"

msgid "IDS_PGSS510_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PGSS510_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PGSS910"
msgstr "U-2601"

msgid "IDS_PGSS910_DESCR"
msgstr "Submarines specifically designed for extended underwater operations. They were equipped with improved batteries and a snorkel for submerged diesel propulsion. These submarines also featured low-noise electric motors for stealthy approaches to targets."

msgid "IDS_PGSS910_FULL"
msgstr "U-2601"

msgid "IDS_PGSS910_NUMBER"
msgstr "267"

msgid "IDS_PGSS910_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

