msgid "IDS_PJSA104"
msgstr "Hōshō"

msgid "IDS_PJSA104_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Japan began its first experiments with aircraft carriers featuring full-length flight decks immediately after World War I. In December 1919, a small naval tanker was laid down, and the decision was made the following year to convert it into an aircraft carrier. British specialists participated in the design process.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The first Japanese aircraft carrier was built in Yokohama. Initially, the ship was used primarily for testing the capabilities of carrier-based aircraft and refining design solutions for application on future carriers. In the 1930s, the carrier participated in the Shanghai Incident and the Second Sino-Japanese War.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1941, the ship joined the Combined Fleet, conducting reconnaissance and anti-submarine patrols in support of the fleet's main forces. In 1945, the ship was damaged by American bombers in Kure Harbor. After the war ended, Hōshō served as a transport vessel for troops returning to Japan.</p>"

