msgstr "Ryūjō"

msgid "IDS_PJSA106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Washington Naval Treaty did not impose restrictions on the number of ships with a displacement of up to 10,000 tons. Therefore, Japan, in addition to large aircraft carriers, began developing small aircraft-carrying ships as well.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>According to the initial design, the hangar was single-level, but during construction, it was transformed into a two-level one. This allowed for an increase in the size of the air group but led to severe overloading and a reduction in the ship's stability. As a result, the new aircraft carrier had to undergo modernization: bulges were added, and part of its armament was removed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The aircraft carrier Ryūjō was built in Yokohama. From 1937 to 1939, the ship participated in the Second Sino-Japanese War. During World War II, the carrier supported the landings in the Philippines, covered convoys heading to the Dutch East Indies, participated in the raid into the Indian Ocean and to the Aleutian Islands. During the Battle of the Eastern Solomons on August 24, 1942, the Ryūjō was sunk by carrier-based aircraft from the American aircraft carrier USS Saratoga.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSA106_FULL"
msgstr "Ryūjō"

msgid "IDS_PJSA106_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSA106_YEAR"
msgstr "1933"

msgid "IDS_PJSA108"
msgstr "Shōkaku"

msgid "IDS_PJSA108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Shōkaku-class aircraft carriers in terms of architecture and construction almost exactly repeated the Hiryū type, however they had improved seakeeping qualities and protection, enhanced anti-aircraft armament, and an increased air group. At the time of their construction, these ships were considered the largest and most powerful purpose-built aircraft carriers in the world.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Shōkaku was built in Yokosuka. The aircraft carrier participated in the attack on Pearl Harbor, bombing American airbases. In 1942, the ship took part in the invasion of Rabaul and the raid into the Indian Ocean, where it sank the British aircraft carrier Hermes. The carrier fought in the battles of the Coral Sea and off the Eastern Solomon Islands.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the Battle of the Santa Cruz Islands, Shōkaku sustained heavy damage from aircraft of the American carrier Hornet, but also inflicted serious damage on the enemy. During the Battle of the Philippine Sea, on June 19, 1944, the carrier was torpedoed by the submarine USS Cavalla, which caused aviation fuel vapors to ignite and led to the ship's loss.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSA108_FULL"
msgstr "Shōkaku"

msgid "IDS_PJSA108_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSA108_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PJSA110"
msgstr "Hakuryū"

msgid "IDS_PJSA110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The G-15 project was an improved version of the Taihō-class aircraft carrier. The new ship was intended to differ from its predecessor with larger dimensions, a more advanced armor system, and an increased air group.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>A total of seven such ships were planned, with the completion of the entire series scheduled for 1948. After the Battle of Midway, the laying of the ships was halted to reallocate resources in favor of the simplified design Unryū-class carriers. In 1943, construction was definitively canceled.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSA110_FULL"
msgstr "Hakuryū"

msgid "IDS_PJSA110_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PJSA110_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PJSA111"
msgstr "Sekiryū"

msgid "IDS_PJSA111_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Hypothetical development of the Taihō and Taihō Kai aircraft carrier line.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The project envisions expanded hangars, a reinforced power plant with economical cruising diesels. The artillery is represented by universal 100 mm guns, similar to those used on Akizuki-class destroyers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSA111_FULL"
msgstr "Sekiryū"

msgid "IDS_PJSA111_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PJSA111_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PJSA206"
msgstr "Zuihō"

msgid "IDS_PJSA206_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Imperial Japanese Navy considered plans to rapidly increase the number of aircraft carriers by converting existing vessels. For instance, the submarine tender Takasaki, laid down in Yokosuka in 1935, was completed as an aircraft carrier.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In December 1940, the carrier was commissioned as the Zuihō. In September 1941, the ship became the flagship of the 3rd Carrier Division, leading it to cover the Combined Fleet's attack on Pearl Harbor.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After the division was disbanded, the carrier joined the 1st Fleet. The ship participated in the Battle of Midway, the Guadalcanal Campaign, and the raid on Rabaul. The carrier also transported aircraft to Truk and Guam. In June 1944, the ship took part in the Battle of the Philippine Sea, and in October of the same year—in the Battle of Leyte Gulf. In the Battle off Cape Engaño on October 25, 1944, Zuihō was attacked by American aircraft, sustained several torpedo and bomb hits, and subsequently sank.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSA206_FULL"
msgstr "Zuihō"

msgid "IDS_PJSA206_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSA206_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PJSA208"
msgstr "Hiryū"

msgid "IDS_PJSA208_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Following the London Naval Conference, Japan gained the opportunity to build two additional aircraft carriers. In terms of architecture, these ships became the prototypes for nearly all subsequent large Japanese aircraft carriers. They featured a continuous flight deck, a two-level hangar, a small island superstructure, three aircraft elevators, and downward-angled funnels. The design combined high speed with powerful anti-aircraft armament and a strong air group. The hull form and power plant were based on the design of heavy cruisers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The aircraft carrier Hiryū was built in Yokosuka. In 1940, the ship supported the invasion of Indochina, and in 1941, it participated in the attack on Pearl Harbor. In the spring of 1942, the carrier took part in the Indian Ocean raid. During the Battle of Midway Atoll, on June 4, 1942, Hiryū was attacked by dive bombers from American aircraft carriers, sustained several bomb hits, and caught fire. The crew abandoned the ship during the night, and it sank the following day.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSA208_FULL"
msgstr "Hiryū"

msgid "IDS_PJSA208_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSA208_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PJSA210"
msgstr "Taihō"

msgid "IDS_PJSA210_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>An aircraft carrier built to an improved design based on the Shōkaku class. The ship featured an armored flight deck that was integrated into the hull and served as part of the ship's structural strength.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Taihō was built at the Kawasaki shipyard in Kobe. In June 1944, the ship participated in the Battle of the Philippine Sea, where it was struck by a torpedo from the submarine USS Albacore. The crew was unable to contain the spreading aviation fuel and gasoline vapors throughout the ship, leading to two massive explosions that caused the aircraft carrier's sinking.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSA210_FULL"
msgstr "Taihō"

msgid "IDS_PJSA210_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSA210_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PJSA507"
msgstr "Kaga (old)"

msgid "IDS_PJSA507_DESCR"
msgstr "This ship was available before the release of Update 0.8.0."

msgid "IDS_PJSA507_FULL"
msgstr "Kaga (< 23.01.2019)"

msgid "IDS_PJSA507_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSA507_YEAR"
msgstr "1928"

msgid "IDS_PJSA508"
msgstr "Akagi"

msgid "IDS_PJSA508_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Akagi was laid down as an Amagi-class battlecruiser at the Kure Naval Arsenal. After the Washington Naval Conference, construction of ships of this class was halted, and it was later decided to convert them into aircraft carriers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship received two flight decks, with aircraft on the lower deck able to take off directly from the hangar. Its 200 mm guns — the maximum caliber permitted by the Washington Naval Treaty — were mounted both in a stern casemate and in two twin turrets located between the lower and upper flight decks in the bow. At the time of its commissioning, Akagi was the largest aircraft carrier in the world.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>From aboard Akagi, Admiral Chūichi Nagumo commanded the attack on Pearl Harbor — aircraft from the carrier sank the battleships Oklahoma and West Virginia. During the Battle of Midway, Akagi sustained several bomb hits from American aircraft, which ignited spilled aviation fuel and detonated aerial torpedoes. The damage was too severe, and on the night of June 4–5, 1942, the abandoned Akagi was scuttled by Japanese destroyers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSA508_FULL"
msgstr "Akagi"

msgid "IDS_PJSA508_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSA508_YEAR"
msgstr "1927"

msgid "IDS_PJSA510"
msgstr "Shinano"

msgid "IDS_PJSA510_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The third battleship of the Yamato class was laid down in May 1940 in Yokosuka, but construction was halted in December 1941. After the Battle of Midway, it was decided to complete the ship as an aircraft carrier. The half-finished hull of the battleship could not accommodate a sufficiently large hangar, limiting the number of aircraft. The torpedo defense was identical to that of the Yamato-class battleships, and the armor of the ammunition magazines, converted into aviation armament storage, remained unchanged, but the rest of the vertical armor was significantly reduced due to the need to reduce weight.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Shinano was launched as an aircraft carrier in October 1944, and in November, the ship was commissioned, though it was not fully equipped. While en route for final outfitting from Yokosuka to Kure, the ship was attacked by the American submarine USS Archerfish on November 28. The carrier was struck by four torpedoes, capsized, and sank.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSA510_FULL"
msgstr "Shinano"

msgid "IDS_PJSA510_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSA510_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PJSA518"
msgstr "Kaga"

msgid "IDS_PJSA518_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Kaga was laid down at the Kawasaki shipyard in Kobe in 1920 as a Tosa-class battleship and launched in 1921. In 1922, construction was canceled due to the limitations of the Washington Naval Treaty.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1923, it was decided to complete the ship as an aircraft carrier, and it was towed to Yokosuka. The carrier's design mirrored that of the Akagi, except for the placement of the smokestacks and a shorter flight deck.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The carrier participated in the Shanghai Incident and the Second Sino-Japanese War. In 1934–35, the ship underwent modernization: its power plant was replaced, the main flight deck was extended in place of the additional lower decks, and anti-torpedo bulges were installed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship took part in the attack on Pearl Harbor, sinking several American vessels. In 1942, it participated in the invasion of Rabaul, the campaign in the Marshall Islands, and the air raid on Darwin. During the Battle of Midway, on June 4, 1942, the Kaga was sunk by dive bombers from the aircraft carrier Enterprise.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSA518_FULL"
msgstr "Kaga"

msgid "IDS_PJSA518_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSA518_YEAR"
msgstr "1928"

msgid "IDS_PJSA598"
msgstr "Kaga B"

msgid "IDS_PJSA598_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Kaga was laid down at the Kawasaki shipyard in Kobe in 1920 as a Tosa-class battleship and launched in 1921. In 1922, construction was canceled due to the limitations of the Washington Naval Treaty.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1923, it was decided to complete the ship as an aircraft carrier, and it was towed to Yokosuka. The carrier's design mirrored that of the Akagi, except for the placement of the smokestacks and a shorter flight deck.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The carrier participated in the Shanghai Incident and the Second Sino-Japanese War. In 1934–35, the ship underwent modernization: its power plant was replaced, the main flight deck was extended in place of the additional lower decks, and anti-torpedo bulges were installed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship took part in the attack on Pearl Harbor, sinking several American vessels. In 1942, it participated in the invasion of Rabaul, the campaign in the Marshall Islands, and the air raid on Darwin. During the Battle of Midway, on June 4, 1942, the Kaga was sunk by dive bombers from the aircraft carrier Enterprise.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSA598_FULL"
msgstr "Kaga B"

msgid "IDS_PJSA598_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSA598_YEAR"
msgstr "1928"

msgid "IDS_PJSA908"
msgstr "Kikaku"

msgid "IDS_PJSA908_DESCR"
msgstr "A fast-moving aircraft carrier with good anti-torpedo protection for its size. A portion of the ship's air group is outfitted with smoke generators, enabling them to swiftly create smoke screens."

msgid "IDS_PJSA908_FULL"
msgstr "Kikaku"

msgid "IDS_PJSA908_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PJSA908_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PJSA910"
msgstr "Taihō"

msgid "IDS_PJSA910_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>An aircraft carrier built to an improved design based on the Shōkaku class. The ship featured an armored flight deck that was integrated into the hull and served as part of the ship's structural strength.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Taihō was built at the Kawasaki shipyard in Kobe. In June 1944, the ship participated in the Battle of the Philippine Sea, where it was struck by a torpedo from the submarine USS Albacore. The crew was unable to contain the spreading aviation fuel and gasoline vapors throughout the ship, leading to two massive explosions that caused the aircraft carrier's sinking.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSA910_FULL"
msgstr "Taihō"

msgid "IDS_PJSA910_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSA910_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PJSA917"
msgstr "[Hakuryū]"

msgid "IDS_PJSA917_DESCR"
msgstr "This ship is a copy of Hakuryū, intended for test purposes only."

msgid "IDS_PJSA917_FULL"
msgstr "[Hakuryū]"

msgid "IDS_PJSA917_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PJSA917_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PJSB001"
msgstr "Kawachi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB001_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Even during the Russo-Japanese War, Japan began constructing ships armed only with large-caliber guns. The first dreadnoughts were intended to be the Satsuma-class battleships, but due to a shortage of 305 mm guns, two 254 mm barrels were installed in the broadside turrets of these ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The further development of the Satsuma class were the two Kawachi-class ships, equipped with six main battery turrets in a rhomboid arrangement.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Kawachi, the lead ship of the class, was built in Yokosuka. During World War I, the ship participated in the Siege of Qingdao. Kawachi was lost due to an explosion in the forward turret's magazine in Tokuyama Bay on July 12, 1918.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB001_FULL"
msgstr "Kawachi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB001_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSB001_YEAR"
msgstr "1912"

msgid "IDS_PJSB003"
msgstr "Myōgi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB003_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Even before the Russo-Japanese War, Japan began developing cruisers with a main battery caliber characteristic of battleships. In 1905, two Tsukuba-class cruisers were laid down, each carrying four 305-mm guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The construction of significantly more powerful and faster battlecruisers in Great Britain led to the development of similar vessels in Japan. Naval architect Yuzuru Hiraga prepared three preliminary design studies for a battlecruiser: B-39, B-40, and B-41.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Design B-40 proposed an armament of 356-mm guns in three twin turrets, two of which were to be located aft. Additionally, sixteen 152-mm guns were planned for installation in casemates. The project was not realized, as it was decided to order a battlecruiser from Great Britain.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB003_FULL"
msgstr "Myōgi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB003_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PJSB003_YEAR"
msgstr "1910"

msgid "IDS_PJSB006"
msgstr "Fusō"

msgid "IDS_PJSB006_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The battleship project A-64 was designed by shipbuilder Yuzuru Hiraga based on the battlecruiser Kongō. To surpass the New York-class dreadnoughts being built in the United States, the number of 356 mm guns was increased to twelve in six turrets. Instead of triple turrets, twin turrets were chosen to improve survivability. The project also resulted in a faster ship than the American dreadnought, reflecting Japan's aim to compensate for its smaller number of ships with superior performance.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Fusō was built in Kure. It saw virtually no action in World War I. Between 1930–35 and 1937–41, it underwent extensive modernization, which improved its armor and increased its power and speed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, the ship covered the raid on Pearl Harbor, participated in the diversionary Aleutian Islands operation during the Battle of Midway, and fought in the Battle of Surigao Strait. In the early morning of October 25, 1944, the battleship was struck by torpedoes from American destroyers and sank, breaking into several pieces.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB006_FULL"
msgstr "Fusō"

msgid "IDS_PJSB006_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSB006_YEAR"
msgstr "1915"

msgid "IDS_PJSB007"
msgstr "Kongō"

msgid "IDS_PJSB007_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>When the British battlecruiser Lion was laid down, Japan began developing its own ship of this class. However, due to a lack of experience in building large ships, it was decided to turn to Great Britain. In October 1910, a contract was signed with the firm Vickers for the design and construction of a new battlecruiser based on the Lion design, with Japanese engineers also participating in the development.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Kongō was built in Barrow-in-Furness. During the First World War, the ship supported Japanese troops during the Siege of Qingdao and later participated in patrol operations. In the interwar period, the ship underwent extensive modernization.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the Second World War, the ship covered landings in Malaya and the Philippines, and participated in the battles of Midway, the Santa Cruz Islands, Guadalcanal, the Philippine Sea, and Leyte Gulf. Kongō suffered two torpedo hits from the submarine USS Sealion on November 21, 1944. A few hours later, the battlecruiser sank.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB007_FULL"
msgstr "Kongō"

msgid "IDS_PJSB007_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSB007_YEAR"
msgstr "1913"

msgid "IDS_PJSB008"
msgstr "Ishizuchi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB008_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The initial design by Vickers, commissioned by Japan, had the designation 472 and featured armament consisting of five twin turrets with 305 mm guns. However, the customer was dissatisfied with the test results of the British 305 mm guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Imperial Japanese Navy General Staff funded the development of guns of a different caliber by Vickers, and further refinement of the design led to the emergence of the Kongō-class ships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB008_FULL"
msgstr "Ishizuchi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB008_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB008_YEAR"
msgstr "1909"

msgid "IDS_PJSB010"
msgstr "Nagato"

msgid "IDS_PJSB010_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Nagato-class battleships were the first Japanese battleships not only built but also entirely designed in Japan. The 410 mm caliber guns were developed in response to British 381 mm armament. Their speed was increased compared to their predecessors, a fact which Japan concealed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Nagato was built in Kure. Between 1933 and 1936, the battleship underwent a major modernization that included enhanced armor. It participated in World War II, serving as the flagship of Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto during the attack on Pearl Harbor. In 1942, it took part in the Battle of Midway and the Solomon Islands campaign. During the Battle of Leyte Gulf, the battleship sustained damage and, after the war, was surrendered to the United States as a war reparation.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1946, the battleship became a target for nuclear weapon tests at Bikini Atoll as part of Operation Crossroads. Nagato, which had been damaged late in World War II, successfully withstood two nuclear explosions and sank on July 30, 1946, as it was impossible to conduct damage control on the radioactive ship.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB010_FULL"
msgstr "Nagato"

msgid "IDS_PJSB010_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSB010_YEAR"
msgstr "1920"

msgid "IDS_PJSB011"
msgstr "Mikasa"

msgid "IDS_PJSB011_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The last of the four battleships ordered under the 1896 ten-year program. In terms of design, it was similar to its predecessors, with its main distinction being improved armor for the secondary battery casemates.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The battleship was designed and built by Armstrong in Elswick, and this ship surpassed the latest British battleships. During the Russo-Japanese War, as the flagship of Admiral Tōgō Heihachirō, Mikasa participated in the Battle of the Yellow Sea and the Battle of Tsushima.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship was sunk in September 1905 in Sasebo due to an explosion in its aft magazines, after which it was raised, repaired, and armed with new main battery guns. By the start of World War I, the battleship was obsolete, and in September 1921, it became a first-class coastal defense ship.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In accordance with the Washington Naval Treaty, the ship was decommissioned from the fleet. From 1926 to 1945, Mikasa operated as a floating museum but was later abandoned. In 1960–61, the ship was restored and installed on land as a memorial.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB011_FULL"
msgstr "Mikasa"

msgid "IDS_PJSB011_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB011_YEAR"
msgstr "1902"

msgid "IDS_PJSB013"
msgstr "Amagi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB013_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the Russo-Japanese War, Japan planned to build eight battleships and eight fast armored cruisers. Slightly later, the armored cruisers were replaced with battleships, and the number of ships was reduced. However, after the start of World War I, funding increased and the design of battlecruisers began. Between 1916 and 1918, about twenty preliminary design variants were developed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Between 1920 and 1921, four ships based on the design designated B-64 were laid down, but construction was canceled according to the decisions of the Washington Naval Conference. Japan was permitted to convert two battlecruisers into aircraft carriers, and in 1923 the conversion of the Amagi began. In September, the hull of the Amagi, which was on the slipways in Yokosuka, was destroyed by the Great Kantō earthquake.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB013_FULL"
msgstr "Amagi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB013_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSB013_YEAR"
msgstr "1919"

msgid "IDS_PJSB018"
msgstr "Yamato"

msgid "IDS_PJSB018_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The design for the new battleship was selected from 24 options developed since 1934. The ship received a fully turbine-powered propulsion system. The hull was refined in a test basin to give it an optimal hydrodynamic form, leading to the creation of a massive bulbous bow. To build such a large ship, it was necessary to deepen the existing dry docks and construct a vessel for transporting the turrets. The building slip and dock were enclosed by a high fence.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Yamato, the lead ship of the class, was built in Kure. Throughout 1942, the ship served as the flagship of the Japanese Combined Fleet. From the bridge of the Yamato, Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto directed the Battle of Midway. In December 1943, the battleship was torpedoed by an American submarine and put in for repairs.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In October 1944, Yamato took part in the Battle of Leyte Gulf, where it fought near Samar Island against American escort carriers. Yamato was sunk off the coast of Kyushu during Operation Ten-Go by a massive strike from U.S. Navy aircraft on April 7, 1945.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB018_FULL"
msgstr "Yamato"

msgid "IDS_PJSB018_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSB018_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PJSB021"
msgstr "Izumo"

msgid "IDS_PJSB021_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Washington Naval Treaty imposed significant restrictions on the size of the Japanese battleship fleet. To maintain defensive capabilities, Japan was once again forced to compensate for the small number of ships with their high performance.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1934, the Japanese navy decided to abandon displacement limitations in order to definitively surpass American battleships in combat power. In October 1934, the Imperial Japanese Navy General Staff issued technical specifications for designing a battleship with a displacement of 69,500 tons.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Some of the developed designs envisioned a ship armed with three turrets in the bow, each intended to mount three 410-mm guns. The secondary armament consisted of 155-mm guns. The propulsion system was a combined diesel-steam turbine arrangement. This project was not realized, and its further refinement led to the emergence of the Yamato-class battleships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB021_FULL"
msgstr "Izumo"

msgid "IDS_PJSB021_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PJSB021_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PJSB111"
msgstr "Satsuma"

msgid "IDS_PJSB111_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Plans for the construction of the Yamato-class battleships envisioned building eight ships by 1950. In early 1941, the program was adjusted: based on intelligence data about the construction of large ships in the United States, the Imperial Japanese Navy General Staff requested the construction of new battleships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Most of the ships were intended to be built according to an improved design based on the Yamato class—with 510 mm main battery artillery. The variant with eight main battery guns in four turrets had a standard displacement of 86,730 tons—a quarter more than that of the Yamato. Two super-battleships were assigned hull numbers 798 and 799, but the project was not realized due to Japan's defeat in the war.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB111_FULL"
msgstr "Satsuma"

msgid "IDS_PJSB111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB111_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PJSB208"
msgstr "Yumihari"

msgid "IDS_PJSB208_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the end of the Russo-Japanese War and the assimilation of its lessons, Japan adopted the \"Eight-Eight\" plan, which called for the navy to consist of eight battleships and eight high-speed armored cruisers. This plan later underwent a series of changes; notably, the armored cruisers were replaced by battlecruisers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Japan viewed America as a likely adversary in a naval war. Therefore, when World War I began and the United States announced its naval expansion plans, Japan increased funding for the construction of new ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Between 1916 and 1918, around twenty preliminary design variants were developed, representing an evolution of the Kongō-class. The most detailed was the project designated B-62, which included eight variants of a ship with four twin-gun main battery turrets. It was planned to build two ships of this type. The project was later modified and led to the development of the Amagi-class ships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB208_FULL"
msgstr "Yumihari"

msgid "IDS_PJSB208_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB208_YEAR"
msgstr "1916"

msgid "IDS_PJSB209"
msgstr "Adatara"

msgid "IDS_PJSB209_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the United States announced an ambitious shipbuilding program in 1919, the Japanese Navy's development plan was revised: it required the construction of four battleships and four battlecruisers. Thirteen design variants for battleships and battlecruisers were proposed. Variant A was selected for the battleship, which became the Kii-class, and Variant K was chosen for the construction of the battlecruiser.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Four ships of the battlecruiser design were ordered. Construction was planned to begin in 1922, but due to the commencement of the Washington Naval Conference, these ships were never laid down, and shortly after its conclusion, their construction was canceled.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB209_FULL"
msgstr "Adatara"

msgid "IDS_PJSB209_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB209_YEAR"
msgstr "1921"

msgid "IDS_PJSB210"
msgstr "Bungo"

msgid "IDS_PJSB210_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Following the review of the Japanese fleet development plan, new battleships and battlecruisers were ordered. The Kii-class battleships were to be equipped with ten 410-mm guns in five turrets, while the No. 13-class battlecruiser was intended to have 457-mm armament.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Concurrently, the L project was developed—an enlarged variant of the Kii-class battleship, also armed with five main battery turrets but with 457-mm guns. However, this project exceeded the displacement of the Kii-class battleships by 9,000 tons, which would have led to a significant increase in its cost. The appearance of both the Kii-class and these ships was prevented by the Washington Conference, which resulted in a temporary ban on battleship construction and a limitation on their total tonnage.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB210_FULL"
msgstr "Bungo"

msgid "IDS_PJSB210_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB210_YEAR"
msgstr "1921"

msgid "IDS_PJSB502"
msgstr "Mikasa '04"

msgid "IDS_PJSB502_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The last of the four battleships ordered under the ten-year program of 1896. In terms of design, it was similar to its predecessors, with its main difference being improved casemate armor for the anti-torpedo guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The battleship was designed and built by Armstrong, surpassing the latest British battleships. Laid down in Elswick in January 1899, launched in November 1900, and commissioned in March 1902. It was Admiral Togo's flagship during the Russo-Japanese War, taking part in the battles in the Yellow Sea and the Battle of Tsushima.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB502_FULL"
msgstr "Mikasa '04"

msgid "IDS_PJSB502_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB502_YEAR"
msgstr "1902"

msgid "IDS_PJSB506"
msgstr "Mutsu"

msgid "IDS_PJSB506_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Mutsu, the second battleship of the Nagato class, was built in Yokosuka and commissioned just as the Washington Conference had begun. The United States insisted on the ship being decommissioned, but the Japanese managed to preserve it: one of the arguments was that part of the funds for its construction had been collected by schoolchildren from all over the country.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>From 1934 to 1936, like all Japanese battleships, Mutsu underwent a major modernization with enhanced armor. The ship participated in World War II: it covered the attack on Pearl Harbor in December 1941, took part in the Battle of Midway and the battles around the Solomon Islands in 1942. On June 8, 1943, Mutsu was lost in Hashirajima harbor due to an explosion in the third turret's magazine.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB506_FULL"
msgstr "Mutsu"

msgid "IDS_PJSB506_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSB506_YEAR"
msgstr "1921"

msgid "IDS_PJSB507"
msgstr "Ashitaka"

msgid "IDS_PJSB507_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The fourth ship of the Amagi class was laid down in December 1921 in Nagasaki at the Mitsubishi shipyard under the name Ashitaka.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In July 1922, in accordance with the decision of the Washington Naval Conference, its construction was cancelled. The ship's hull was dismantled on the slipway in 1924 — precisely the year the ship was scheduled to enter service.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB507_FULL"
msgstr "Ashitaka"

msgid "IDS_PJSB507_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSB507_YEAR"
msgstr "1919"

msgid "IDS_PJSB508"
msgstr "Kii"

msgid "IDS_PJSB508_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the United States announced an ambitious shipbuilding program in 1919, Japan revised its fleet development plans: it required the construction of four more battleships and four battlecruisers. Naval architect Yuzuru Hiraga developed thirteen preliminary design options for ships of these classes.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The main battleship design chosen was a variant with 410mm artillery in five twin-gun turrets — essentially an enhanced version of the Amagi-class battlecruisers. The first two ships were ordered in October 1921, but by February of the following year, preparations for construction were halted due to the Washington Naval Conference, and in 1924, construction was definitively canceled.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB508_FULL"
msgstr "Kii"

msgid "IDS_PJSB508_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PJSB508_YEAR"
msgstr "1921"

msgid "IDS_PJSB509"
msgstr "Musashi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB509_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Musashi, the second Yamato-class battleship, was built in Nagasaki at the Mitsubishi shipyard. In 1943, the ship became the flagship of the Combined Fleet.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In early 1943, Musashi was transferred to Truk, Japan's main naval base in the South Pacific theater of operations, and was primarily used for patrol operations. In early 1944, the battleship sustained a torpedo hit and returned to Japan for repairs.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Musashi participated in the Battle of the Philippine Sea. During the Battle of Leyte Gulf on October 24, 1944, the battleship was sunk by American carrier-based bombers and torpedo bombers, sustaining approximately 19 torpedo and 17 bomb hits.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB509_FULL"
msgstr "Musashi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB509_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSB509_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PJSB510"
msgstr "Shikishima"

msgid "IDS_PJSB510_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The plans for constructing the Yamato-class battleships envisioned the building of eight ships by 1950. At the beginning of 1941, the program was adjusted: based on intelligence data regarding the scale of large ship construction in the United States, the leadership of the Japanese Navy decided to increase the number of battleships under construction. One of the ships was to be built within the Yamato class, and all subsequent ones — according to an improved design, with 510-mm main caliber artillery.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The new project received the designation A-150. One of the variants proposed six main caliber guns in three turrets and a standard displacement of 65,700 tons. The project was not realized due to Japan's defeat in the war.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB510_FULL"
msgstr "Shikishima"

msgid "IDS_PJSB510_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB510_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PJSB517"
msgstr "Hyūga"

msgid "IDS_PJSB517_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The second battleship of the Ise class was built at the Mitsubishi shipyard in Nagasaki. The ship did not fight in the First World War but participated in the intervention in Soviet Russia.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The battleship was modernized in two stages in 1927–28 and 1931–32, and underwent a significant reconstruction in 1934–36, which included strengthening its armor and power plant. By the start of the Second World War, the ship was considered obsolete and saw almost no combat action in the Pacific.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB517_FULL"
msgstr "Hyūga"

msgid "IDS_PJSB517_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSB517_YEAR"
msgstr "1918"

msgid "IDS_PJSB519"
msgstr "Hizen"

msgid "IDS_PJSB519_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1934, work began in Japan on the A-140 battleship project. By 1936, 24 variants of the preliminary design were ready, differing in displacement, maximum speed, and main battery caliber.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>One of the variants featured a classic superfiring arrangement of four turrets, each with three 410-mm guns. The secondary battery consisted of 155-mm guns in triple turrets. The propulsion system was a combined diesel-steam turbine arrangement. This project was not realized, and its further development led to the emergence of the Yamato-class battleships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB519_FULL"
msgstr "Hizen"

msgid "IDS_PJSB519_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB519_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PJSB520"
msgstr "Raijū"

msgid "IDS_PJSB520_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The experimental battleship Raijū was built under conditions of utmost secrecy at the naval shipyard in Kure as part of the Alliance's program to create new-generation universal combat platforms.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship's name refers to a mythical creature from Japanese folklore associated with lightning and storms. This image was reflected in the vessel's concept: Raijū was conceived as a strike combat unit capable of delivering crushing blows with terrifying power, reacting swiftly to threats, and withstanding attacks even from submarines thanks to enhanced torpedo protection.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Classified. Not officially listed in any fleet's arsenal.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB520_FULL"
msgstr "Raijū"

msgid "IDS_PJSB520_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB520_YEAR"
msgstr "1956"

msgid "IDS_PJSB526"
msgstr "Ise"

msgid "IDS_PJSB526_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The battleships built under the 1912 program were initially intended to repeat the Fusō class. However, during the design process, changes were made to the blueprints, incorporating lessons learned from the construction of their predecessors and new requirements from the naval staff. The main difference was the new arrangement of the artillery, which simplified fire control.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Ise was built in Kobe at the Kawasaki shipyard. The ship did not see combat in World War I but participated in the intervention in Soviet Russia. During its modernization in the 1930s, the ship's armor and the power of its propulsion plant were enhanced.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>By the beginning of World War II, the battleship was obsolete and saw almost no action in the Pacific. Following the loss of carriers in the Battle of Midway, Ise was rebuilt into a hybrid battleship-carrier: the aft group of turrets was replaced with a flight deck. In 1944, Ise participated in the Battle of Cape Engaño, and in 1945, it transported strategic cargo from China to Japan. In July 1945, the ship was sunk by American bombers near Ondo Seto Island.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB526_FULL"
msgstr "Ise"

msgid "IDS_PJSB526_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSB526_YEAR"
msgstr "1917"

msgid "IDS_PJSB529"
msgstr "Iwami"

msgid "IDS_PJSB529_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A battleship project variant developed in Japan in the mid-1930s. The ship was intended to have four twin-gun main battery turrets.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The project is distinguished by its reinforced 100 mm dual-purpose artillery, improved anti-aircraft armament, and the presence of torpedo tubes.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB529_FULL"
msgstr "Iwami"

msgid "IDS_PJSB529_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB529_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PJSB539"
msgstr "Daisen"

msgid "IDS_PJSB539_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1916, as part of fleet development, the Japanese parliament authorized the construction of two battlecruisers in addition to the Kongō-class ships. That same year, Japanese naval architects presented four battlecruiser designs, which differed in the power of their propulsion plants and specific elements of their armor.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>A characteristic feature of these designs was their six-shaft propulsion system. After a series of refinements, they became the basis for the Amagi-class battlecruisers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB539_FULL"
msgstr "Daisen"

msgid "IDS_PJSB539_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB539_YEAR"
msgstr "1916"

msgid "IDS_PJSB549"
msgstr "Tsurugi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB549_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The differences between the battleship and battlecruiser projects under the new Japanese naval development plan were minimal: the Type No. 13 battlecruisers did not differ in speed from the Kii-class battleships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>This fell outside the framework of the accepted naval tactics in Japan, which assumed the existence of a \"fast wing of the fleet.\" To increase speed and transform the No. 13 design into a true battlecruiser, it would have been possible to sacrifice one of the main battery turrets.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB549_FULL"
msgstr "Tsurugi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB549_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB549_YEAR"
msgstr "1921"

msgid "IDS_PJSB559"
msgstr "Iwami B"

msgid "IDS_PJSB559_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A battleship project variant developed in Japan in the mid-1930s. The ship was intended to have four twin-gun main battery turrets.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The project is distinguished by its reinforced 100 mm dual-purpose artillery, improved anti-aircraft armament, and the presence of torpedo tubes.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB559_FULL"
msgstr "Iwami B"

msgid "IDS_PJSB559_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB559_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PJSB569"
msgstr "Hakusan"

msgid "IDS_PJSB569_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1907, Japan adopted the \"Eight-Eight\" plan, under which the fleet was to consist of eight modern battleships and the same number of cruisers. The plan was revised several times — it was reviewed once again when the United States announced an ambitious shipbuilding program in 1919. Pre-war ships such as Fusō, Ise, and Kongō were excluded from the program, and the count started from Nagato and Amagi. Thus, four battleships and four battlecruisers needed to be built.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Thirteen variants of battleships and battlecruisers were proposed. Variant K was chosen for the battlecruisers, and four ships were ordered under it. Construction was scheduled to begin in 1922, but due to the start of the Washington Conference, the ships were not laid down, and after its conclusion, they were canceled. The battlecruisers did not even receive names — in history, they are known as \"Type No. 13\".</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB569_FULL"
msgstr "Hakusan"

msgid "IDS_PJSB569_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB569_YEAR"
msgstr "1921"

msgid "IDS_PJSB599"
msgstr "Owari-no-Oni"

msgid "IDS_PJSB599_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A snow-white giant with golden patterns adorning its hull like motifs on an expensive kimono. On the stern rests the visage of an Oni—a guardian of ancient fury dwelling in the province of Owari.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Its appearance combines exquisite beauty with the merciless power of a demon. This is not merely a ship, but a legend born of great craftsmanship. Its appearance on the horizon signals the beginning and end of battle, and the roar of its guns forces the sea to fall silent in respectful terror.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB599_FULL"
msgstr "Owari-no-Oni"

msgid "IDS_PJSB599_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB599_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PJSB700"
msgstr "ARP Yamato"

msgid "IDS_PJSB700_DESCR"
msgstr ""
"A Fleet of Fog heavy battleship modeled after the lead ship of the Imperial Japanese Navy's Yamato-class series of battleships. \n"
"This warship has been modeled after the same-name ship from \"ARPEGGIO OF BLUE STEEL -ARS NOVA-.\""

msgid "IDS_PJSB700_FULL"
msgstr "ARP Yamato"

msgid "IDS_PJSB700_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSB700_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PJSB705"
msgstr "ARP Kongō"

msgid "IDS_PJSB705_DESCR"
msgstr ""
"A Fleet of Fog battleship modeled after the lead ship of the Imperial Japanese Navy's Kongō-class battleships, Kongō. As the flagship of the First Oriental Fleet, her Mental Model—Kongō—honors the Admiralty Code above all else. She seeks to eliminate the submarine I-401, who chose to deviate from the Code.\n"
"This warship has been modeled after Kongō as she appears in \"ARPEGGIO OF BLUE STEEL -ARS NOVA-.\""

msgid "IDS_PJSB705_FULL"
msgstr "ARP Kongō"

msgid "IDS_PJSB705_NUMBER"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PJSB705_YEAR"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PJSB706"
msgstr "ARP Kirishima"

msgid "IDS_PJSB706_DESCR"
msgstr ""
"A Fleet of Fog battleship modeled after the Imperial Japanese Navy's 4th Kongō-class battleship, Kirishima. She was first seen engaging I-401, along with Haruna, at the battle in Yokosuka Bay. She was eventually defeated by I-401 due to her \"trump card\" backfiring under her rival's tactics.\n"
"This warship has been modeled after Kirishima as she appears in \"ARPEGGIO OF BLUE STEEL -ARS NOVA-.\""

msgid "IDS_PJSB706_FULL"
msgstr "ARP Kirishima"

msgid "IDS_PJSB706_NUMBER"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PJSB706_YEAR"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PJSB707"
msgstr "ARP Haruna"

msgid "IDS_PJSB707_DESCR"
msgstr ""
"A Fleet of Fog battleship modeled after the Imperial Japanese Navy's 3rd Kongō-class battleship, Haruna. First seen engaging I-401 in combat alongside Kirishima, she was defeated by the superior tactics of Gunzo Chihaya, commander of I-401. Her Mental Model—Haruna—always wears a large overcoat and has a keen interest in the human language, \"collecting\" word samples as she hears them.\n"
"This warship has been modeled after Haruna as she appears in \"ARPEGGIO OF BLUE STEEL -ARS NOVA-.\""

msgid "IDS_PJSB707_FULL"
msgstr "ARP Haruna"

msgid "IDS_PJSB707_NUMBER"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PJSB707_YEAR"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PJSB708"
msgstr "ARP Hiei"

msgid "IDS_PJSB708_DESCR"
msgstr ""
"A Fleet of Fog battleship modeled after the Imperial Japanese Navy's 2nd Kongō-class battleship, Hiei. Equipped with the Mirroring System bestowed by Musashi, she became the new flagship of the First Oriental Fleet after Kongō's departure. Repurposing the Fleet as the Student Council of the Fog, she maintains strict order within the Fleet of Fog as the Student Council President.\n"
"This warship has been modeled after Hiei as she appears in \"ARPEGGIO OF BLUE STEEL -ARS NOVA- DC.\""

msgid "IDS_PJSB708_FULL"
msgstr "ARP Hiei"

msgid "IDS_PJSB708_NUMBER"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PJSB708_YEAR"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PJSB715"
msgstr "HSF Hiei"

msgid "IDS_PJSB715_DESCR"
msgstr "A nautical training ship assigned to Yokosuka Girls' Marine High School. Despite the ship's long history, she is not inferior to modern ships due to frequent remodeling."

msgid "IDS_PJSB715_FULL"
msgstr "HSF Hiei"

msgid "IDS_PJSB715_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSB715_YEAR"
msgstr "1913"

msgid "IDS_PJSB799"
msgstr "ARP Kirishima"

msgid "IDS_PJSB799_FULL"
msgstr "ARP Kirishima"

msgid "IDS_PJSB801"
msgstr "Kawachi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB801_DESCR"
msgstr "Battleship Kawachi, which is used in the Introductory Mission"

msgid "IDS_PJSB802"
msgstr "Kawachi"

msgid "IDS_PJSB802_DESCR"
msgstr "Battleship Kawachi, which is used in the Introductory Mission"

msgid "IDS_PJSB810"
msgstr "Yamato CLR"

msgid "IDS_PJSB810_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The design for the new battleship was selected from 24 options developed since 1934. The ship received a fully turbine-powered propulsion system. The hull was refined in a test basin to give it an optimal hydrodynamic form, leading to the creation of a massive bulbous bow. To build such a large ship, it was necessary to deepen the existing dry docks and construct a vessel for transporting the turrets. The building slip and dock were enclosed by a high fence.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Yamato, the lead ship of the class, was built in Kure. Throughout 1942, the ship served as the flagship of the Japanese Combined Fleet. From the bridge of the Yamato, Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto directed the Battle of Midway. In December 1943, the battleship was torpedoed by an American submarine and put in for repairs.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In October 1944, Yamato took part in the Battle of Leyte Gulf, where it fought near Samar Island against American escort carriers. Yamato was sunk off the coast of Kyushu during Operation Ten-Go by a massive strike from U.S. Navy aircraft on April 7, 1945.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSB810_FULL"
msgstr "Yamato CLR"

msgid "IDS_PJSB810_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSB810_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PJSB878"
msgstr "Ignis Purgatio"

msgid "IDS_PJSB878_DESCR"
msgstr "Originally designed for a Naval mission of the purpose now long forgotten, Ignis Purgatio was lost during the Horus Heresy. After missing for several millennia, the ship was rediscovered on one of the Frontier worlds in the Jericho Reach. Though clearly a warship, it was put to use as a transport by its new, uncouth masters who failed to recognize its true potential. After undergoing intensive repairs and cleansing its quarters of its previous inhabitants, Ignis Purgatio was eventually returned to Imperial Navy service and, since then, it has become an essential component of Battlefleet Solar. Once again, it acts as a fearsome guard dog for humanity and, by proxy, the Emperor Himself."

msgid "IDS_PJSB878_FULL"
msgstr "Ignis Purgatio"

msgid "IDS_PJSB878_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSB878_YEAR"
msgstr "1919"

msgid "IDS_PJSB888"
msgstr "Ragnarok"

msgid "IDS_PJSB888_DESCR"
msgstr "The vessel is immeasurably ancient—a relic of days long forgotten. It was built in the shipyards orbiting a world of proud and fearsome warriors. Though the citizens adhered to the Imperial creed and paid their tithes, secret cabals on the planet's surface worshipped Khorne, the Lord of Rage. These cults were strong. They were hated. They were outnumbered. At the appointed hour, they rose up and tried to overthrow their loyalist masters. They failed. They were defeated in a long, bloody war and all, to a man, perished in flames. Their name was erased from history. But the fickle whims of the Warp decreed that their vengeful spirits would forever haunt the vessel Ragnarok, whose hull was being constructed in the docks above the planet's surface. The furious spirits invaded the hull and ensured the vessel served none but the Ruinous Powers. To this day, Ragnarok and all aboard it cry, \"Vengeance! Death! Blood for the Blood God!\""

msgid "IDS_PJSB888_FULL"
msgstr "Ragnarok"

msgid "IDS_PJSB888_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSB888_YEAR"
msgstr "1919"

msgid "IDS_PJSB906"
msgstr "Ise 2"

msgid "IDS_PJSB906_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The battleships built under the 1912 program were initially intended to repeat the Fusō class. However, during the design process, changes were made to the blueprints, incorporating lessons learned from the construction of their predecessors and new requirements from the naval staff. The main difference was the new arrangement of the artillery, which simplified fire control.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Ise was built in Kobe at the Kawasaki shipyard. The ship did not see combat in World War I but participated in the intervention in Soviet Russia. During its modernization in the 1930s, the ship's armor and the power of its propulsion plant were enhanced.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>By the beginning of World War II, the battleship was obsolete and saw almost no action in the Pacific. Following the loss of carriers in the Battle of Midway, Ise was rebuilt into a hybrid battleship-carrier: the aft group of turrets was replaced with a flight deck. In 1944, Ise participated in the Battle of Cape Engaño, and in 1945, it transported strategic cargo from China to Japan. In July 1945, the ship was sunk by American bombers near Ondo Seto Island.</p>"

