msgid "IDS_PJSB999"
msgstr "Satsuma (old)"

msgid "IDS_PJSB999_DESCR"
msgstr "A further development of the Design A-150 super battleships, equipped with an even stronger battery of eight 510 mm guns. "

msgid "IDS_PJSB999_FULL"
msgstr "Satsuma (< 22.09.2021)"

msgid "IDS_PJSB999_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSB999_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PJSC001_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSC001_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PJSC004"
msgstr "Yūbari"

msgid "IDS_PJSC004_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>An experimental light cruiser designed by the chief shipbuilder of the Japanese Navy, Yuzuru Hiraga. This ship was intended to test design solutions meant for scout cruisers—the future Furutaka-class ships. Specifically, it was necessary to verify the possibility of reducing the hull's proportion of the displacement without compromising strength. This was achieved by incorporating the armor belt into the hull as a structural element. The design features of Yūbari became the standard for all subsequent Japanese cruisers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Yūbari was built in Sasebo. For most of its peacetime career, the cruiser was held in reserve or served as a training ship.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, it served as the flagship of the invasion force for Wake Atoll, participated in the Battle of the Coral Sea, the invasion of New Guinea, the Solomon Islands campaign, and fought in the Battle of Savo Island. The cruiser Yūbari was sunk on April 28, 1944, off the coast of Palau by the submarine USS Bluegill.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC004_FULL"
msgstr "Yūbari"

msgid "IDS_PJSC004_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC004_YEAR"
msgstr "1923"

msgid "IDS_PJSC005"
msgstr "Furutaka"

msgid "IDS_PJSC005_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>When Japan learned about the characteristics of the American Omaha-class cruisers, the designs for future ships for the Japanese fleet were revised. The appearance of the new cruiser was significantly influenced by the British Hawkins-class ships. In the summer of 1921, naval architect Yuzuru Hiraga proposed a design for a scout cruiser, based on the structural solutions tested on the Yūbari. The ship's armament consisted of six 203 mm guns in single turret mounts.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Furutaka was built in Nagasaki. In 1937–39, the ship underwent a radical modernization: the single turrets were replaced with three twin turrets, and the coal-fired boilers were replaced with oil-fired ones.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, the cruiser participated in military operations in the South Pacific, fought in the Battle of the Coral Sea and off Savo Island. During the Battle of Cape Esperance on October 11, 1942, the Furutaka was hit by approximately 90 shells and one torpedo, which caused a torpedo detonation. The cruiser sank on the morning of October 12.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC005_FULL"
msgstr "Furutaka"

msgid "IDS_PJSC005_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSC005_YEAR"
msgstr "1926"

msgid "IDS_PJSC006"
msgstr "Furutaka"

msgid "IDS_PJSC006_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>When Japan learned about the characteristics of the American Omaha-class cruisers, the designs for future ships for the Japanese fleet were revised. The appearance of the new cruiser was significantly influenced by the British Hawkins-class ships. In the summer of 1921, naval architect Yuzuru Hiraga proposed a design for a scout cruiser, based on the structural solutions tested on the Yūbari. The ship's armament consisted of six 203 mm guns in single turret mounts.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Furutaka was built in Nagasaki. In 1937–39, the ship underwent a radical modernization: the single turrets were replaced with three twin turrets, and the coal-fired boilers were replaced with oil-fired ones.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, the cruiser participated in military operations in the South Pacific, fought in the Battle of the Coral Sea and off Savo Island. During the Battle of Cape Esperance on October 11, 1942, the Furutaka was hit by approximately 90 shells and one torpedo, which caused a torpedo detonation. The cruiser sank on the morning of October 12.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC006_FULL"
msgstr "Furutaka"

msgid "IDS_PJSC006_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSC006_YEAR"
msgstr "1926"

msgid "IDS_PJSC007"
msgstr "Aoba"

msgid "IDS_PJSC007_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Aoba-class cruisers were originally intended to replicate the Furutaka class; however, at the insistence of the Japanese Navy General Staff, a series of changes were made to the design. Thus, the single-gun main battery turrets were replaced with twin-gun turrets, the 76-mm anti-aircraft guns were replaced with new 120-mm ones, and a catapult for seaplanes was installed between the aft superstructure and the turret.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Aoba, was built in Nagasaki. Between 1938 and 1940, the cruiser underwent a modernization that included strengthening its anti-aircraft armament and converting all boilers to oil-firing.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship actively participated in World War II: it landed troops on Guam and Wake Island, fought in the Coral Sea, off Savo Island, at Cape Esperance, and also escorted convoys. The cruiser Aoba sank in Kure harbor after being bombed by Allied forces on July 24, 1945.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC007_EXAMPLE"
msgstr "AOBA"

msgid "IDS_PJSC007_FULL"
msgstr "Aoba"

msgid "IDS_PJSC007_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSC007_YEAR"
msgstr "1927"

msgid "IDS_PJSC008"
msgstr "Myōkō"

msgid "IDS_PJSC008_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Japanese naval development program called for the construction of cruisers with a displacement of 10,000 tons. The final design differed from the original specifications in several ways: by eliminating half of the torpedo tubes, the number of main battery turrets was increased to five, and anti-torpedo protection was enhanced with longitudinal bulkheads. The thickness of the armor belt was increased, and anti-aircraft defense was strengthened.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Myōkō was built in Yokosuka. From 1933 to 1935, the cruiser underwent modernization. During the Second Sino-Japanese War, the ship participated in the landing on Hainan Island.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, the cruiser fought in the Java Sea and Coral Sea, at Midway and the Solomon Islands, in the Philippine Sea and Leyte Gulf, where it sustained a torpedo hit. From February 1945, the ship served as a floating anti-aircraft battery in Singapore.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The cruiser Myōkō surrendered to the British Royal Navy in September 1945 and was scuttled in the Strait of Malacca in July of the following year.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC008_FULL"
msgstr "Myōkō"

msgid "IDS_PJSC008_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSC008_YEAR"
msgstr "1929"

msgid "IDS_PJSC009"
msgstr "Mogami"

msgid "IDS_PJSC009_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the construction of the four Takao-class ships, under the terms of the London Naval Treaty, Japan could no longer build cruisers with 203 mm guns. However, the country still had the right to build cruisers with guns up to 155 mm in caliber.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The next fleet replenishment program included four cruisers with 155 mm guns in five triple turrets. The design was intended to allow the replacement of these turrets with twin turrets mounting 203 mm guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Mogami, was built in Kure. During the Fourth Fleet Incident, the ship sustained damage from a typhoon, leading to an unscheduled repair.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, the cruiser participated in the occupation of Indochina, the invasions of Malaya, Sarawak, and Indonesia, and the battles in the Sunda Strait, at Midway, and in the Philippine Sea. During the Battle of Leyte Gulf on October 25, 1944, Mogami was heavily damaged by American torpedo bombers and finished off by the Japanese destroyer Akebono.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC009_FULL"
msgstr "Mogami"

msgid "IDS_PJSC009_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSC009_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PJSC012"
msgstr "Ibuki"

msgid "IDS_PJSC012_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Under the 1941 Emergency Fleet Replenishment Program, heavy cruisers of an improved design based on the Mogami class were built. In 1942, two ships were laid down in Kure and Nagasaki, but a month later, the construction of the second cruiser was canceled.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Ibuki was laid down in April 1942 and launched in May 1943. After the launch, construction was suspended, and then the cruiser began to be converted into a light aircraft carrier. The work was finally halted when the ship was approximately 80% complete.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC012_FULL"
msgstr "Ibuki"

msgid "IDS_PJSC012_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSC012_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PJSC013"
msgstr "Kuma"

msgid "IDS_PJSC013_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Upon learning the characteristics of the American Omaha-class ships, the leadership of the Japanese Navy ordered cruisers of increased size and combat power. Based on the Tenryū, a cruiser design was created that combined the functions of a destroyer leader and a scout. Thanks to the increase in displacement, the machinery was strengthened, allowing for high speed, and the number of main battery guns grew to seven.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Kuma was built in Sasebo. The cruiser participated in the intervention in Soviet Russia. In the 1920s–30s, the ship patrolled the Chinese coast. During World War II, the Kuma participated in the landings in the Philippines. In 1942–44, the cruiser took part in combat operations in New Guinea and then patrolled the waters of the Dutch East Indies. On January 11, 1944, the Kuma was torpedoed by the British submarine HMS Tally-Ho in the Strait of Malacca, after which it caught fire and sank.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC013_FULL"
msgstr "Kuma"

msgid "IDS_PJSC013_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PJSC013_YEAR"
msgstr "1920"

msgid "IDS_PJSC014"
msgstr "Kitakami"

msgid "IDS_PJSC014_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The cruiser Kitakami, a Kuma-class vessel, was built in Sasebo. From 1922 to 1924, the ship served as the flagship of the 2nd Destroyer Flotilla, and later served with the 1st Submarine Flotilla, the Taiwan Defense Force, and the Sasebo Naval Base Defense Force.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>With the outbreak of the Second Sino-Japanese War, the cruiser covered Japanese troop landing operations. In 1941, Kitakami was converted into a torpedo cruiser, receiving an armament of ten quadruple 610-mm torpedo tubes.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1941–42, Kitakami primarily performed convoy and training missions, after which it was converted into a high-speed transport. In this role, it escorted convoys to New Guinea, the Solomon Islands, and Truk.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1944, Kitakami sustained two torpedo hits from the British submarine HMS Templar. During repairs, the ship was converted into a carrier for Kaiten manned torpedoes.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In July 1945, the cruiser was damaged by bombers from Task Force 38 and subsequently used as a tender. Kitakami was decommissioned from the fleet in November 1945.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC014_FULL"
msgstr "Kitakami"

msgid "IDS_PJSC014_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PJSC014_YEAR"
msgstr "1921"

msgid "IDS_PJSC015"
msgstr "Tenryū"

msgid "IDS_PJSC015_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The design of the Tenryū-class cruisers was heavily influenced by the British Arethusa and C-class cruisers. The cruisers were intended to serve as destroyer leaders and operate alongside the Furutaka-class fleet scouts. Powerful steam turbines allowed them to achieve respectable speed despite their outdated mixed-firing boilers. The ships were equipped with new 140 mm main battery guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class was built in Yokosuka. During the 1920s and 1930s, Tenryū participated in interventions in Soviet Russia and Shanghai, and in the Second Sino-Japanese War.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Tenryū took part in World War II. In 1941–42, it was used as an escort vessel during the invasions of New Guinea and the Solomon Islands. The cruiser participated in the Battle of Savo Island and the Guadalcanal Campaign. Near the port of Madang, Tenryū was torpedoed by the American submarine USS Albacore and sank on December 19, 1942.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC015_FULL"
msgstr "Tenryū"

msgid "IDS_PJSC015_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSC015_YEAR"
msgstr "1919"

msgid "IDS_PJSC018"
msgstr "Tone"

msgid "IDS_PJSC018_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>To augment the Japanese fleet, the construction of two cruisers based on the Mogami-class design was planned. To improve protection and stability, the design omitted the fifth turret, and the remaining four were relocated to the bow. This allowed for a reduction in the length of the armored belt, lightening the hull and ends of the ship, which in turn improved seakeeping qualities. The freed-up stern section allowed for the placement of seaplanes.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, the Tone, was built in Nagasaki. In 1941, the cruiser participated in the attack on Pearl Harbor and the invasion of Wake Island. In 1942, the Tone covered landings at Rabaul, participated in the Battle of the Java Sea, the Indian Ocean raid, the Battle of Midway, as well as the battles of the Eastern Solomons and the Santa Cruz Islands. In 1944, the ship fought in the Philippine Sea and in Leyte Gulf. The cruiser Tone was sunk in Kure Harbor during an American bomber raid on July 24, 1945.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC018_FULL"
msgstr "Tone"

msgid "IDS_PJSC018_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSC018_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PJSC026"
msgstr "Iwaki A"

msgid "IDS_PJSC026_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>According to the plans for the development of the Japanese fleet, in the late 1910s, it was intended to build six cruisers based on an improved design of the Tenryū-class.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>These ships were to carry an additional fifth main-caliber gun and achieve a lighter hull through a series of new design solutions. The project was not realized, and its further development led to the emergence of the Kuma-class cruisers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC026_FULL"
msgstr "Iwaki Alpha"

msgid "IDS_PJSC026_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PJSC026_YEAR"
msgstr "1916"

msgid "IDS_PJSC034"
msgstr "Zaō"

msgid "IDS_PJSC034_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>According to Japan's fleet replenishment program, it was planned to build eight heavy cruisers, which represented a further development of the Takao, Tone, and Mogami types.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ships' armament was to consist of twelve 203-mm guns in four turrets arranged in the classic layout, and their armor was to provide protection against 203-mm high-explosive shells. Subsequently, the number of ships was reduced to four, and then their construction was canceled altogether.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC034_FULL"
msgstr "Zaō"

msgid "IDS_PJSC034_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PJSC034_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PJSC035"
msgstr "Chikuma"

msgid "IDS_PJSC035_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the war against Russia, Japan began developing light cruisers. The foundation was the ship Tone, laid down in 1905, which adopted the \"clipper\" hull design from Russian auxiliary cruisers. The project was also influenced by British Town-class ships. For the first time on a Japanese cruiser, turbines were used.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Chikuma was built in Sasebo. During World War I, the ship pursued the squadron of Admiral Maximilian von Spee and the cruiser Emden. After the war, Chikuma patrolled the coast of China, and from 1924 served as a training ship for the Yokosuka Naval District.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC035_FULL"
msgstr "Chikuma"

msgid "IDS_PJSC035_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PJSC035_YEAR"
msgstr "1912"

msgid "IDS_PJSC037"
msgstr "Hashidate"

msgid "IDS_PJSC037_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A small gunboat built in Osaka and designed for operations in coastal areas. For its size, it was well-armed, including with anti-aircraft artillery. It had no armor and developed low speed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1940–41, Hashidate was part of the China Expeditionary Fleet and participated in the invasion of Hong Kong. The ship was sunk by the American submarine USS Picuda near Pratas Island on May 22, 1944.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC037_FULL"
msgstr "Hashidate"

msgid "IDS_PJSC037_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSC037_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PJSC038"
msgstr "Atago"

msgid "IDS_PJSC038_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Atago, a Takao-class cruiser, was built in Kure. Upon commissioning, the ship was assigned to the 4th Cruiser Division.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, it supported landings during the invasions of Malaya and the Philippines, participated in the Battle of the Java Sea, the Battle of the Eastern Solomons, the Battle of the Santa Cruz Islands, the Naval Battle of Guadalcanal, and the Battle of the Philippine Sea.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>From July 1944, the cruiser served as the flagship of Admiral Takeo Kurita's 1st Mobile Strike Force. Atago was sunk by the submarine USS Darter during the Battle of Leyte Gulf on October 23, 1944.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC038_FULL"
msgstr "Atago"

msgid "IDS_PJSC038_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSC038_YEAR"
msgstr "1932"

msgid "IDS_PJSC111"
msgstr "Senjō"

msgid "IDS_PJSC111_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Hypothetical development of Japanese heavy cruisers, which involves the installation of 60-caliber German 210 mm guns. Technical cooperation between Germany and Japan included the exchange of blueprints and technological charts for advanced models of equipment and armaments.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>This project differs from its predecessor (the 1941 heavy cruiser design) by its enhanced armor and auxiliary armament, primarily anti-aircraft weaponry.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC111_FULL"
msgstr "Senjō"

msgid "IDS_PJSC111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC111_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PJSC205"
msgstr "Agano"

msgid "IDS_PJSC205_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The design of the Agano-class cruisers began in the second half of the 1930s. Their primary intended role was reconnaissance, which demanded high speed above all else, necessitating a reduction in armor protection. The ships were armed with six 152-mm guns in three turrets.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Agano, was built in Sasebo. Upon entering service, the ship became the flagship of the 10th Destroyer Squadron. During World War II, the cruiser Agano participated in escort and patrol operations, including the Battle of Empress Augusta Bay near Bougainville Island. The cruiser was sunk by the submarine USS Skate while en route for repairs on February 17, 1944.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC205_FULL"
msgstr "Agano"

msgid "IDS_PJSC205_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSC205_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PJSC206"
msgstr "Gokase"

msgid "IDS_PJSC206_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>According to the fleet replenishment program, it was planned to build cruisers to lead flotillas of Shimakaze-class destroyers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Based on the Agano-class cruisers, a project was developed that differed from its predecessor by an increased number of main battery turrets, enhanced power plant, and anti-aircraft armament. After the Battle of Midway, the program was adjusted, and two ships were planned for construction. In June 1943, the construction of the ships was canceled.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC206_FULL"
msgstr "Gokase"

msgid "IDS_PJSC206_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC206_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PJSC207"
msgstr "Omono"

msgid "IDS_PJSC207_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The development of the Ōyodo-class cruisers, flagships of submarine squadrons, into full-fledged light cruisers with four triple 155mm gun turrets.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Unlike its predecessors, the ship lacks a stern hangar, the catapult has been moved amidships, two torpedo launchers have been added, and anti-aircraft armament has been strengthened.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC207_FULL"
msgstr "Omono"

msgid "IDS_PJSC207_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC207_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PJSC208"
msgstr "Shimanto"

msgid "IDS_PJSC208_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A hypothetical modernization of Mogami-class ships into anti-aircraft cruisers, featuring the installation of universal 150mm/60 guns developed in 1944 as the main battery.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The installation of 150mm universal and 100mm anti-aircraft guns would have transformed the Mogami-class cruisers into an effective weapon against American carrier-based and land-based aircraft.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC208_FULL"
msgstr "Shimanto"

msgid "IDS_PJSC208_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC208_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PJSC209"
msgstr "Takahashi"

msgid "IDS_PJSC209_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Japanese fleet expansion program included the construction of two cruisers armed with fifteen 155-mm guns in triple turrets and with a displacement of 8,450 tons.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The project was not implemented, and its further development led to the emergence of the Tone-class heavy cruisers. An alternative development option involves arming the cruisers with dual-purpose 150-mm guns with the aim of creating an anti-aircraft cruiser to counter American carrier-based and land-based aircraft.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC209_FULL"
msgstr "Takahashi"

msgid "IDS_PJSC209_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC209_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PJSC210"
msgstr "Yodo"

msgid "IDS_PJSC210_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the archive of Japanese shipbuilder Yuzuru Hiraga, there is a design for a cruiser with a displacement of 10,000 tons, armed with six triple turrets mounting 150-mm dual-purpose guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship was intended to carry aviation on board and was likely a design for a cruiser intended to serve as a flotilla leader for submarine squadrons.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC210_FULL"
msgstr "Yodo"

msgid "IDS_PJSC210_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC210_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PJSC503"
msgstr "Katori"

msgid "IDS_PJSC503_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Under the 1937 and 1939 fleet replenishment programs, training cruisers were built, designed to replace old armored cruisers from the Russo-Japanese War era. These ships had a mixed propulsion system, as well as a diverse set of armaments: 140 mm main guns, dual-purpose 127 mm guns, 25 mm anti-aircraft autocannons, 533 mm torpedoes, and a catapult with a seaplane. This variety was necessary for training cadets.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Katori was built in Yokohama. During World War II, the ship was primarily used as the flagship of the Sixth Fleet. Katori was sunk by the battleship Iowa's gunfire during the Japanese evacuation from Truk Atoll on February 18, 1944.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC503_FULL"
msgstr "Katori"

msgid "IDS_PJSC503_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSC503_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PJSC505"
msgstr "Yahagi"

msgid "IDS_PJSC505_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Yahagi, an Agano-class cruiser, was built in Sasebo. As the flagship of a combined destroyer division, the ship participated in the Battle of the Philippine Sea in June 1944, providing cover for the 1st Carrier Striking Force.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The cruiser took part in the Battle of Leyte Gulf, again leading a combined destroyer division. During an attack by American forces near Okinawa on April 7, 1945, Yahagi sustained numerous torpedo and bomb hits, lost propulsion, and sank.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC505_FULL"
msgstr "Yahagi"

msgid "IDS_PJSC505_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSC505_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PJSC506"
msgstr "Ōyodo"

msgid "IDS_PJSC506_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Under the 1939 Japanese fleet expansion program, it was planned to build two cruisers intended to serve as submarine squadron leaders. The requirements for the cruisers included powerful communication equipment and the capability to carry a group of high-speed seaplanes. The hull of such a ship was developed based on the Agano-class cruiser, and two main battery turrets were borrowed from the refitting of the Mogami-class cruisers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The cruiser Ōyodo was built in Kure. As part of the Third Fleet, the ship participated in the campaigns of 1943. Serving as the flagship of the Combined Fleet, Ōyodo took part in the Battle off Cape Engaño in October 1944. In the spring of 1945, the ship was damaged by American bombers in Kure and was sunk on July 28 in Etajima as a result of several near-miss bomb explosions.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC506_FULL"
msgstr "Ōyodo"

msgid "IDS_PJSC506_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC506_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PJSC507"
msgstr "Tokachi"

msgid "IDS_PJSC507_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The initial armament of the scout cruisers was planned to consist of six twin 140-mm gun turrets, but the project was later revised.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the 1930s, the Furutaka-class cruisers were rebuilt following the pattern of the Aoba-class cruisers, receiving three twin turrets instead of six single ones. Had this not occurred, during World War II, such cruisers would most likely have been converted into anti-aircraft ships, receiving twin 127-mm dual-purpose gun mounts instead of single 200-mm ones.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC507_FULL"
msgstr "Tokachi"

msgid "IDS_PJSC507_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC507_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PJSC509"
msgstr "Azuma B"

msgid "IDS_PJSC509_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Shortly before the start of World War II, Japan learned that the United States had begun designing large cruisers of the Alaska class. Almost immediately, Japanese designers began work on a ship of this class to counter the American cruisers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ships of the project were architecturally similar to the Yamato-class battleships and carried nine of the latest 310-mm guns in three turrets. Development was halted in November 1941 and finally canceled after the Battle of Midway.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC509_FULL"
msgstr "Azuma B"

msgid "IDS_PJSC509_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC509_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PJSC510"
msgstr "Azuma"

msgid "IDS_PJSC510_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Shortly before the start of World War II, Japan learned that the United States had begun designing large cruisers of the Alaska class. Almost immediately, Japanese designers began work on a ship of this class to counter the American cruisers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ships of the project were architecturally similar to the Yamato-class battleships and carried nine of the latest 310-mm guns in three turrets. Development was halted in November 1941 and finally canceled after the Battle of Midway.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC510_FULL"
msgstr "Azuma"

msgid "IDS_PJSC510_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC510_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PJSC512"
msgstr "Asama"

msgid "IDS_PJSC512_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1896, a fleet expansion program was adopted, which included the construction of four armored cruisers. A year later, the number of planned ships was increased to six. The first two cruisers—Asama and Tokiwa—were ordered from Britain. These ships were an improved version of the cruiser O'Higgins, built for Chile. During trials, the cruisers exceeded their design speed by one and a half knots. Both ships participated in the Russo-Japanese War and World War I.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Asama was laid down in Elswick in November 1896, launched in March 1898, and commissioned in March 1899. During the Russo-Japanese War, the cruiser was part of Admiral Kamimura's squadron, taking part in the battles of Chemulpo, the Yellow Sea, and Tsushima. In World War I, Asama was heavily damaged after running aground off the coast of Mexico in 1915.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, Asama served as a training ship for naval gunners in Shimonoseki.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC512_FULL"
msgstr "Asama"

msgid "IDS_PJSC512_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSC512_YEAR"
msgstr "1899"

msgid "IDS_PJSC517"
msgstr "Maya"

msgid "IDS_PJSC517_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Maya, a Takao-class cruiser, was built at the Kawasaki shipyard in Kobe. Upon commissioning, the ship was assigned to the 4th Cruiser Division. During the Second Sino-Japanese War, the cruiser escorted transport vessels.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, the Maya supported landings in the Philippines and the Aleutian Islands, and participated in the battles of the Eastern Solomons, the Santa Cruz Islands, and the Komandorski Islands in 1942–43.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1943, the ship underwent a refit during which it lost one of its main battery turrets and its aircraft hangar—these were replaced with anti-aircraft guns. In 1944, the cruiser fought in the Philippine Sea and in Leyte Gulf. The cruiser Maya was torpedoed by the submarine USS Dace on October 23, 1944, and subsequently sank rapidly.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC517_FULL"
msgstr "Maya"

msgid "IDS_PJSC517_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSC517_YEAR"
msgstr "1932"

msgid "IDS_PJSC519"
msgstr "AL Azuma"

msgid "IDS_PJSC519_DESCR"
msgstr ""
"Azuma is one of the most powerful cruisers of the Sakura Empire. She belongs to the so-called \"large cruiser\" type.\n"
"From the Azur Lane game."

