msgid "IDS_PJSC529"
msgstr "Chōkai"

msgid "IDS_PJSC529_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The fourth Takao-class cruiser was built in Nagasaki at the Mitsubishi shipyard. After the Fourth Fleet Incident, which revealed the structural weakness of Japanese warship hulls, Chōkai underwent modernization in Yokosuka in 1936–37.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In late 1941, the ship took part in the invasion of Malaya, and in 1942, in landings on Borneo, Sumatra, and Java. In April 1942, the cruiser participated in the Indian Ocean raid. In August 1942, Chōkai fought in the Battle of Savo Island, where, together with the 6th Cruiser Division, it sank four ships. In 1943, the cruiser underwent two modernizations in Yokosuka.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1944, Chōkai participated in the Battle of the Philippine Sea, and after a third modernization, it took part in the Battle of Leyte Gulf. On October 25, during that battle, it sustained several hits from aircraft bombs launched from the USS Kitkun Bay. The cruiser lost power and was engulfed in a severe fire that could not be contained. The crew abandoned ship, and Chōkai was finished off by torpedoes from the destroyer Fujinami.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC529_FULL"
msgstr "Chōkai"

msgid "IDS_PJSC529_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSC529_YEAR"
msgstr "1932"

msgid "IDS_PJSC530"
msgstr "Kuzuryū"

msgid "IDS_PJSC530_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the late 1930s, Japan developed a project for a cruiser designed to lead submarine flotillas. The ship was supposed to carry aircraft and have versatile main battery guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In one version of the project, a sizable hangar, similar to an aircraft carrier, was positioned between the bow and stern turret groups, with a full-fledged flight deck and island-type superstructure.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC530_FULL"
msgstr "Kuzuryū"

msgid "IDS_PJSC530_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC530_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PJSC540"
msgstr "Chikugo"

msgid "IDS_PJSC540_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The 1941 fleet replenishment program included four specialized AA cruisers to lead Akizuki-class destroyer flotillas. These \"Project 815\" ships were small cruisers with 5,800t standard displacement.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>An alternative considered converting Ōyodo-class cruisers or building new ones based on their hulls. The cruisers would carry twelve twin 100-mm/65 guns, matching three Akizuki-class destroyers' firepower. Enclosed mounts were planned amidships, semi-enclosed along sides.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC540_FULL"
msgstr "Chikugo"

msgid "IDS_PJSC540_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC540_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PJSC550"
msgstr "Kaimon"

msgid "IDS_PJSC550_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Shortly before World War II, Japan learned that the United States had begun designing large cruisers of the Alaska class. Almost immediately, Japanese shipbuilders began developing a ship that could counter the American cruisers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The project received the designation B-65, which, according to Japanese classification, allows it to be classified as a battlecruiser. In terms of architecture, the ship under development was similar to the Yamato-class battleships, and in one variant of the project, the armament consisted of 356-mm guns. The construction of the ships was canceled shortly after the Battle of Midway.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC550_FULL"
msgstr "Kaimon"

msgid "IDS_PJSC550_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSC550_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

