msgid "IDS_PJSC598"
msgstr "Atago B"

msgid "IDS_PJSC598_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Atago, a Takao-class cruiser, was built in Kure. Upon commissioning, the ship was assigned to the 4th Cruiser Division.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, it supported landings during the invasions of Malaya and the Philippines, participated in the Battle of the Java Sea, the Battle of the Eastern Solomons, the Battle of the Santa Cruz Islands, the Naval Battle of Guadalcanal, and the Battle of the Philippine Sea.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>From July 1944, the cruiser served as the flagship of Admiral Takeo Kurita's 1st Mobile Strike Force. Atago was sunk by the submarine USS Darter during the Battle of Leyte Gulf on October 23, 1944.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC598_FULL"
msgstr "Atago B"

msgid "IDS_PJSC598_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSC598_YEAR"
msgstr "1932"

msgid "IDS_PJSC610"
msgstr "Kitakami"

msgid "IDS_PJSC610_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Kitakami, a Kuma-class vessel, was built in Sasebo. With the outbreak of the Second Sino-Japanese War, the cruiser covered Japanese troop landing operations. In 1941, Kitakami was converted into a torpedo cruiser, receiving an armament of ten quadruple 610-mm torpedo tubes.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1941–42, Kitakami primarily performed convoy and training missions, after which it was converted into a high-speed transport. In this role, it escorted convoys to New Guinea, the Solomon Islands, and Truk.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1944, Kitakami sustained two torpedo hits from the British submarine HMS Templar. During repairs, the ship was converted into a carrier for Kaiten manned torpedoes.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In July 1945, the cruiser was damaged by bombers from Task Force 38 and subsequently used as a tender. Kitakami was decommissioned from the fleet in November 1945.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSC610_FULL"
msgstr "Kitakami"

msgid "IDS_PJSC610_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PJSC610_YEAR"
msgstr "1921"

