msgid "IDS_PJSD001"
msgstr "Tachibana"

msgid "IDS_PJSD001_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Sakura-class destroyers were designed based on the Umikaze-class ships. Unlike their predecessors, the new destroyers were equipped with steam engines rather than turbines.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Ships of this class became the first destroyers fully designed in Japan, as well as the most modern ships of this type in the Imperial Navy at the start of World War I. In the West, the Sakura-class destroyers became a symbol of the success of Japanese shipbuilding, leading France to order twelve Arabe-class destroyers from Japan.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Tachibana, a Sakura-class destroyer, was built in Maizuru. From 1919 to 1925, the ship was engaged in patrolling the Chinese coast. In April 1932, the destroyer was placed in reserve, and in 1936, it was finally decommissioned.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD001_FULL"
msgstr "Tachibana"

msgid "IDS_PJSD001_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSD001_YEAR"
msgstr "1912"

msgid "IDS_PJSD002"
msgstr "Umikaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD002_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The experience of the Russo-Japanese War showed that the existing types of destroyers were too small for operations on the open sea. For normal cooperation with the fleet, ships much larger than the 300-ton torpedo boat destroyers were required.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Immediately after the war ended, Japan began developing destroyers of increased size. The first were the two Umikaze-class destroyers, whose design was based on British ships of this class. The new destroyers were the first to use steam turbines.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Umikaze, was built in Maizuru. During the First World War, the destroyer participated in the hunt for Admiral Maximilian von Spee's squadron. From 1930, the obsolete Umikaze was used as a minesweeper.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD002_FULL"
msgstr "Umikaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD002_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSD002_YEAR"
msgstr "1911"

msgid "IDS_PJSD003"
msgstr "Isokaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD003_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Isokaze-class destroyers were built based on an improved design derived from the Umikaze class. The new destroyers featured increased engine power and were equipped with stronger armament. These ships were intended to escort the Ise and Fusō-class battleships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Isokaze, was built in Kure. The destroyer participated in World War I, patrolling the coast of China.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD003_FULL"
msgstr "Isokaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD003_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSD003_YEAR"
msgstr "1917"

msgid "IDS_PJSD004"
msgstr "Minekaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD004_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>To escort the Amagi-class battlecruisers, Japan built a series of large destroyers. Compared to their predecessors, the Minekaze class featured increased size, a greater number of guns, and the placement of the forward torpedo mount behind the forecastle break. These new ships became the first mass-produced Japanese destroyers equipped with geared turbines.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Minekaze, was built in Maizuru. Upon entering service, the ship was assigned to the 2nd Destroyer Division, which in the 1930s formed the escort for the aircraft carrier Akagi.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the Second Sino-Japanese War, Minekaze patrolled the Chinese coast. In World War II, the by-then obsolete ship participated in patrol and escort operations in the East China Sea and the western Pacific Ocean. The destroyer Minekaze was torpedoed and sunk by the American submarine USS Pogy off the coast of Taiwan on February 10, 1944.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD004_FULL"
msgstr "Minekaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD004_NUMBER"
msgstr "15"

msgid "IDS_PJSD004_YEAR"
msgstr "1920"

