msgid "IDS_PJSD012"
msgstr "Shimakaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD012_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>An experimental destroyer with an exceptionally powerful propulsion system, as well as enhanced anti-aircraft and torpedo armament. The planned performance improvements were to be achieved through new boilers with higher steam parameters, developed based on the experience of German designers. Shimakaze was intended to be the lead ship in a series of 16 destroyers, but ultimately remained the only ship of its class ever built.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The destroyer Shimakaze was built in Maizuru. During World War II, the ship participated in the evacuation of the Aleutian Islands, escorted convoys, and took part in the Battle of Leyte Gulf. During the Battle of Ormoc Bay on November 11, 1944, the destroyer Shimakaze was sunk by American carrier-based aircraft.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD012_FULL"
msgstr "Shimakaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD012_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSD012_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PJSD014"
msgstr "Tachibana L"

msgid "IDS_PJSD014_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Sakura-class destroyers were designed based on the Umikaze-class ships. Unlike their predecessors, the new destroyers were equipped with steam engines rather than turbines.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Ships of this class became the first destroyers fully designed in Japan, as well as the most modern ships of this type in the Imperial Navy at the start of World War I. In the West, the Sakura-class destroyers became a symbol of the success of Japanese shipbuilding, leading France to order twelve Arabe-class destroyers from Japan.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Tachibana, a Sakura-class destroyer, was built in Maizuru. From 1919 to 1925, the ship was engaged in patrolling the Chinese coast. In April 1932, the destroyer was placed in reserve, and in 1936, it was finally decommissioned.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD014_FULL"
msgstr "Tachibana Lima"

msgid "IDS_PJSD014_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PJSD014_YEAR"
msgstr "1912"

msgid "IDS_PJSD017"
msgstr "Fūjin"

msgid "IDS_PJSD017_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The second series of the Minekaze-class destroyers, also known as the Kamikaze class, differed from its predecessors in its draft, which was increased to improve seaworthiness. The lead ship of the series was built in Nagasaki at the Mitsubishi shipyard.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, the ship participated in the Aleutian Islands campaign and escorted convoys to the Kuril Islands. In January 1945, the destroyer was transferred to the Combined Fleet, after which it escorted the Ise-class battleships on their voyage from Singapore to Japan and then patrolled the Malay coast.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In October 1945, the ship was decommissioned from the fleet and used as a transport for demobilized Japanese soldiers. The destroyer ran aground off Cape Omaezaki on June 7, 1946, and was scrapped the following year.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD017_FULL"
msgstr "Fūjin"

msgid "IDS_PJSD017_NUMBER"
msgstr "9"

msgid "IDS_PJSD017_YEAR"
msgstr "1922"

msgid "IDS_PJSD024"
msgstr "Wakatake"

msgid "IDS_PJSD024_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the Japanese Navy of the 1910s, destroyers were divided into Class I and Class II. The latter were significantly smaller and cheaper, meaning they could be built in larger numbers. The last of these small destroyers were the Wakatake-class ships, built between 1921 and 1923. These ships were an enlarged version of the preceding Momi-class.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The destroyer Wakatake was built in Kobe at the Kawasaki shipyard. The ship participated in the Second Sino-Japanese War, patrolling the Chinese coast. During World War II, it served as an escort vessel. The ship was sunk during an Allied air raid on Palau on March 30, 1944.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD024_FULL"
msgstr "Wakatake"

msgid "IDS_PJSD024_NUMBER"
msgstr "8"

msgid "IDS_PJSD024_YEAR"
msgstr "1922"

msgid "IDS_PJSD025"
msgstr "Kamikaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD025_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The second series of the Minekaze-class destroyers, also known as the Kamikaze class, differed from its predecessors in its draft, which was increased to improve seaworthiness.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the series was built in Nagasaki at the Mitsubishi shipyard. In August 1928, the destroyer was named Kamikaze.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, the ship participated in the Aleutian Islands campaign and escorted convoys to the Kuril Islands. In January 1945, the destroyer was transferred to the Combined Fleet, after which it escorted the Ise-class battleships on their voyage from Singapore to Japan and then patrolled the Malay coast.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In October 1945, the ship was decommissioned and used as a transport for demobilized Japanese soldiers. The destroyer ran aground off Cape Omaezaki on June 7, 1946, and was scrapped the following year.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD025_FULL"
msgstr "Kamikaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD025_NUMBER"
msgstr "9"

msgid "IDS_PJSD025_YEAR"
msgstr "1922"

msgid "IDS_PJSD026"
msgstr "Kamikaze R"

msgid "IDS_PJSD026_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The second series of the Minekaze-class destroyers, also known as the Kamikaze class, differed from its predecessors in its draft, which was increased to improve seaworthiness.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the series was built in Nagasaki at the Mitsubishi shipyard. In August 1928, the destroyer was named Kamikaze.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, the ship participated in the Aleutian Islands campaign and escorted convoys to the Kuril Islands. In January 1945, the destroyer was transferred to the Combined Fleet, after which it escorted the Ise-class battleships on their voyage from Singapore to Japan and then patrolled the Malay coast.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In October 1945, the ship was decommissioned and used as a transport for demobilized Japanese soldiers. The destroyer ran aground off Cape Omaezaki on June 7, 1946, and was scrapped the following year.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD026_FULL"
msgstr "Kamikaze R"

msgid "IDS_PJSD026_NUMBER"
msgstr "9"

msgid "IDS_PJSD026_YEAR"
msgstr "1922"

msgid "IDS_PJSD105"
msgstr "Mutsuki"

msgid "IDS_PJSD105_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Mutsuki-class destroyers were built to an improved design based on the Kamikaze class. These new ships became the last type of Japanese destroyers with a torpedo launcher mounted forward of the bridge superstructure. Furthermore, the torpedo launchers became triple-tube mounts, which allowed their number to be reduced while maintaining the same number of torpedoes in a broadside salvo, and the torpedo caliber was increased to 610 mm.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Mutsuki, was built in Sasebo. The destroyer participated in the Second Sino-Japanese War and World War II. In December 1941, serving as the flagship of the 30th Destroyer Division, it took part in the unsuccessful landing on Wake Island, and in 1942—in landings on the Solomon Islands and New Guinea. The ship was sunk by American land-based aircraft as a result of a bomb hit near the Solomon Islands on August 24, 1942.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD105_FULL"
msgstr "Mutsuki"

msgid "IDS_PJSD105_NUMBER"
msgstr "12"

msgid "IDS_PJSD105_YEAR"
msgstr "1926"

msgid "IDS_PJSD106"
msgstr "Fubuki"

msgid "IDS_PJSD106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1922, Japan signed the Washington Naval Treaty, which limited the total tonnage of battleships and aircraft carriers. However, the restrictions did not apply to ships with a displacement of up to 10,000 tons.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In October 1922, the technical design for a destroyer with a displacement of 2000 tons was created. During its development, the displacement was reduced to 1900 tons, while the caliber of the main artillery was increased and a third torpedo launcher was added. As a result, the destroyers possessed outstanding specific combat power, but this came at the cost of significant construction overload and poor stability.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Fubuki, was built in Maizuru. During World War II, the destroyer covered Japanese landings in French Indochina, Malaya, and the Dutch East Indies, participated in the Battle of the Sunda Strait and the Battle of Midway. Fubuki was sunk by artillery fire from American cruisers and destroyers in the Battle of Cape Esperance on October 11, 1942.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD106_FULL"
msgstr "Fubuki"

msgid "IDS_PJSD106_NUMBER"
msgstr "10"

msgid "IDS_PJSD106_YEAR"
msgstr "1928"

msgid "IDS_PJSD107"
msgstr "Akatsuki"

msgid "IDS_PJSD107_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Akatsuki-class destroyer project was based on the Fubuki class, featuring a slightly shorter hull and reduced displacement. By increasing boiler steam production, it was possible to reduce their total number from four to three.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Akatsuki, was built in Sasebo. Upon commissioning, the destroyer was assigned to the 6th Destroyer Division. During World War II, the ship covered Japanese landings in Malaya, the Dutch East Indies, and the Philippines, and participated in the Solomon Islands campaign. The destroyer was involved in the \"Tokyo Express\" runs—reinforcement deliveries by light combat vessels. Akatsuki was sunk by naval gunfire from a U.S. Navy cruiser and destroyer task force in the Battle of Cape Esperance on October 13, 1942.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD107_FULL"
msgstr "Akatsuki"

msgid "IDS_PJSD107_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PJSD107_YEAR"
msgstr "1932"

msgid "IDS_PJSD108"
msgstr "Akizuki"

msgid "IDS_PJSD108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Akizuki-class destroyers were planned as specialized anti-aircraft ships for escorting fast carrier task forces. Their primary armament was to consist of eight state-of-the-art 100mm/65 caliber guns, around which the ship was essentially designed. The destroyers turned out to be significantly larger than their predecessors.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship, Akizuki, was built in Maizuru. During World War II, the destroyer participated in the Battle of the Eastern Solomons and the Guadalcanal Campaign, the Battle of the Philippine Sea, and the Battle of Leyte Gulf. During the Battle off Cape Engaño on October 25, 1944, Akizuki was sunk by an American torpedo.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD108_FULL"
msgstr "Akizuki"

msgid "IDS_PJSD108_NUMBER"
msgstr "13"

msgid "IDS_PJSD108_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PJSD111"
msgstr "Yamagiri"

msgid "IDS_PJSD111_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1939, Japan ordered an experimental destroyer based on an improved design of the Yūgumo class. The ship was equipped with enhanced torpedo armament and a powerful propulsion system that allowed it to achieve high speeds during trials.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The initial design proposed using six- or seven-tube torpedo launchers to create a powerful broadside salvo. However, the project had to be abandoned due to the excessive total weight of the torpedo launchers and their rapid reload systems.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD111_FULL"
msgstr "Yamagiri"

msgid "IDS_PJSD111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSD111_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PJSD206"
msgstr "Hatsuharu"

msgid "IDS_PJSD206_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A lighter version of the Fubuki-class destroyer, constructed with extensive use of welding and stronger grades of steel. The ship represented an attempt to create a destroyer that complied with the limitations of the London Naval Treaty while not being inferior to its predecessors in combat capabilities.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Hatsuharu-class destroyers initially had a classic turret arrangement but later underwent radical modernization. The ships' displacement increased and their speed decreased, but the risk of capsizing was reduced.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Hatsuharu, was built in Sasebo. The destroyer participated in World War II: it covered landings in the Dutch East Indies and took part in the Aleutian Islands campaign. In October 1942, the ship was damaged by an aerial bomb and spent a year under repair. In 1944, it participated in the Battle of Leyte Gulf. The destroyer Hatsuharu was sunk by American bombers in Manila Bay on November 13, 1944.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD206_FULL"
msgstr "Hatsuharu"

msgid "IDS_PJSD206_NUMBER"
msgstr "6"

msgid "IDS_PJSD206_YEAR"
msgstr "1933"

msgid "IDS_PJSD207"
msgstr "Shiratsuyu"

msgid "IDS_PJSD207_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Shiratsuyu-class destroyers were built to an improved design based on the Hatsuharu class. The new ships had an increased draft and were armed with two quadruple torpedo launchers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Shuratsuyu, was built in Sasebo. Upon commissioning, the destroyer joined the 27th Destroyer Division.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, the ship escorted convoys in the western Pacific, participated in the Battle of Midway, the campaigns in the Solomon and Marshall Islands, the First Naval Battle of Guadalcanal, and the Battle of Empress Augusta Bay. On June 14, 1944, near the Surigao Strait, Shiratsuyu collided with the tanker Seiyo Maru and sank due to the damage sustained.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD207_FULL"
msgstr "Shiratsuyu"

msgid "IDS_PJSD207_NUMBER"
msgstr "10"

msgid "IDS_PJSD207_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PJSD208"
msgstr "Kagerō"

msgid "IDS_PJSD208_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Japan's refusal to sign the Second London Naval Treaty in 1937 removed all tonnage restrictions for Japanese warships. Consequently, the Imperial Japanese Navy General Staff prepared requirements for a new destroyer: improved stability and increased range compared to preceding ships of this class while retaining their armament. Welding was extensively used in the construction.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Kagerō, was built in Maizuru. During World War II, the destroyer fought in the battles of the Eastern Solomons, the Santa Cruz Islands, Guadalcanal, and Tassafaronga. The destroyer Kagerō was damaged by a naval mine near Vila port in the Solomon Islands on May 7, 1943, was nearly immobilized, and was destroyed a short time later by aircraft.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD208_FULL"
msgstr "Kagerō"

msgid "IDS_PJSD208_NUMBER"
msgstr "19"

msgid "IDS_PJSD208_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PJSD209"
msgstr "Yūgumo"

msgid "IDS_PJSD209_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Yūgumo-class destroyers were built to an improved design based on the Kagerō class. They differed from their predecessors primarily in their new type of gun mount.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Yūgumo, was built in Maizuru. During World War II, the destroyer fought at the Battle of Midway, the Eastern Solomons, and the Santa Cruz Islands, and delivered reinforcements to Guadalcanal. In 1943, the ship was primarily engaged in troop evacuations — from the Aleutians to the Solomons. The destroyer Yūgumo was sunk by American destroyers near Vella Lavella Island on October 7, 1943.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD209_FULL"
msgstr "Yūgumo"

msgid "IDS_PJSD209_NUMBER"
msgstr "19"

msgid "IDS_PJSD209_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PJSD210"
msgstr "Harugumo"

msgid "IDS_PJSD210_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A variant of Japanese destroyer development aimed at enhancing combat capabilities based on the Akizuki-class ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The number of main battery turrets has been increased to five, and to maintain an acceptable speed, the main propulsion system from the experimental destroyer Shimakaze has been adopted. For the basis of the small-caliber anti-aircraft artillery, a 40-mm mount developed by Japanese engineers based on captured Bofors guns is used.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD210_FULL"
msgstr "Harugumo"

msgid "IDS_PJSD210_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PJSD210_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PJSD219"
msgstr "Kitakaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD219_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the experimental destroyer Shimakaze was laid down in 1941, intended to mark a new milestone in Japanese shipbuilding, 16 ships of this type were included in the fleet replenishment program. However, the heavy losses inflicted by American aviation in 1942 forced Japan to revise the program and replace some of these ships with Akizuki-class destroyers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In October 1943, seven ships based on an improved design of the Akizuki class were ordered. In May of the following year, their construction was canceled, as the fleet required mass-produced and inexpensive ships for convoy escort, not expensive and time-consuming destroyers. Subsequently, the resources intended for these destroyers were used in the construction of Tachibana-class escort destroyers and Kaibōkan — Japanese escort ships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD219_FULL"
msgstr "Kitakaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD219_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PJSD219_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PJSD503"
msgstr "Disabled: Isokaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD503_DESCR"
msgstr "Disabled: Isokaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD503_FULL"
msgstr "Disabled: Isokaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD503_NUMBER"
msgstr "Disabled: Isokaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD503_YEAR"
msgstr "Disabled: Isokaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD504"
msgstr "Disabled: Minekaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD504_DESCR"
msgstr "Disabled: Minekaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD504_FULL"
msgstr "Disabled: Minekaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD504_NUMBER"
msgstr "Disabled: Minekaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD504_YEAR"
msgstr "Disabled: Minekaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD507"
msgstr "Yūdachi"

msgid "IDS_PJSD507_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Yūdachi, a Shiratsuyu-class destroyer, was built in Sasebo. During World War II, the ship escorted troop transports during the invasions of the Philippines and the Dutch East Indies, and participated in the Battle of the Java Sea and the Battle of the Santa Cruz Islands. In September 1942, the destroyer began running supply runs on the route to Guadalcanal.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the Naval Battle of Guadalcanal, the ship lost power due to damage inflicted by the destroyer USS Sterett and was abandoned by its crew. The abandoned Yūdachi was finished off by gunfire from the cruiser USS Portland southeast of Savo Island.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD507_FULL"
msgstr "Yūdachi"

msgid "IDS_PJSD507_NUMBER"
msgstr "10"

msgid "IDS_PJSD507_YEAR"
msgstr "1937"

msgid "IDS_PJSD510"
msgstr "Hayate"

msgid "IDS_PJSD510_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A hypothetical project of a Shimakaze-class destroyer, armed with a twin 127-mm universal naval gun mount.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The new mount was developed towards the end of World War II; its key features were the use of unitary ammunition and an increased rate of fire. Equipped with such modern armament, this ship could have been significantly more effective in countering enemy aircraft at high altitudes and would have gained a firepower advantage in artillery duels with enemy light forces.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD510_FULL"
msgstr "Hayate"

msgid "IDS_PJSD510_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSD510_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PJSD512"
msgstr "Sazanami"

msgid "IDS_PJSD512_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Ikazuchi-class torpedo boats were built between 1897 and 1899 by the British firm Yarrow. The ships participated in the suppression of the Boxer Rebellion in China and then fought in the Russo-Japanese War.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Sazanami was laid down in June 1897, launched and commissioned in August 1899, and arrived in Japan in 1900. It participated in the Russo-Japanese War, engaging in battle with the Russian torpedo boat Steregushchy and capturing the severely wounded Admiral Z. Rozhestvensky, who was aboard the destroyer Bedovy during the Battle of Tsushima.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD512_FULL"
msgstr "Sazanami"

msgid "IDS_PJSD512_NUMBER"
msgstr "6"

msgid "IDS_PJSD512_YEAR"
msgstr "1899"

msgid "IDS_PJSD518"
msgstr "Asashio"

msgid "IDS_PJSD518_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Imperial Japanese Navy General Staff was dissatisfied that the Shiratsuyu class was inferior in speed and firepower to the Fubuki-class ships. As a result, the new Asashio-class destroyers were built with a design significantly altered from their predecessors. Meeting the new requirements was only possible if the displacement exceeded the limits of the London Naval Treaty, but by this time Japan had already privately abandoned compliance with them.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Asashio, was built in Sasebo. During World War II, the destroyer participated in the landings in the Philippines and Malaya, fought in the Badung Strait, and in the battles of Midway and Guadalcanal. The destroyer Asashio was sunk by American bombers in the Battle of the Bismarck Sea on March 3, 1943.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD518_FULL"
msgstr "Asashio"

msgid "IDS_PJSD518_NUMBER"
msgstr "10"

msgid "IDS_PJSD518_YEAR"
msgstr "1937"

msgid "IDS_PJSD528"
msgstr "HSF Harekaze II"

msgid "IDS_PJSD528_DESCR"
msgstr "Kagerō-class nautical training ship that was initially going to be named Okikaze. She is part of the reserve fleet in Yokosuka Girls' Marine High School dock and is currently equipped with components from the salvaged Harekaze."

msgid "IDS_PJSD528_FULL"
msgstr "HSF Harekaze II"

msgid "IDS_PJSD528_NUMBER"
msgstr "19"

msgid "IDS_PJSD528_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PJSD548"
msgstr "Mika"

msgid "IDS_PJSD548_DESCR"
msgstr "Once upon a time, a model-building contest was held at the 'Sovyonyok' camp. After long hours spent working on the drawings, the winners were rewarded with a trip to the beach, where a ship made from painfully familiar parts appeared..."

msgid "IDS_PJSD548_FULL"
msgstr "Mika"

msgid "IDS_PJSD548_NUMBER"
msgstr "30"

msgid "IDS_PJSD548_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PJSD596"
msgstr "Shinonome B"

msgid "IDS_PJSD596_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Shinonome, a Fubuki-class destroyer, was built in Sasebo. Upon commissioning, the ship was assigned to the Imperial Japanese Navy's 12th Destroyer Division. During the Second Sino-Japanese War, it patrolled the coast.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In World War II, the destroyer participated in the invasion of French Indochina, covered the Japanese troop landings at Kota Bharu in Malaya and the Borneo landings. Shinonome was sunk on December 17, 1941, off the coast of Sarawak by two bombs from a Dornier Do 24 flying boat, which detonated the aft magazines.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD596_FULL"
msgstr "Shinonome B"

msgid "IDS_PJSD596_NUMBER"
msgstr "10"

msgid "IDS_PJSD596_YEAR"
msgstr "1928"

msgid "IDS_PJSD598"
msgstr "Asashio B"

msgid "IDS_PJSD598_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Imperial Japanese Navy General Staff was dissatisfied that the Shiratsuyu class was inferior in speed and firepower to the Fubuki-class ships. As a result, the new Asashio-class destroyers were built with a design significantly altered from their predecessors. Meeting the new requirements was only possible if the displacement exceeded the limits of the London Naval Treaty, but by this time Japan had already privately abandoned compliance with them.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the class, Asashio, was built in Sasebo. During World War II, the destroyer participated in the landings in the Philippines and Malaya, fought in the Badung Strait, and in the battles of Midway and Guadalcanal. The destroyer Asashio was sunk by American bombers in the Battle of the Bismarck Sea on March 3, 1943.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD598_FULL"
msgstr "Asashio B"

msgid "IDS_PJSD598_NUMBER"
msgstr "10"

msgid "IDS_PJSD598_YEAR"
msgstr "1937"

msgid "IDS_PJSD610"
msgstr "Natsukaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD610_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The experimental destroyer Shimakaze, commissioned in 1943, was intended to be the lead ship of a new class. Under this project, 15 more destroyers were prepared for construction.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>If one of the planned ships had been built, it could have received 533-mm torpedo tubes with fast reload and high torpedo speed.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD610_FULL"
msgstr "Natsukaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD610_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSD610_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PJSD706"
msgstr "Shinonome"

msgid "IDS_PJSD706_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Shinonome, a Fubuki-class destroyer, was built in Sasebo. Upon commissioning, the ship was assigned to the Imperial Japanese Navy's 12th Destroyer Division. During the Second Sino-Japanese War, it patrolled the coast.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In World War II, the destroyer participated in the invasion of French Indochina, covered the Japanese troop landings at Kota Bharu in Malaya and the Borneo landings. Shinonome was sunk on December 17, 1941, off the coast of Sarawak by two bombs from a Dornier Do 24 flying boat, which detonated the aft magazines.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD706_FULL"
msgstr "Shinonome"

msgid "IDS_PJSD706_NUMBER"
msgstr "10"

msgid "IDS_PJSD706_YEAR"
msgstr "1928"

msgid "IDS_PJSD708"
msgstr "HSF Harekaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD708_DESCR"
msgstr ""
"Kagerō-class training ship of the Yokosuka Girls' Marine High School. She is operated by the protagonist of the High School Fleet anime—Akeno Misaki. In the anime, the ship has experimental modules installed that allow her to travel at higher speeds than her sister ships at the cost of stability, and her crew complement has been reduced thanks to automation, allowing a single class of 30 students to operate the ship.\n"
"From the series \"High School Fleet\"."

msgid "IDS_PJSD708_FULL"
msgstr "HSF Harekaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD708_NUMBER"
msgstr "18"

msgid "IDS_PJSD708_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PJSD718"
msgstr "AL Yukikaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD718_DESCR"
msgstr ""
"Famously known as the \"Fortune Ship\" for her achievements in the Great War. She's arrogant and believes that all her success is due to her ability. Gets upset when ignored and left alone. There is a rivalry between her and Shigure, although neither is sure why this is the case.\n"
"From the Azur Lane game."

msgid "IDS_PJSD718_FULL"
msgstr "AL Yukikaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD718_NUMBER"
msgstr "19"

msgid "IDS_PJSD718_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PJSD803"
msgstr "Isokaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD803_DESCR"
msgstr "Destroyer Isokaze, which is used in the Introductory Mission"

msgid "IDS_PJSD808"
msgstr "Akizuki CLR"

msgid "IDS_PJSD808_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Akizuki-class destroyers were planned as specialized anti-aircraft ships for escorting fast carrier task forces. Their primary armament was to consist of eight state-of-the-art 100mm/65 caliber guns, around which the ship was essentially designed. The destroyers turned out to be significantly larger than their predecessors.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship, Akizuki, was built in Maizuru. During World War II, the destroyer participated in the Battle of the Eastern Solomons and the Guadalcanal Campaign, the Battle of the Philippine Sea, and the Battle of Leyte Gulf. During the Battle off Cape Engaño on October 25, 1944, Akizuki was sunk by an American torpedo.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSD808_FULL"
msgstr "Akizuki CLR"

msgid "IDS_PJSD808_NUMBER"
msgstr "13"

msgid "IDS_PJSD808_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PJSD889"
msgstr "STAR Kitakaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD889_DESCR"
msgstr "A ship captured by a space crystal. Created by a celestial body to communicate with humans, Andromeda's avatar realizes that its behavior is becoming aggressive and tries to help humanity find out why the crystal came down to Earth."

msgid "IDS_PJSD889_FULL"
msgstr "STAR Kitakaze"

msgid "IDS_PJSD889_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PJSD889_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PJSD912"
msgstr "[Shimakaze]"

msgid "IDS_PJSD912_DESCR"
msgstr "This ship is a copy of Shimakaze and is suitable for Clan Battles only"

msgid "IDS_PJSD912_FULL"
msgstr "[Shimakaze]"

msgid "IDS_PJSD912_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSD912_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PJSS508"
msgstr "I-58"

msgid "IDS_PJSS508_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The I-58, a submarine of the Otsu-Gata B3 type, was built in Yokosuka. Even during its construction, it was converted to carry Kaitens—manned torpedoes.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War II, the I-58 participated in the attack on Guam, the raid on Okinawa, and hunted convoys south of Japan. In July 1945, the submarine sank the cruiser USS Indianapolis. After the war ended, in 1946, the I-58 was scuttled in the Goto Islands area.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSS508_FULL"
msgstr "I-58"

msgid "IDS_PJSS508_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PJSS508_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PJSS518"
msgstr "I-56"

msgid "IDS_PJSS518_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The third submarine of the Otsu-Gata B3 type was built in Yokosuka. I-56 participated in the defense of the Philippine Islands and the Battle of Leyte Gulf, during which it sustained damage.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1944, the submarine was converted to carry Kaitens — manned torpedoes. In April 1945, during the Battle of Okinawa, I-56 went missing. It is presumed to have been sunk by the escort of the American battleship Missouri.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSS518_FULL"
msgstr "I-56"

msgid "IDS_PJSS518_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PJSS518_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PJSS520"
msgstr "I-2"

msgid "IDS_PJSS520_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Within the Kai-Ko-Taka AM type, which was an improvement of the Ko-Gata A2 type, the construction of two series of submarines was planned.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The second series was a development of the first: the submarines were to carry not one, but two aircraft, and not reconnaissance planes, but attack aircraft. The cylindrical hangar was shifted to the side, giving the entire structure an unusual appearance. Such submarines were intended to be used for strikes against the locks of the Panama Canal.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>A total of four boats of this series were planned for construction, but they were never laid down because the attention of the Japanese naval command shifted towards aircraft carriers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSS520_FULL"
msgstr "I-2"

msgid "IDS_PJSS520_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PJSS520_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PJSS528"
msgstr "I-54"

msgid "IDS_PJSS528_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Otsu-Gata type submarine cruisers were designed for long-range reconnaissance and joint operations with surface and other submarine forces. Specifically for this purpose, they were equipped with a catapult and a hangar for one seaplane. The Kaidai 6B type was used as the basis for the project. Compared to other cruiser submarines, the Otsu-Gata type was distinguished by more streamlined forms.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Submarines of this type were built in several series. The third series of the type, Otsu-Gata B3, differed from its predecessors by having reduced engine power, which allowed for a significant increase in operational range. The number of spare torpedoes was slightly increased.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead submarine of the series, I-54, was built in Yokosuka. In the summer of 1944, the submarine participated in the Mariana Islands campaign, evacuating Japanese troops, and in the fall, it took part in the Philippine campaign. At the end of October 1944, during the Battle of Leyte Gulf, I-54 disappeared without a trace.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PJSS528_FULL"
msgstr "I-54"

msgid "IDS_PJSS528_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PJSS528_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

