msgid "IDS_PRSA104"
msgstr "Komsomolets"

msgid "IDS_PRSA104_DESCR"
msgstr "Training ship Ocean was designed based on a passenger liner and constructed at the German Howaldtswerke shipyard as a vessel for training naval machinists and fire feeders. After the Russo-Japanese war broke out, Ocean escorted the ships of the 2nd Pacific Squadron to the Mediterranean Sea as a supply vessel. The ship was renamed Komsomolets in 1922. In 1927, she was supposed to be rebuilt as a training aircraft carrier—all of her upper superstructures were intended to be removed, and a flight deck was to be rigged in their place. It was also planned to erect a double-deck hangar on her upper deck."

msgid "IDS_PRSA104_FULL"
msgstr "Komsomolets"

msgid "IDS_PRSA104_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSA104_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PRSA106"
msgstr "Serov"

msgid "IDS_PRSA106_DESCR"
msgstr "The preliminary design of Project 71 for an aircraft carrier with low displacement based on the hull and mechanisms of the Project 68 ship was developed in 1939; however, the ship's low hangar capacity and insufficient number of airplanes drew criticism. It was planned to increase dimensions of the hangar and increase the number of airplanes on board to 40–45 machines, all of which should have been fighters. A requirement was also formed to increase the number of dual-purpose guns to 12 and investigate the possibility of replacing them with eight 130 mm coupled mounts. Work continued in the form of a project for a heavy aircraft carrier codenamed 71B."

msgid "IDS_PRSA106_FULL"
msgstr "Serov"

msgid "IDS_PRSA106_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSA106_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSA108"
msgstr "Pobeda"

msgid "IDS_PRSA108_DESCR"
msgstr "In April 1945, the scientific and technical committee filed an offer to the Soviet Naval Shipbuilding Bureau to finalize the construction of Kronshtadt-class heavy cruisers under Project 69I as aircraft carriers because building the ships under the initial project had already become pointless. It was intended for the ship to carry 76 airplanes. The project was codenamed 69AV. The offer wasn't implemented because the completion ratio of the hulls and associated hardware was low."

msgid "IDS_PRSA108_FULL"
msgstr "Pobeda"

msgid "IDS_PRSA108_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSA108_YEAR"
msgstr "1950"

msgid "IDS_PRSA110"
msgstr "Nakhimov"

msgid "IDS_PRSA110_DESCR"
msgstr "After World War II ended, the Soviets were left with the hulls of unfinished Sovetsky Soyuz-class battleships of Project 23 and didn't know what to do with them, as building the ships under the initial project had already been deemed pointless. One possible solution was to finish the ships as aircraft carriers—the suggested project was codenamed 23AV. That solution could have been implemented in the U.S.S.R. between the end of the 1940s and the beginning of the 1950s. However, the top leadership of the U.S.S.R. didn't find the project compelling, so the hulls of the Project 23 battleships were eventually scrapped for metal."

msgid "IDS_PRSA110_FULL"
msgstr "Admiral Nakhimov"

msgid "IDS_PRSA110_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSA110_YEAR"
msgstr "1951"

msgid "IDS_PRSA111"
msgstr "Ulyanovsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSA111_DESCR"
msgstr "A hypothetical project of a strike aircraft carrier for the Soviet Navy, equipped with an angled flight deck and steam catapults. Designed for navigation in the waters of the Northern Arctic Ocean and for operations against enemy military bases and carrier groups. The carrier-based aircraft of such a carrier could use armament with a specialized warhead."

msgid "IDS_PRSA111_FULL"
msgstr "Ulyanovsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSA111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSA111_YEAR"
msgstr "1957"

msgid "IDS_PRSA508"
msgstr "Chkalov"

msgid "IDS_PRSA508_DESCR"
msgstr "The further development of the Project 71 aircraft carrier continued under the \"large aircraft carrier\" project, codenamed 71B. The ship would have made it possible for the main naval forces to utilize their aviation quickly and efficiently in the open seas and near the coastline in areas located far from naval bases, but the project wasn't implemented due to the outbreak of World War II."

msgid "IDS_PRSA508_FULL"
msgstr "Chkalov"

msgid "IDS_PRSA508_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSA508_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSA598"
msgstr "Chkalov B"

msgid "IDS_PRSA598_DESCR"
msgstr "The project of a high-speed aircraft carrier armed with dual-purpose main battery artillery developed in the U.S.S.R. in the late 1930s (Project 71B)."

msgid "IDS_PRSA598_FULL"
msgstr "Chkalov B"

msgid "IDS_PRSA598_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSA598_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSA990"
msgstr "Nakhimov Test"

msgid "IDS_PRSA990_DESCR"
msgstr "Ship for test."

msgid "IDS_PRSA990_FULL"
msgstr "Admiral Nakhimov Test"

msgid "IDS_PRSA990_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSA990_YEAR"
msgstr "1951"

msgid "IDS_PRSB001"
msgstr "Nikolai I"

msgid "IDS_PRSB001_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Before World War I, the Ottoman Empire ordered two battleships with 343 mm guns from Great Britain and planned to acquire one more. To prevent the Turkish fleet from gaining an advantage over the Russian Black Sea Fleet, in June 1914, a fourth Black Sea dreadnought was laid down in Nikolayev, named 'Emperor Nicholas I.'</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Initially, the ship was to be armed with 356 mm guns, but after Great Britain confiscated the Turkish battleships, the caliber was revised in favor of 305 mm to accelerate construction. The freed-up displacement was used to enhance armor protection, which ultimately was no worse than that of the under-construction battleships of the 'Izmail' class. In October 1916, the ship was launched, but by 1917, construction was halted due to the lack of machinery and equipment.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB001_FULL"
msgstr "Imperator Nikolai I"

msgid "IDS_PRSB001_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB001_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PRSB103"
msgstr "Knyaz Suvorov"

msgid "IDS_PRSB103_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the Russo-Japanese War, in which almost all battleships of the Russian fleet were lost, there was a need to build new ships of this class. By the spring of 1906, the characteristics of ships under construction for the British and American fleets—Dreadnought and Michigan—had become known, prompting discussions in the Admiralty about creating ships that would not be inferior in power to foreign models.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Following these discussions, the Marine Technical Committee began developing preliminary designs for a new ship. In April 1907, a program was adopted that envisioned the construction of four ships with a displacement of about 20,000 tons. One of the final project variants, dated September 1907, had the following characteristics: ten 305 mm guns in five twin turrets arranged in a linear-rhombic pattern, 14 secondary guns, 22,000 tons full displacement, a 203 mm main armor belt, and a speed of 22 knots.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB103_FULL"
msgstr "Knyaz Suvorov"

msgid "IDS_PRSB103_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB103_YEAR"
msgstr "1907"

msgid "IDS_PRSB104"
msgstr "Gangut"

msgid "IDS_PRSB104_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In December 1907, the Main Directorate of Shipbuilding and Supplies proposed that several Russian and foreign enterprises participate in a competition for the design of a new battleship. Among the projects submitted to the Naval Ministry by February 28, 1908, the best was recognized as the work of the Baltic Plant. At the end of 1908, the Naval Ministry received permission to build the ships, and after detailed refinement of the project, the new battleships were laid down in June 1909.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>'Gangut,' the lead ship in a series of four Baltic battleships, was built at the New Admiralty shipyard in St. Petersburg. During the winter of 1914-15, the ship spent time in Helsingfors, then began intensive combat training as part of the Baltic Fleet. However, 'Gangut's' participation in combat operations was limited to covering mine-laying operations—the command did not want to risk the new ships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB104_FULL"
msgstr "Gangut"

msgid "IDS_PRSB104_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PRSB104_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PRSB105"
msgstr "Pyotr Velikiy"

msgid "IDS_PRSB105_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>According to the naval armament development program drafted in the second half of 1907, four ships were to be built to 'cover the head of the enemy column.' The final requirements for the ship's characteristics were formed after senior officers of the Naval General Staff visited German shipyards and familiarized themselves with the battlecruisers being built there.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In August 1911, the Naval Ministry invited a number of Russian and foreign shipyards to participate in a competition for the best preliminary design of a new ship. One of the project variants, developed by the German firm Blohm & Voss, stood out for its layout: the artillery was arranged not in a longitudinal but in a linearly elevated scheme. The ship was to have a displacement of 30,378 tons, armament of eight 356 mm guns in four turrets, and 24 secondary guns. In design, the project resembled German battlecruisers of the time, but in terms of armor, it was close to the 'Gangut'-class battleships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB105_FULL"
msgstr "Pyotr Velikiy"

msgid "IDS_PRSB105_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB105_YEAR"
msgstr "1911"

msgid "IDS_PRSB106"
msgstr "Izmail"

msgid "IDS_PRSB106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>An international competition for the design of a combat ship for the Baltic Sea concluded in May 1912, but the Naval General Staff was impressed by some of the submitted sketches. The admirals were attracted by the prospect of increasing firepower with minor changes by adding another main battery turret. After determining the main characteristics, the Naval General Staff decided to merge the projects proposed by the Admiralty and Baltic Plants. The project was approved and sent for detailed working drawings in August 1912, and a month later, orders were issued for the construction of four ships. All four battlecruisers were laid down on December 6.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>'Ishmael' was built at the Baltic Plant and launched in June 1915. Further construction of the ship proceeded slowly, mainly due to the shipyards being occupied with urgent military orders, and during the Civil War, it was completely frozen. By that time, the hull's readiness was about 100%, and the machinery and artillery—about 50%. In the 1920s, several projects were proposed to complete the ship, including as an aircraft carrier.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB106_FULL"
msgstr "Izmail"

msgid "IDS_PRSB106_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PRSB106_YEAR"
msgstr "1912"

msgid "IDS_PRSB107"
msgstr "Sinop"

msgid "IDS_PRSB107_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the laying of the 'Ishmael'-class battlecruisers, the Naval General Staff began working on the next generation of battleships. During the research, the concept of the future ship was defined: it was to carry 406 mm guns and have a speed of at least 25 knots.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Due to the outbreak of World War I, plans to build such ships had to be postponed, and the design of new battleships was resumed only at the end of 1916, reflecting on the experience of recent naval battles. In October 1916, in Nikolayev, naval engineer V. P. Kostenko began designing the future battleship.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Four variants of the battleship were developed, differing in armament schemes, the number of main battery guns, speed, and armor protection. Variant No. 2 was a compromise in all respects: relatively high speed combined with good armor protection, the main battery consisted of nine guns in three turrets, and the secondary battery caliber was increased to 152 mm.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB107_FULL"
msgstr "Sinop"

msgid "IDS_PRSB107_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB107_YEAR"
msgstr "1917"

msgid "IDS_PRSB108"
msgstr "Vladivostok"

msgid "IDS_PRSB108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1935, against the backdrop of tense international relations, the shipbuilding policy of the USSR was adjusted in favor of constructing modern heavy ships. In February 1936, technical specifications were formed for the design of large combat ships, including a battleship for the Pacific Ocean. In June 1936, the final characteristics of the future battleship were determined: nine 406 mm guns in three linearly elevated turrets, twelve 152 mm guns in six turrets as secondary armament. Work on the project was carried out in parallel by KB-4 and TsKBS-1, and by October 1936, the preliminary designs were completed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In early 1937, information was received about battleships under construction in Germany and Japan, whose displacement, according to estimates, was about 50,000 tons. After this, KB-4 continued refining the project, increasing its protection and armament, which ultimately led to the appearance of the 'Soviet Union'-class battleships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB108_FULL"
msgstr "Vladivostok"

msgid "IDS_PRSB108_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB108_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PRSB109"
msgstr "Sov. Soyuz"

msgid "IDS_PRSB109_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the spring of 1937, the Soviet leadership learned about the start of construction of new German and Japanese battleships. Ships, each with a displacement estimated at at least 50,000 tons, appeared significantly stronger than the Soviet Project 23 battleship. In connection with this, the Shipbuilding Directorate proposed that design bureaus revise the project of the Soviet battleship, increasing its displacement to 55,000 tons—without regard to treaty limitations. In May 1938, after a series of refinements, the Defense Committee under the Council of People's Commissars decided to begin the construction of four ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The battleship 'Soviet Union' was laid down on July 15, 1938, at Plant No. 189 named after Ordzhonikidze. The outbreak of World War II and then the Great Patriotic War disrupted foreign orders for elements of the power plant and armor, and the blockade of Leningrad effectively froze the ship's construction at 21% overall readiness.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB109_FULL"
msgstr "Sovetsky Soyuz"

msgid "IDS_PRSB109_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PRSB109_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSB110"
msgstr "Kremlin"

msgid "IDS_PRSB110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The first drafts for the battleship project, numbered 24, began in January 1941. Initially, the ship repeated the original Project 23 but proposed an increase in anti-aircraft artillery and enhanced armor. In 1944, the requirements changed: universal artillery appeared in the project, replacing the secondary battery, and anti-torpedo protection and end protection were strengthened. In December 1945, the operational-technical specifications were approved, according to which the ship's primary purpose was to destroy enemy surface ships of all classes in naval battles, both near and far from its shores.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>According to the ship design plan from 1946, the development of the preliminary design was to begin in 1951, but work on the new battleship was already underway at TsKB-L since 1948. Among other things, variants with 457 mm main battery guns were considered. In 1950, the main variant of the pre-draft project was approved, but there was no place to build a ship with a displacement of 70,000 tons, and by that time, the country's leadership had lost interest in large surface ships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB110_FULL"
msgstr "Kremlin"

msgid "IDS_PRSB110_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB110_YEAR"
msgstr "1949"

msgid "IDS_PRSB111"
msgstr "Ushakov"

msgid "IDS_PRSB111_DESCR"
msgstr "Within the framework of the operational-tactical assignment for the design of the Project 24 battleship, issued by the Navy General Staff in 1945, one and a half dozen pre-draft projects were considered. The largest of them was a ship with a projected displacement of 110,000 tons, architecturally similar to the heavy cruisers of Project 82. It was planned to build the lead ship by 1955. Had this project been realized, such a ship would have become the largest and most powerful battleship in history."

msgid "IDS_PRSB111_FULL"
msgstr "Admiral Ushakov"

msgid "IDS_PRSB111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB111_YEAR"
msgstr "1949"

msgid "IDS_PRSB207"
msgstr "Senyavin"

msgid "IDS_PRSB207_DESCR"
msgstr "In 1921–22, several variants of a modernization project for the unfinished battlecruiser Kinburn of the Izmail class were developed. The ship's design was to be improved, taking into account the experience of the World War. The main difference from the original project would have been the installation of twin turrets with 406 mm guns, and some variants also suggested enhanced armor protection."

msgid "IDS_PRSB207_FULL"
msgstr "Admiral Senyavin"

msgid "IDS_PRSB207_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB207_YEAR"
msgstr "1922"

msgid "IDS_PRSB208"
msgstr "Istomin"

msgid "IDS_PRSB208_DESCR"
msgstr "While awaiting new major battleship construction programs, a series of projects were developed in 1914 through the joint efforts of Russian and German engineers. The ships shared characteristics with the Bayern class, which was being developed in Germany at that time. Project No. 4 featured 406 mm guns in triple turrets arranged along the centerline."

msgid "IDS_PRSB208_FULL"
msgstr "Admiral Istomin"

msgid "IDS_PRSB208_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB208_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PRSB209"
msgstr "Kornilov"

msgid "IDS_PRSB209_DESCR"
msgstr "In February 1914, the design bureau of the Putilov Shipyard, in cooperation with engineers from the German firm Blohm & Voss, developed a series of battleship projects in the style of the German school of design. The ships featured characteristics of the Bayern-class battleships being developed in Germany. Project No. 9 had three quadruple turrets with 406 mm guns arranged along the centerline."

msgid "IDS_PRSB209_FULL"
msgstr "Admiral Kornilov"

msgid "IDS_PRSB209_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB209_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PRSB210"
msgstr "Lazarev"

msgid "IDS_PRSB210_DESCR"
msgstr "In 1914, the Naval Ministry created recommendations for a new battleship, based on developments from the Main Directorate of Shipbuilding. Engineers from the Putilov Shipyard developed several versions of this project. Structurally, the battleship would be based on an enlarged hull of the Izmail-class. Variant No. VIII was armed with twelve 406 mm guns in three turrets arranged in a linear-elevated pattern."

msgid "IDS_PRSB210_FULL"
msgstr "Admiral Lazarev"

msgid "IDS_PRSB210_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB210_YEAR"
msgstr "1915"

msgid "IDS_PRSB502"
msgstr "Borodino '04"

msgid "IDS_PRSB502_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A series of ships built under an improved design based on the battleship 'Tsesarevich,' laid down in St. Petersburg in 1900–1902. The ships differed from the prototype in their more powerful power plants and heavier turrets, requiring designers to increase displacement while slightly reducing armor weight. The battleships took part in the voyage of the Second Pacific Squadron to the Far East and in the Battle of Tsushima.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Borodino was laid down at the New Admiralty Shipyards in May 1900, launched in September 1901, and commissioned in September 1904. Under the command of Captain 1st Rank P. Serebrennikov, the battleship made the voyage from Kronstadt to the Sea of Japan. During the Battle of Tsushima, the ship suffered heavy damage and sank.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB502_FULL"
msgstr "Borodino '04"

msgid "IDS_PRSB502_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PRSB502_YEAR"
msgstr "1904"

msgid "IDS_PRSB505"
msgstr "Okt. Revolutsiya"

msgid "IDS_PRSB505_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In October 1917, the battleship 'Gangut' was one of the first in the Baltic to raise the red flag, and in 1925, the ship received a new name—'October Revolution.'</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1931-34, the battleship underwent modernization, during which the old coal-fired boilers were replaced with oil-fired ones, a forecastle was added to improve seaworthiness, and the fire control system was upgraded. Another modernization, significantly enhancing anti-aircraft armament, was completed before the Great Patriotic War.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship participated in the defense of Leningrad, firing its main battery at advancing German troops and repelling enemy air raids. After the war, the former battleship underwent another modernization, was transferred to the training category in 1951, and decommissioned in 1956.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB505_FULL"
msgstr "Oktyabrskaya Revolutsiya"

msgid "IDS_PRSB505_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PRSB505_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PRSB508"
msgstr "Poltava"

msgid "IDS_PRSB508_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In May 1936, the Council of Labor and Defense adopted a resolution on the construction of 8 battleships and 18 heavy cruisers. The first four heavy cruisers with 305 mm guns were planned to be built in 1937-41. The technical specifications were approved on August 3, 1936, with the main battery caliber increased to 356 mm. The project was developed by TsKBS-1.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship's characteristics were constantly adjusted and revised, and further development continued under Project 69, which was worked on by TsKB-17. By the end of 1937, the ship's displacement had grown to 48,000 tons, closely approaching the battleships of Project 23. Ultimately, it was decided to abandon further development in favor of the 'Soviet Union'-class battleships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB508_FULL"
msgstr "Poltava"

msgid "IDS_PRSB508_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB508_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PRSB509"
msgstr "Navarin"

msgid "IDS_PRSB509_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1913, a design bureau was organized under the Main Directorate of Shipbuilding, headed by Major General I. G. Bubnov. In February 1914, the development of a battleship project began based on the technical specifications of the Naval General Staff.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The main requirement was the placement of 16-inch guns in quadruple turrets developed by the Technical Bureau at the Admiralty Plant. The secondary battery consisted of 130 mm guns from the Obukhov Plant, and the main battery turrets were arranged according to the 'Russian scheme': longitudinally along the centerline. Had it been realized, this project would have led to the appearance of the most powerful battleship at the time, but due to the outbreak of World War I, battleships under this project were not laid down.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB509_FULL"
msgstr "Navarin"

msgid "IDS_PRSB509_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB509_YEAR"
msgstr "1915"

msgid "IDS_PRSB510"
msgstr "Slava"

msgid "IDS_PRSB510_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A few years after the laying of Project 23 ships, the development of the next generation of battleships began. The new project, numbered 24, proposed an increase in the number of anti-aircraft guns and enhanced armor, and later universal artillery instead of the secondary battery. In December 1945, the technical specifications were approved, and in 1948, TsKB-L began work on the project. In 1950, one of the pre-draft variants of the project was accepted by the Ministry of Shipbuilding and the Main Directorate of Shipbuilding as the main one.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>For main battery artillery, the ship carried nine 406 mm B-52 guns in triple turrets. A weak point, as with all large projects of that time, was the anti-aircraft artillery: it was assumed that battleships would be covered by escort ships. Had Project 24 ships been completed and commissioned, modern ZIF-75 and 2M-3 anti-aircraft mounts would have been installed on them in the late 1950s.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB510_FULL"
msgstr "Slava"

msgid "IDS_PRSB510_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSB510_YEAR"
msgstr "1950"

msgid "IDS_PRSB515"
msgstr "P. Commune"

msgid "IDS_PRSB515_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A battleship built at the Baltic Shipyard and launched in June 1911 under the name 'Sevastopol', it became the first dreadnought of the Imperial Navy. It participated in World War I and the Civil War.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In March 1921, the ship was renamed 'Paris Commune'. In 1921–24, the battleship was repaired and subsequently entered active service in the Baltic Fleet. In 1929, as part of modernization, a bow extension was installed to improve its seakeeping and speed. Later that year, 'Paris Commune' was transferred to the Black Sea. In 1930–36, in Sevastopol, the battleship underwent extensive modernization with boiler replacement and upgrades to its anti-aircraft armament.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the Great Patriotic War, the ship participated in the defense of Odessa and Sevastopol. 'Paris Commune' supported the operations of the Crimea Front and the Feodosia landing.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB515_FULL"
msgstr "Paris Commune"

msgid "IDS_PRSB515_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PRSB515_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PRSB516"
msgstr "Novorossiysk"

msgid "IDS_PRSB516_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Italian battleship Giulio Cesare surrendered to Allied forces in 1943 and was transferred to the USSR as reparations after World War II. In the Soviet fleet, the ship was renamed 'Novorossiysk.'</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship was in unsatisfactory technical condition, and in the following years, extensive repair and modernization work was performed on it. Meanwhile, 'Novorossiysk' participated in maneuvers as the squadron flagship and performed combat training tasks.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In May 1955, the ship joined the Black Sea Fleet. On October 29, while the battleship was in Sevastopol Bay, an explosion occurred under its hull, and a few hours later, 'Novorossiysk' sank. 617 people died in the tragedy. The cause of the explosion was never determined, with the most popular theory being the detonation of a German mine from World War II.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB516_FULL"
msgstr "Novorossiysk"

msgid "IDS_PRSB516_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PRSB516_YEAR"
msgstr "1951"

msgid "IDS_PRSB518"
msgstr "Lenin"

msgid "IDS_PRSB518_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1935, the USSR leadership decided to build a 'large fleet,' and design bureaus began drafting sketches of battleships. In February 1936, technical specifications for design were formed. The battleship for the Baltic Sea (Project 21) was designed by KB-4 of the Baltic Plant based on characteristics issued by the head of naval forces: displacement of 35,000 tons, armament of nine 406 mm guns, and a 350 mm armor belt. By June, the preliminary sketch was ready.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In layout, the ship resembled the British battleship Nelson—with three main battery turrets in the bow. The universal caliber was abandoned in favor of separating into 152 mm secondary guns and 100 mm long-range anti-aircraft artillery. For torpedo protection, KB-4 adopted the modern Pugliese system. The developments for Project 21 battleships formed the basis of sketches, the evolution of which would lead to the appearance of the 'Soviet Union'-class battleships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB518_FULL"
msgstr "Lenin"

msgid "IDS_PRSB518_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB518_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PRSB519"
msgstr "Perm Velikaya"

msgid "IDS_PRSB519_DESCR"
msgstr "In the spring of 1937, the Soviet leadership learned that Germany and Japan were building new battleships with higher characteristics than the existing Soviet project. In connection with this, the design bureau was tasked with increasing the displacement and maximum speed of the future ship without regard to treaty limitations. The battleships of Project 23 (Sovetsky Soyuz class) were laid down in the USSR between 1938 and 1940. Construction was delayed, and in July 1941, it was halted by a decree of the State Defense Committee."

msgid "IDS_PRSB519_FULL"
msgstr "Perm Velikaya"

msgid "IDS_PRSB519_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB519_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PRSB520"
msgstr "Severomorsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSB520_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the 1930s, as part of strengthening the Soviet fleet and later within Stalin's 'large fleet' program, several large artillery ships were designed, including battleships. At the same time, American experience in building such ships was actively studied.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1938, a special commission established contacts with an American firm that undertook to develop a project based on Soviet specifications. As a result, a hybrid battleship-aircraft carrier project was developed, one variant of which proposed armament of twelve 406 mm guns in four turrets. It was planned to build two such ships—one in the USA and one in the USSR. Had the project been accepted, the ship built in the USSR would have received Soviet armament and aircraft.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB520_FULL"
msgstr "Severomorsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSB520_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB520_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PRSB526"
msgstr "Top Secret"

msgid "IDS_PRSB526_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>On October 28, 1943, the U.S. Navy conducted a secret experiment in Philadelphia called 'Project Rainbow,' in which the destroyer USS Eldridge supposedly teleported several kilometers away. Officials claim the Philadelphia Experiment is a hoax, but we know the TRUTH.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1947, after the Roswell incident, the government continued its experiments. Eyewitnesses (who remain anonymous for their safety) reported sightings of a Soviet battlecruiser off the Philadelphia coast, in Groom Lake, and near the Bermuda Triangle.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>We suspect the government used alien technology from Area 51 to uncover something unimaginable... and is hiding it from us.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Want to believe? It's hard to.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB526_FULL"
msgstr "Top Secret"

msgid "IDS_PRSB526_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB526_YEAR"
msgstr "???"

msgid "IDS_PRSB528"
msgstr "Borodino"

msgid "IDS_PRSB528_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>As part of the work on designing the Project 82 heavy cruiser, by order of the Ministry of Shipbuilding, TsNII-45 began developing sketches of a so-called small battleship. A feature of such a battleship's armor would be reinforced bow protection, ensuring defense against enemy fire at sharp angles.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In total, nine pre-draft projects of such a ship were developed in 1951-52. One of the variants recommended by TsNII-45 for further development carried armament of two triple turrets with 180 mm guns. At the end of 1952 and beginning of 1953, the set of materials for the small battleship was reviewed by the Main Directorate of Shipbuilding. However, soon the construction of heavy artillery ships ceased to be relevant, and the project was not realized.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB528_FULL"
msgstr "Borodino"

msgid "IDS_PRSB528_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB528_YEAR"
msgstr "1952"

msgid "IDS_PRSB529"
msgstr "Sov. Russia"

msgid "IDS_PRSB529_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>'Soviet Russia,' a Project 23 battleship (of the 'Soviet Union' class), was laid down in Molotovsk in July 1940. The ships were built for the Pacific and Northern fleets, and the largest covered slipway in the USSR was erected for their construction.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Construction proceeded with delays, and in July 1941, it was halted by a decree of the State Defense Committee. In October 1945, it was proposed to complete the battleship, but a special commission concluded that the pre-war battleship would be inferior in combat qualities to modern foreign ships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB529_FULL"
msgstr "Soviet Russia"

msgid "IDS_PRSB529_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PRSB529_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSB530"
msgstr "Krasnoye Znamya"

msgid "IDS_PRSB530_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the early 1940s, preliminary work on a new battleship began, based on Project 23, but with enhanced AA weaponry, improved armor and TDS (torpedo-defense systems), and better end protection. In December 1945, an operational and technical plan for the Project 24 battleship was approved, where the ship’s main objective was to engage all classes of surface ships at both close and distant ranges. Development of the new battleship started at CKB-L in 1948, and by 1950 one variant of the preliminary design was approved.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>If such a ship had been built, then, based on operational experience, the second hull might have been completed to an updated design. Its 130 mm universal guns in BL-110A mounts would have been replaced by 152 mm guns in BL-118 mounts. The ship was conceived as an answer to the U.S. Iowa-class battleships, and featured 457 mm B-45 main guns mounted in SM-8 turrets.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB530_FULL"
msgstr "Krasnoye Znamya"

msgid "IDS_PRSB530_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB530_YEAR"
msgstr "1953"

msgid "IDS_PRSB538"
msgstr "V. I. Lenin"

msgid "IDS_PRSB538_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1935, the USSR leadership decided to build a 'large fleet,' and design bureaus began drafting sketches of battleships. In February 1936, technical specifications for design were formed. The battleship for the Baltic Sea (Project 21) was designed by KB-4 of the Baltic Plant based on characteristics issued by the head of naval forces: displacement of 35,000 tons, armament of nine 406 mm guns, and a 350 mm armor belt. By June, the preliminary sketch was ready.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In layout, the ship resembled the British battleship Nelson—with three main battery turrets in the bow. The universal caliber was abandoned in favor of separating into 152 mm secondary guns and 100 mm long-range anti-aircraft artillery. For torpedo protection, KB-4 adopted the modern Pugliese system. The developments for Project 21 battleships formed the basis of sketches, the evolution of which would lead to the appearance of the 'Soviet Union'-class battleships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB538_FULL"
msgstr "V. I. Lenin"

msgid "IDS_PRSB538_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB538_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PRSB539"
msgstr "Ural"

msgid "IDS_PRSB539_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In February 1936, on assignment from the Directorate of Naval Forces of the Red Army, technical specifications were drawn up for the design of new heavy warships. The design of battleships was handled by Design Bureau No. 4 of the Baltic Shipyard.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The battleship project received the designation \"Type A battleship.\" In June, the characteristics of the future ship were finalized: 406 mm main caliber guns in three turrets with a superfiring arrangement, and 152 mm guns as secondary armament.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>If such a ship had been built, it would have undergone modernization after the Great Patriotic War. It is assumed that during the work, the anti-mine artillery and the aft main caliber turret would have been replaced with universal gun mounts, while the remaining main caliber guns would have been replaced with 55-caliber 305 mm guns.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB539_FULL"
msgstr "Ural"

msgid "IDS_PRSB539_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB539_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PRSB540"
msgstr "Strana Sovetov"

msgid "IDS_PRSB540_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In February 1914, against the backdrop of new naval programs, a series of designs for new Russian battleships were prepared. The projects were developed at the Putilov Plant with the participation of German specialists from Blohm & Voss and envisaged battleships with 356 mm and 406 mm artillery.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship built according to Variant 7 would have had four triple turrets with 406 mm guns—two in each end. The project featured a superfiring arrangement of artillery and mixed coal-oil fired boilers. The ships were too large for the Baltic and were conceived as the foundation for a future ocean-going fleet.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB540_FULL"
msgstr "Strana Sovetov"

msgid "IDS_PRSB540_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB540_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PRSB548"
msgstr "Frunze"

msgid "IDS_PRSB548_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The battleship 'Poltava' was severely damaged by fire in November 1919: the wiring was completely destroyed, and the boilers and power stations were damaged. With extremely limited funding, the decision was made to disarm and mothball the ship. In 1926, the battleship was renamed 'Frunze.' That same year, the ship's restoration was included in the Red Army's naval construction program.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Restoration projects were developed in 1928 and 1930. In 1931, a proposal was made to convert 'Frunze' into a three-turret battlecruiser using the original turrets. The following year, the Shipbuilding Directorate placed an order for the development of a preliminary design, and in 1933, the Baltic Shipyard Design Bureau prepared two variants, differing in smokestack configuration and anti-aircraft artillery layout. The battleship's modernization was included in the naval construction program for 1933–38. However, in the following year, the modernization was postponed and later canceled.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB548_FULL"
msgstr "Frunze"

msgid "IDS_PRSB548_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB548_YEAR"
msgstr "1933"

msgid "IDS_PRSB549"
msgstr "Gruzia"

msgid "IDS_PRSB549_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Beyond the gorge gates where castle towers stand like giant sentinels, lies an ancient land. There, emerald valleys slumber under golden clouds while eternal snow crowns the peaks. This land is harsh and proud, yet full of life like its ancient fortresses.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Beyond the wind-carved cliffs lies a land where legends live and sweet songs flow - ancient yet forever young.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB549_FULL"
msgstr "Gruzia"

msgid "IDS_PRSB549_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB549_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PRSB550"
msgstr "GDR"

msgid "IDS_PRSB550_DESCR"
msgstr "A ship for testing."

msgid "IDS_PRSB550_FULL"
msgstr "GDR"

msgid "IDS_PRSB550_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB550_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PRSB709"
msgstr "AL Sov. Rossiya"

msgid "IDS_PRSB709_DESCR"
msgstr ""
"Sovetskaya Rossiya is the pride of the Northern Parliament—one of the biggest and mightiest battleships at sea. She was designed and built during the Great War as part of the overall strategic plans.\n"
"From the Azur Lane game."

msgid "IDS_PRSB709_FULL"
msgstr "AL Sov. Rossiya"

msgid "IDS_PRSB709_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSB709_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSB710"
msgstr "Cyberslava"

msgid "IDS_PRSB710_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A warship created in the image and likeness of a mighty half-blooded hero possessing superhuman strength.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Incredible power and endurance helped Cyberslav become the most successful hunter of evil spirits. The hero hates his beastly nature and voluntarily agrees to wear a church collar that restrains the manifestation of his 'dark essence'.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>From the 'Cyberslav' series.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB710_FULL"
msgstr "Cyberslava"

msgid "IDS_PRSB710_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB710_YEAR"
msgstr "1950"

msgid "IDS_PRSB910"
msgstr "Nesokrushimy"

msgid "IDS_PRSB910_DESCR"
msgstr "A ship for the first battle."

msgid "IDS_PRSB910_FULL"
msgstr "Nesokrushimy"

msgid "IDS_PRSB910_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PRSB910_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PRSB999"
msgstr "Krasnoye Znamya (R)"

msgid "IDS_PRSB999_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the early 1940s, preliminary work on a new battleship began, based on Project 23, but with enhanced AA weaponry, improved armor and TDS (torpedo-defense systems), and better end protection. In December 1945, an operational and technical plan for the Project 24 battleship was approved, where the ship’s main objective was to engage all classes of surface ships at both close and distant ranges. Development of the new battleship started at CKB-L in 1948, and by 1950 one variant of the preliminary design was approved.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>If such a ship had been built, then, based on operational experience, the second hull might have been completed to an updated design. Its 130 mm universal guns in BL-110A mounts would have been replaced by 152 mm guns in BL-118 mounts. The ship was conceived as an answer to the U.S. Iowa-class battleships, and featured 457 mm B-45 main guns mounted in SM-8 turrets.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSB999_FULL"
msgstr "Krasnoye Znamya (R)"

msgid "IDS_PRSB999_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSB999_YEAR"
msgstr "1953"

msgid "IDS_PRSC001"
msgstr "Aurora"

msgid "IDS_PRSC001_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>'Aurora,' the third cruiser of the 'Diana' class, was built at the New Admiralty shipyard in St. Petersburg. In 1904-05, the ship moved to the Far East and participated in the Battle of Tsushima, where the cruiser's commander was killed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After the Russo-Japanese War, 'Aurora' returned to the Baltic. During World War I, it actively participated in naval combat operations, including the defense of the Gulf of Riga.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the October Revolution in 1917, after a blank shot from 'Aurora's' gun, the assault on the Winter Palace began. The cruiser's radio station transmitted the appeal 'To the Citizens of Russia!', written by V. I. Lenin.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the Great Patriotic War, 'Aurora' defended Kronstadt, and in September 1941, the ship was damaged by aerial bombing and grounded. In 1945, 'Aurora' was raised and converted first into a base for the Nakhimov School and then into a museum ship.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC001_FULL"
msgstr "Aurora"

msgid "IDS_PRSC001_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PRSC001_YEAR"
msgstr "1903"

msgid "IDS_PRSC002"
msgstr "Diana"

msgid "IDS_PRSC002_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A competition for the design of an armored cruiser—a commerce raider—was announced by the Marine Technical Committee in 1894. In 1896, the project of a 6,000-ton cruiser was approved, and in May 1897, three ships were laid down.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship, 'Diana,' was built on Galerny Island and launched in October 1899. At the end of 1902—early 1903, 'Diana' made the transition to the Far East, where it joined the Pacific Squadron. In July 1904, the cruiser participated in the Battle of the Yellow Sea, after which it broke through to Saigon, where it was interned. After the Russo-Japanese War, it underwent a major overhaul at the Baltic Plant.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War I, the cruiser participated in operations on enemy communications, served on patrol duty, covered the actions of the Baltic Fleet's light forces, and took part in the defense of the Gulf of Riga and the Battle of Moon Sound. In January 1918, it participated in the Ice Cruise to Kronstadt.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC002_FULL"
msgstr "Diana"

msgid "IDS_PRSC002_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PRSC002_YEAR"
msgstr "1901"

msgid "IDS_PRSC003"
msgstr "Murmansk"

msgid "IDS_PRSC003_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>USS Milwaukee (CL-5), the second of ten Omaha-class cruisers, was built in Tacoma, Washington. From 1928, the cruiser served in the U.S. Pacific Fleet and then in the Caribbean Patrol. After the U.S. entered World War II, the ship became the flagship of the 2nd Cruiser Division and operated in the Atlantic Ocean, escorting convoys and conducting patrol operations.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In April 1944, the cruiser was transferred to the USSR Navy, where it was renamed 'Murmansk.' Until the end of the war, the ship participated in convoy cover, and in March 1949, it was returned to the U.S.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC003_FULL"
msgstr "Murmansk"

msgid "IDS_PRSC003_NUMBER"
msgstr "10"

msgid "IDS_PRSC003_YEAR"
msgstr "1923"

msgid "IDS_PRSC010"
msgstr "Diana L"

msgid "IDS_PRSC010_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A competition for the design of an armored cruiser—a commerce raider—was announced by the Marine Technical Committee in 1894. In 1896, the project of a 6,000-ton cruiser was approved, and in May 1897, three ships were laid down.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship, 'Diana,' was built on Galerny Island and launched in October 1899. At the end of 1902—early 1903, 'Diana' made the transition to the Far East, where it joined the Pacific Squadron. In July 1904, the cruiser participated in the Battle of the Yellow Sea, after which it broke through to Saigon, where it was interned. After the Russo-Japanese War, it underwent a major overhaul at the Baltic Plant.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War I, the cruiser participated in operations on enemy communications, served on patrol duty, covered the actions of the Baltic Fleet's light forces, and took part in the defense of the Gulf of Riga and the Battle of Moon Sound. In January 1918, it participated in the Ice Cruise to Kronstadt.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC010_FULL"
msgstr "Diana Lima"

msgid "IDS_PRSC010_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PRSC010_YEAR"
msgstr "1901"

msgid "IDS_PRSC101"
msgstr "Orlan"

msgid "IDS_PRSC101_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In December 1941, TsKB-17 came forward with an initiative proposal for a project of a small mass-produced destroyer. It was assumed that such ships would be built at shipbuilding plants in the Volga basin and, upon completion, would be transferred to the North and Baltic via inland waterways.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In April 1943, the design bureau presented the Main Staff with a tactical-technical assignment developed by the bureau itself for the destroyer. According to the presented destroyer project, the caliber of artillery armament increased to 130 mm. However, the project did not receive further development, and in 1945, work on it was discontinued.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC101_FULL"
msgstr "Orlan"

msgid "IDS_PRSC101_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSC101_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PRSC102"
msgstr "Novik"

msgid "IDS_PRSC102_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A competition for the development of a small reconnaissance cruiser for the Pacific Fleet was announced in April 1898. Shipbuilding firms from various countries joined the competition for the right to build the ship—preference was given to the German shipyard Schichau. Construction began in March 1900 in Danzig, and in August of the same year, the ship was launched. The ship developed an extremely high speed for its time and class, for which it was maximally lightened.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>At the end of 1902—early 1903, the cruiser, under the command of Captain N. O. Essen, moved to the Far East, where it was included in the Pacific Squadron. The ship actively participated in the Russo-Japanese War from its very beginning. In the Battle of the Yellow Sea, 'Novik' received minor damage. The cruiser managed to break through to Qingdao, and to reach Vladivostok, it was decided to sail around Japan. 'Novik' withstood a battle with the Japanese cruiser Tsushima near the Korsakov post on Sakhalin, but the crew was forced to scuttle the ship in anticipation of Japanese reinforcements. In 1906, 'Novik' was raised by the Japanese and, after repairs, entered the Japanese fleet under the name Suzuya.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC102_FULL"
msgstr "Novik"

msgid "IDS_PRSC102_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC102_YEAR"
msgstr "1901"

msgid "IDS_PRSC103"
msgstr "Bogatyr"

msgid "IDS_PRSC103_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The project of a 'squadron reconnaissance cruiser' appeared in 1897-98. The ships, intended for the Pacific Squadron, were to surpass Japanese armored cruisers in speed and combat power.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>'Bogatyr' was laid down in Stettin in December 1899 and launched in January 1901. The cruiser joined the ranks of the Pacific Squadron and was included in the Vladivostok Cruiser Detachment. In May 1904, the ship was damaged on rocks in Amur Bay and stood in repair throughout the war. After the Russo-Japanese War, 'Bogatyr' was transferred to the Baltic Fleet, as part of which it actively participated in World War I. In 1918, it participated in the Ice Cruise of the fleet from Helsingfors.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC103_FULL"
msgstr "Bogatyr"

msgid "IDS_PRSC103_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PRSC103_YEAR"
msgstr "1902"

msgid "IDS_PRSC104"
msgstr "Svietlana"

msgid "IDS_PRSC104_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>During the Russo-Japanese War, Russian cruisers suffered heavy losses—mostly outdated ships remained in the fleets. The construction of cruisers that would meet all modern requirements was needed. Work on a new type of cruiser began in 1907, and the final technical specifications were issued in 1912. In the process, both domestic and foreign projects were considered, and it was ultimately decided to combine the efforts of the Revel and Putilov Plants.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>'Svetlana' was laid down in Revel at the end of 1913. Completion was planned for 1915-16, but World War I delayed these plans. During the war, 'Svetlana' was towed to Petrograd, as Revel was under threat of capture by the enemy. 'Svetlana' was launched in December 1915 and entered service in July 1928, receiving a new name—'Profintern.'</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC104_FULL"
msgstr "Svietlana"

msgid "IDS_PRSC104_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PRSC104_YEAR"
msgstr "1928"

msgid "IDS_PRSC105"
msgstr "Kirov (OLD)"

msgid "IDS_PRSC105_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After World War I and the Civil War, the Soviet fleet urgently needed cruisers, and ships of this class were included in the plans for the construction of the Navy for the second five-year plan. In the early 1930s, the possibility of building ships in other countries was considered, in particular, negotiations were held with the Italian firm Ansaldo. In 1932-33, TsKBS-1 developed a project for a light cruiser with Italian-produced machinery—as on the modern Italian cruiser Raimondo Montecuccoli. The project, numbered 26, was approved at the end of 1934.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>'Kirov' was built at Plant No. 189 named after Ordzhonikidze in Leningrad. It participated in the Soviet-Finnish War. During the Great Patriotic War, it took part in the defense of Tallinn and the Tallinn evacuation, as well as the defense of Leningrad. In the spring of 1942, the cruiser was heavily damaged by German air raids.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC105_FULL"
msgstr "Kirov (< 10.06.2020)"

msgid "IDS_PRSC105_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PRSC105_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PRSC106"
msgstr "Budyonny"

msgid "IDS_PRSC106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In February 1940, the government instructed the People's Commissariat of Defense and the People's Commissariat of Heavy Industry to begin designing a light cruiser. On June 8, the People's Commissariat of the Navy approved the operational-tactical assignment for the designed ship. The preliminary design was developed by OKB-196 at Shipbuilding Plant No. 196. The project received the number 94.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The main purpose of Project 94 cruisers was to support destroyers in attacks, reconnaissance, patrol, and raiding operations, protect and cover linear forces from enemy destroyers, and participate in mine-laying. According to the technical specifications, this required strong combat protection for all vital parts, advantageous placement of all types of artillery for close combat, proper high speed, and high survivability. In December 1940, OKB-196 presented two variants of the preliminary design to the NKVMF for consideration, one of which carried six 152 mm main battery guns. However, the outbreak of the Great Patriotic War prevented the realization of this project.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC106_FULL"
msgstr "Budyonny"

msgid "IDS_PRSC106_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSC106_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PRSC107"
msgstr "Shchors"

msgid "IDS_PRSC107_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In June 1936, the Soviet government approved a program for large-scale naval shipbuilding. It provided, among other things, for the construction of escort cruisers for the squadron, whose main task was to protect battleships from enemy light forces. The tactical-technical assignment for the new light cruiser was approved in August—the project received the number 28.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The preliminary design was prepared by the Leningrad Design Institute in June 1937. To unify, the new cruiser borrowed from its predecessor—Project 26—the general layout, silhouette, and hull lines, as well as the power plant. The project was never implemented due to changes in the leadership of the USSR Navy and the revision of the 'large fleet' program in the fall of 1937.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC107_FULL"
msgstr "Shchors"

msgid "IDS_PRSC107_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSC107_YEAR"
msgstr "1937"

msgid "IDS_PRSC108"
msgstr "Chapayev"

msgid "IDS_PRSC108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>At the beginning of August 1937, the command of the Soviet fleet presented an updated plan for the construction of combat ships, which included light cruisers of a new type. The project received the index 68, and its development was carried out by TsKB-17. A total of 26 Project 68 cruisers were planned to be built in 1938-47.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>'Chapayev' was laid down in October 1939 at Plant No. 189 named after Ordzhonikidze in Leningrad, and in April 1941, the cruiser was launched. With the beginning of the Great Patriotic War, the construction of the ships was suspended—the hulls were mothballed. After the war, the ship was completed according to the adjusted Project 68K and entered service in May 1950. It served as part of the Baltic Fleet.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC108_FULL"
msgstr "Chapayev"

msgid "IDS_PRSC108_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PRSC108_YEAR"
msgstr "1950"

msgid "IDS_PRSC109"
msgstr "Dm. Donskoi"

msgid "IDS_PRSC109_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1943, the Main Naval Staff issued a task for the design of the main light cruiser (Project 65), intended to accompany battleships and heavy cruisers. The hull of the Project 68-bis cruiser was taken as the basis. It was planned to transition to serial construction of these ships from 1948 and build 18 units by 1955.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1946, TsKB-17 and TsNIIVK Navy developed 40 variants of the project, including one with 180 mm main battery artillery. During the design process, the ship's displacement increased to 19,000 tons. Ultimately, it was decided to abandon further development of the project, focusing on the completion of Project 68-bis ships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC109_FULL"
msgstr "Dmitri Donskoi"

msgid "IDS_PRSC109_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSC109_YEAR"
msgstr "1951"

msgid "IDS_PRSC110"
msgstr "Moskva"

msgid "IDS_PRSC110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>During the development of Project 68-bis, the commander of the USSR Navy, Admiral N. G. Kuznetsov, proposed creating a cruiser project with 220 mm artillery. In December 1951, at a meeting of the top leadership, it was ordered to urgently develop cruisers capable of destroying American ships of the Des Moines class. The project received the number 66.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The development was carried out by TsKB-17, and the preliminary design was completed in March 1953. The construction of the lead ship was planned at Plant No. 194 in Leningrad. The ship was to be delivered in 1957, but after changes in the country's leadership, the development of Project 66 was discontinued.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC110_FULL"
msgstr "Moskva"

msgid "IDS_PRSC110_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSC110_YEAR"
msgstr "1953"

msgid "IDS_PRSC111"
msgstr "Novosibirsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSC111_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the mid-1930s, the USSR began designing large artillery ships for the future 'large fleet.' In February 1936, the Shipbuilding Department of the Navy Directorate formed a tactical-technical assignment for the heavy cruiser project. Engineers from TsKBS-1 prepared four variants of the preliminary design with armament ranging from 254 to 305 mm, but in the course of further development, these drafts led to the appearance of battleship projects 25 and 64.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The idea of building a heavy cruiser was revisited in the late 1930s—the new project received the index 69. Had construction begun in the second half of the 1930s, the ship would have been completed only after the end of the Great Patriotic War. Such a cruiser would have performed tasks such as convoy escort, support for landing operations, and would have been used as the flagship of an expeditionary squadron of the Mediterranean Fleet.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC111_FULL"
msgstr "Novosibirsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSC111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC111_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PRSC208"
msgstr "Tallinn"

msgid "IDS_PRSC208_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Before the start of the Great Patriotic War, the leadership of the USSR took steps to strengthen the naval forces in light of a possible clash with Germany. Among other things, the USSR purchased ships, naval armament, and equipment from Germany itself. In 1940, the unfinished heavy cruiser Lützow was acquired, which was later renamed 'Petropavlovsk.'</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The unfinished ship participated in the defense of Leningrad, firing from its four mounted guns. In September 1941, due to damage received, the cruiser grounded. In the summer of 1942, the ship was raised and towed down the Neva to Plant No. 189 named after Ordzhonikidze. The repaired ship continued to be used as a floating battery in the Leningrad defense system. Later, the ship was renamed 'Tallinn,' and after the war, there were plans to complete it using the mechanisms of the cruiser Seydlitz and 180 mm artillery.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC208_FULL"
msgstr "Tallinn"

msgid "IDS_PRSC208_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC208_YEAR"
msgstr "1947"

msgid "IDS_PRSC209"
msgstr "Riga"

msgid "IDS_PRSC209_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After Soviet specialists studied the technical documentation of the cruiser Lützow, purchased from Germany, the question arose of creating more advanced ships based on the German project. On the instructions of the People's Commissar of the Navy, Admiral N. G. Kuznetsov, the Main Naval Staff prepared a preliminary operational-tactical assignment for the design of such a cruiser, which received the project index 82.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The operational-tactical assignment was repeatedly adjusted, and the development was initially carried out by TsKB-17, and then by TsKB-16. The technical project was approved in February 1951, and in December of the same year, the cruiser 'Stalingrad' was laid down at Plant No. 444 in Nikolaev. The launch was planned for 1953, but after changes in the country's leadership, the construction of ships of this project was canceled.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC209_FULL"
msgstr "Riga"

msgid "IDS_PRSC209_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC209_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PRSC210"
msgstr "A. Nevsky"

msgid "IDS_PRSC210_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In February 1955, the operational-tactical assignment for the design of a light cruiser with universal 180 mm main battery artillery was approved. The project received the number 84. Such a cruiser would have been tasked with anti-aircraft artillery cover for ship formations at sea.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The preliminary design was to be ready by the second quarter of 1958, and the technical design—by the first quarter of 1959. The ship was to receive the most modern ZIF-75 anti-aircraft mounts. The lead ship was scheduled to enter service in 1960, and by 1964, a total of five such ships were to be built. In 1955, all work on Project 84 was stopped.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC210_FULL"
msgstr "Alexander Nevsky"

msgid "IDS_PRSC210_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC210_YEAR"
msgstr "1955"

msgid "IDS_PRSC215"
msgstr "Kotovsky"

msgid "IDS_PRSC215_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the early 1930s, the USSR, as part of rebuilding its fleet, began developing contacts with Italy within the framework of naval cooperation. In April 1932, the first operational-tactical requirement for a light cruiser project was approved. In the summer of the same year, a Soviet commission was sent to Italy, tasked with familiarizing itself with the Italian shipbuilding industry, selecting a prototype for the Soviet cruiser, and purchasing a boiler-turbine power plant with a capacity of 100–120 thousand hp.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The choice fell on the cruiser Raimondo Montecuccoli, and the commission proposed purchasing the theoretical blueprint and power plant of this ship. In March 1933, a revised version of the operational-technical requirement was ready, and in April, the preliminary design of the 'cruiser with a displacement of 6,630 t' was approved, one variant of which envisaged armament of eight 152 mm guns. During further development, Project 26 cruisers emerged.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC215_FULL"
msgstr "Kotovsky"

msgid "IDS_PRSC215_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC215_YEAR"
msgstr "1933"

msgid "IDS_PRSC310"
msgstr "Petropavlovsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSC310_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Further development of the operational-tactical assignment for Project 82 cruiser led to the appearance of new editions of the operational-tactical assignment, which was repeatedly adjusted in 1944-46.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In August 1947, the Ministry of Shipbuilding and the Navy presented their sketches, and later both sketches were merged into one—with 220 mm main battery artillery and balanced speed and displacement characteristics. A larger caliber would have been excessive, given that one of the ship's main tasks was to fight American heavy cruisers of the Des Moines class. Ultimately, another variant was chosen, the development of which led to the appearance of the 'Stalingrad'-class heavy cruisers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC310_FULL"
msgstr "Petropavlovsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSC310_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC310_YEAR"
msgstr "1947"

msgid "IDS_PRSC503"
msgstr "Oleg"

msgid "IDS_PRSC503_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>'Oleg' became the fourth and last armored cruiser of the 'Bogatyr' class. During the Russo-Japanese War, the ship joined the Second Pacific Squadron, as part of which it moved to the Far East and participated in the Battle of Tsushima. 'Oleg' suffered heavy damage and, together with other cruisers, was interned in Manila, returning to the Baltic after the war.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During World War I, the ship participated in the operations of the Baltic Fleet's cruiser detachment. The cruiser 'Oleg' was sunk on the night of June 17-18, 1919, in the port of Kronstadt by a British torpedo boat.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC503_FULL"
msgstr "Oleg"

msgid "IDS_PRSC503_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PRSC503_YEAR"
msgstr "1904"

msgid "IDS_PRSC505"
msgstr "Krasny Krym"

msgid "IDS_PRSC505_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The cruiser 'Svetlana' was completed and entered service in 1928 under the name 'Profintern,' and in 1939, the ship was renamed again—to 'Red Crimea.'</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the Great Patriotic War, the cruiser participated in the defense of Odessa and Sevastopol, the Kerch and Sudak landing operations, and the evacuation of Novorossiysk. For its valor, the cruiser was awarded the honorary title of Guards Ship.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1949, 'Red Crimea' transferred to the training detachment and was later withdrawn from combat service and became a training cruiser. In subsequent years, the ship served as an experimental vessel and a floating barracks, and in 1959, it was finally removed from the fleet list.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC505_FULL"
msgstr "Krasny Krym"

msgid "IDS_PRSC505_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PRSC505_YEAR"
msgstr "1928"

msgid "IDS_PRSC506"
msgstr "Molotov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC506_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1937, the leadership of the USSR Navy adjusted Project 26 cruiser. The improved Project 26-bis received a modified superstructure, enhanced armor, and a new fire control system. The changes were also implemented on ships already under construction.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The cruiser 'Molotov' was laid down in January 1937 and built in Nikolaev. In the first months of the Great Patriotic War, the ship participated in repelling air attacks on Sevastopol, then took part in the Kerch landing operation and the defense of Sevastopol. In August 1942, 'Molotov' was hit by a torpedo from a German aircraft—the explosion tore off the stern along with the steering compartment and rudder. 'Molotov' was towed to Poti, where it was repaired using a floating dock, utilizing the stern from an unfinished cruiser. After the war, the ship was part of the Black Sea Fleet.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC506_FULL"
msgstr "Molotov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC506_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PRSC506_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PRSC508"
msgstr "Kutuzov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC508_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Project 68-bis cruiser 'Mikhail Kutuzov' was built in Nikolaev. The ship served as part of the 50th Cruiser Division of the Black Sea Fleet, based in Sevastopol. In 1955, the first Soviet shipborne helicopter, the Ka-15, was tested on the cruiser.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1967-68, 'Mikhail Kutuzov' performed a combat mission in the Mediterranean during the Arab-Israeli conflict, providing assistance to the armed forces of Egypt and Syria. In 1969, the cruiser became the flagship of the 30th Division of the Black Sea Fleet. In 1979-86, the ship underwent modernization under Project 68A, but the following year, it was transferred to the reserve. It was decommissioned in 1992 and later converted into a museum ship in Novorossiysk.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC508_FULL"
msgstr "Mikhail Kutuzov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC508_NUMBER"
msgstr "21"

msgid "IDS_PRSC508_YEAR"
msgstr "1954"

msgid "IDS_PRSC509"
msgstr "Kronshtadt"

msgid "IDS_PRSC509_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In August, the Defense Committee under the Council of People's Commissars decided to build heavy cruisers with 254 mm main battery artillery instead of Type B battleships. In September 1937, the commission developed a tactical-technical assignment for the new ship. The development of the preliminary design, which received the index 69, was carried out by TsKB-17. In connection with the construction of ships of the Scharnhorst class in Germany, the cruiser's main caliber was revised in favor of increasing to 305 mm. In 1938, the project was adjusted again with enhanced armor, and in January 1939, the preliminary design was presented to the Defense Committee.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In November 1939, two heavy cruisers were laid down—in Leningrad and Nikolaev. But the domestic industry was loaded with military orders and could not cope with the supply of armament and equipment for the ships, and with the beginning of the Great Patriotic War, construction was completely stopped. After the end of the war, there were projects to complete the heavy cruisers as aircraft carriers, but these plans were not realized.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC509_FULL"
msgstr "Kronshtadt"

msgid "IDS_PRSC509_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PRSC509_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSC510"
msgstr "Stalingrad"

msgid "IDS_PRSC510_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After Soviet specialists studied the technical documentation of the cruiser Lützow, purchased from Germany, the question arose of creating more advanced ships based on the German project. On the instructions of the People's Commissar of the Navy, Admiral N. G. Kuznetsov, the Main Naval Staff prepared a preliminary operational-tactical assignment for the design of such a cruiser, which received the project index 82.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The operational-tactical assignment was repeatedly adjusted, and the development was initially carried out by TsKB-17, and then by TsKB-16. The technical project was approved in February 1951, and in December of the same year, the cruiser 'Stalingrad' was laid down at Plant No. 444 in Nikolaev. The launch was planned for 1953, but after changes in the country's leadership, the construction of ships of this project was canceled.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC510_FULL"
msgstr "Stalingrad"

msgid "IDS_PRSC510_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PRSC510_YEAR"
msgstr "1951"

msgid "IDS_PRSC512"
msgstr "Gromoboy"

msgid "IDS_PRSC512_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Gromoboy was a further development of the armored cruisers—commerce raiders 'Rurik' and 'Rossiya,' featuring a completely new power plant with three identical triple-expansion steam engines and improved armor.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The cruiser was laid down at the Baltic Shipyard in May 1898, launched a year later, and commissioned in November 1899. Immediately after acceptance trials, the ship was transferred to the Far East, where it joined the Pacific Squadron.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the Russo-Japanese War, Gromoboy was part of the Vladivostok Cruiser Squadron. It took part in the Battle of the Korea Strait in August 1904. In October 1904, the ship was damaged in Posyet Bay. After repairs were completed in May 1905, it struck a mine and remained under repair until the end of the war. In 1906, the cruiser returned to the Baltic. It participated in World War I and remained in Kronstadt from the end of 1917.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC512_FULL"
msgstr "Gromoboy"

msgid "IDS_PRSC512_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC512_YEAR"
msgstr "1899"

msgid "IDS_PRSC513"
msgstr "Varyag"

msgid "IDS_PRSC513_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A cruiser built under the program of squadron reconnaissance cruisers for the Pacific Fleet in Philadelphia. After commissioning, it was transferred to the Far East, where it was used as a station ship in Chemulpo.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>At the beginning of the Russo-Japanese War, the enemy blockaded Chemulpo and demanded that the cruiser leave the port. 'Varyag' and the accompanying gunboat 'Koreets' attempted a breakthrough—they were met by six Japanese cruisers. After an hour-long battle, the heavily damaged cruiser retreated back to the port, where it was decided to scuttle the ships. In August 1905, the Japanese raised the cruiser, which after repairs entered the Japanese fleet under the name Soya.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1916, Russia, to strengthen the newly formed Arctic Ocean Flotilla, bought back its old ships captured during the war from Japan. At the beginning of 1917, 'Varyag,' having regained its old name, moved to Great Britain for repairs. After the October Revolution, the Soviet government refused to recognize the debts of Tsarist Russia, and Great Britain confiscated the cruiser.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC513_FULL"
msgstr "Varyag"

msgid "IDS_PRSC513_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC513_YEAR"
msgstr "1901"

msgid "IDS_PRSC515"
msgstr "Mikoyan"

msgid "IDS_PRSC515_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In April 1932, the first operational-technical assignment for a light cruiser was approved in the USSR. In the summer of the same year, a Soviet commission was sent to Italy to familiarize itself with the Italian shipbuilding industry, select a prototype for the new Soviet cruiser, and purchase a boiler-turbine power plant.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In March 1933, a revised version of the operational-technical assignment was approved, and in April, the preliminary design of the cruiser was ready. The first variant of the project proposed armament of six 180 mm guns. In the course of further development and evolution of this project, Project 26 cruisers appeared.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC515_FULL"
msgstr "Mikoyan"

msgid "IDS_PRSC515_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC515_YEAR"
msgstr "1933"

msgid "IDS_PRSC516"
msgstr "Kerch"

msgid "IDS_PRSC516_DESCR"
msgstr "The Italian cruiser Emanuele Filiberto Duca d’Aosta was transferred to the USSR in 1949 under the provisional name Z-11 as part of reparations. In the Soviet fleet, the ship was first renamed 'Stalingrad' and later 'Kerch'. The cruiser was modernized at the Sevastopol Marine Plant named after S. Ordzhonikidze, receiving Soviet equipment. The ship was primarily used for training purposes."

msgid "IDS_PRSC516_FULL"
msgstr "Kerch"

msgid "IDS_PRSC516_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PRSC516_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PRSC518"
msgstr "Lazo"

msgid "IDS_PRSC518_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>At the beginning of August 1937, the command of the Soviet fleet presented an updated plan for the construction of combat ships, which included light cruisers of a new type. The project received the index 68, and its development was carried out by TsKB-17. A total of 26 Project 68 cruisers were planned to be built in 1938-47.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Due to the outbreak of the Great Patriotic War, none of the seven ships laid down by that time were completed. Two cruisers under construction in Nikolaev were blown up by the Germans during their retreat, and the remaining ships were completed according to the adjusted Project 68K.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC518_FULL"
msgstr "Lazo"

msgid "IDS_PRSC518_NUMBER"
msgstr "7"

msgid "IDS_PRSC518_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSC519"
msgstr "Shcherbakov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC519_DESCR"
msgstr "In 1954, a project was approved to rearm the 68-bis cruisers, which were still on the slipways, with new quadruple 45-mm mounts. It was also planned to install 25-mm mounts, improve electronic and radar equipment, and increase the hull's strength. The first in line for completion was 'Shcherbakov', laid down in Leningrad in 1951 and launched in March 1954. However, in the latter half of the 1950s, the leadership changed its view on the role of large artillery ships, and the cruisers of the improved project were not completed."

msgid "IDS_PRSC519_FULL"
msgstr "Shcherbakov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC519_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC519_YEAR"
msgstr "1954"

msgid "IDS_PRSC520"
msgstr "STALINGRAD #2"

msgid "IDS_PRSC520_DESCR"
msgstr "One of the few heavy artillery ships developed after the end of World War II. She had a very high speed for her size and her armament consisted of specially designed 305 mm guns, unrivaled in terms of her maximum firing range and ballistics."

msgid "IDS_PRSC520_FULL"
msgstr "STALINGRAD #2"

msgid "IDS_PRSC520_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PRSC520_YEAR"
msgstr "1951"

msgid "IDS_PRSC523"
msgstr "AL Avrora"

msgid "IDS_PRSC523_DESCR"
msgstr ""
"Avrora is a light Pallada-class cruiser and one of the oldest ships of the Northern Parliament. Her name means \"dawn.\"\n"
"From the Azur Lane game."

msgid "IDS_PRSC523_FULL"
msgstr "AL Avrora"

msgid "IDS_PRSC523_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PRSC523_YEAR"
msgstr "1903"

msgid "IDS_PRSC525"
msgstr "Kirov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC525_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After World War I and the Civil War, the Soviet fleet urgently needed cruisers, and ships of this class were included in the plans for the construction of the Navy for the second five-year plan. In the early 1930s, the possibility of building ships in other countries was considered, in particular, negotiations were held with the Italian firm Ansaldo. In 1932-33, TsKBS-1 developed a project for a light cruiser with Italian-produced machinery—as on the modern Italian cruiser Raimondo Montecuccoli. The project, numbered 26, was approved at the end of 1934.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>'Kirov' was built at Plant No. 189 named after Ordzhonikidze in Leningrad. It participated in the Soviet-Finnish War. During the Great Patriotic War, it took part in the defense of Tallinn and the Tallinn evacuation, as well as the defense of Leningrad. In the spring of 1942, the cruiser was heavily damaged by German air raids.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC525_FULL"
msgstr "Kirov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC525_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PRSC525_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PRSC526"
msgstr "Molotov B"

msgid "IDS_PRSC526_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1937, the leadership of the USSR Navy adjusted Project 26 cruiser. The improved Project 26-bis received a modified superstructure, enhanced armor, and a new fire control system. The changes were also implemented on ships already under construction.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The cruiser 'Molotov' was laid down in January 1937 and built in Nikolaev. In the first months of the Great Patriotic War, the ship participated in repelling air attacks on Sevastopol, then took part in the Kerch landing operation and the defense of Sevastopol. In August 1942, 'Molotov' was hit by a torpedo from a German aircraft—the explosion tore off the stern along with the steering compartment and rudder. 'Molotov' was towed to Poti, where it was repaired using a floating dock, utilizing the stern from an unfinished cruiser. After the war, the ship was part of the Black Sea Fleet.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC526_FULL"
msgstr "Molotov B"

msgid "IDS_PRSC526_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PRSC526_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PRSC528"
msgstr "Ochakov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC528_DESCR"
msgstr "In 1950, three design bureaus of the 4th Special Department of the USSR Ministry of Internal Affairs developed preliminary designs for several light cruisers. One of them was armed with four twin BL-115 turrets. The designers managed to achieve more efficient use of displacement for the placement of main battery artillery compared to ships of Projects 26-bis and 68K. However, in the early 1950s, the enterprises of the Ministry of Shipbuilding were busy building ships of other projects, and the ministry's leadership did not include the construction of new ships with armament not yet mastered in production in the approved plans."

msgid "IDS_PRSC528_FULL"
msgstr "Ochakov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC528_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC528_YEAR"
msgstr "1950"

msgid "IDS_PRSC529"
msgstr "Tolyatti"

msgid "IDS_PRSC529_DESCR"
msgstr "A hypothetical development of heavy cruiser projects designed by the Italian firm Ansaldo for Spain. This variant combines features of the Anteprojecto preliminar VIII-203 (four 203-mm gun mounts) and Anteprojecto preliminar XII-152 (four triple-gun mounts). If such a ship had been built for the Italian fleet, it would have been handed over to the Soviet Union as war reparations after World War II."

msgid "IDS_PRSC529_FULL"
msgstr "Tolyatti"

msgid "IDS_PRSC529_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC529_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PRSC530"
msgstr "Smolensk B"

msgid "IDS_PRSC530_DESCR"
msgstr "In 1950, three design bureaus of the 4th Special Department of the USSR Ministry of Internal Affairs developed preliminary designs for several light cruisers. The cruiser project MLK-16x130 was to be armed with four quadruple 130 mm BL-132 mounts. This variant was considered promising in terms of solving the assigned tasks and had the highest firepower per ton of displacement. However, in the early 1950s, the enterprises of the Ministry of Shipbuilding were busy building ships of other projects, and the ministry's leadership refused to include the construction of new ships, especially those with armament not yet mastered in production, in the approved plan."

msgid "IDS_PRSC530_FULL"
msgstr "Smolensk B"

msgid "IDS_PRSC530_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSC530_YEAR"
msgstr "1950"

msgid "IDS_PRSC538"
msgstr "P. Bagration"

msgid "IDS_PRSC538_DESCR"
msgstr "From 1943 to 1946, specialists from TsKB-17 and TsNIIVK Navy worked on Project 65, within which 40 draft projects were prepared. The largest of them was a cruiser with a displacement of 15,000 tons, armed with three triple mounts with 180 mm guns and eight twin SM-5-1 100 mm mounts. Ultimately, preference was given to the construction of new ships of Projects 68K and 68-bis."

msgid "IDS_PRSC538_FULL"
msgstr "Pyotr Bagration"

msgid "IDS_PRSC538_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC538_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PRSC539"
msgstr "Y. Dolgoruky"

msgid "IDS_PRSC539_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Project 68K and 68-bis cruisers, completed in the USSR in 1945, lagged behind contemporary American cruisers like the Worcester and Des Moines classes. In September 1945, Admiral N. Kuznetsov approved technical requirements for a new cruiser with 152 mm or 180 mm guns, including universal mounts. Further design development led to preliminary designs for the KRL-22 and Project 66 medium cruiser, armed with 220 mm artillery.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Following a tactical simulation conducted in spring 1954, it was recommended to enhance the universal artillery while reducing the ship's weight by replacing the non-universal 220 mm guns with 180 mm universal guns. The 130 mm artillery could then be replaced with smaller universal mounts.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC539_FULL"
msgstr "Yuri Dolgoruky"

msgid "IDS_PRSC539_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC539_YEAR"
msgstr "1954"

msgid "IDS_PRSC548"
msgstr "Bagration"

msgid "IDS_PRSC548_DESCR"
msgstr "From 1943 to 1946, specialists from TsKB-17 and TsNIIVK Navy worked on Project 65, within which 40 draft projects were prepared. The largest of them was a cruiser with a displacement of 15,000 tons, armed with three triple mounts with 180 mm guns and eight twin SM-5-1 100 mm mounts. Ultimately, preference was given to the construction of new ships of Projects 68K and 68-bis."

msgid "IDS_PRSC548_FULL"
msgstr "Bagration"

msgid "IDS_PRSC548_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC548_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PRSC549"
msgstr "Rus. Alaska"

msgid "IDS_PRSC549_DESCR"
msgstr "White as the peaks of the Wrangell Mountains. Adorned with gold, like the hopes of prospectors on the Yukon. Cold on the outside, but burning within, like the geysers of Umnak. Dangerous and dazzlingly beautiful... This is the 'Russian Alaska'!"

msgid "IDS_PRSC549_FULL"
msgstr "Russkaya Alyaska"

msgid "IDS_PRSC549_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PRSC549_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PRSC558"
msgstr "D. Pozharsky"

msgid "IDS_PRSC558_DESCR"
msgstr "Project 68-bis cruiser Dmitry Pozharsky was laid down in Leningrad at Ordzhonikidze Shipyard No. 189 in February 1952. The ship was launched in June 1953 and commissioned in December 1954. In February 1955, she was assigned to the Northern Fleet, but during the summer of the same year, the cruiser headed for Petropavlovsk-Kamchatsky to join the 14th Cruiser Division of the Pacific Fleet. In 1961, Dmitry Pozharsky joined the 9th Division of ASW Ships, and from 1967, she was on active duty within the 10th Tactical Squadron permanently operating in the Indian Ocean."

msgid "IDS_PRSC558_FULL"
msgstr "Dmitry Pozharsky"

msgid "IDS_PRSC558_NUMBER"
msgstr "21"

msgid "IDS_PRSC558_YEAR"
msgstr "1954"

msgid "IDS_PRSC559"
msgstr "Sverdlovsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSC559_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>By decision of the Defense Committee under the SNK No. 87cc of August 1937, it was decided to build new heavy cruisers, and in September a project specification was developed. The sketch project, indexed as 69, was carried out by TsKB-17.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1938, the project was adjusted to strengthen the armor. In November 1939, two heavy cruisers were laid down in Leningrad and Nikolaev, but after the start of the Great Patriotic War, construction was halted.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>This version assumes the armament of such a ship with triple 240 mm main battery turrets from the heavy cruisers of Project X, developed by TsKBS-1.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC559_FULL"
msgstr "Sverdlovsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSC559_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC559_YEAR"
msgstr "1937"

msgid "IDS_PRSC560"
msgstr "G. Suvorov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC560_DESCR"
msgstr "Snow-white, like the ceremonial uniform of a victor, this ship bears the name of Generalissimo Suvorov – a symbol of indomitable will and military valor. The inscriptions \"Principe d’Italia\" refer to his Italian campaign and the title of Prince of Italy, awarded for victories over the French army. Gold ornaments and the insignia of the highest award underscore his status. In this ship, two military traditions merge – and both serve victory."

msgid "IDS_PRSC560_FULL"
msgstr "Generalissimo Suvorov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC560_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC560_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PRSC568"
msgstr "Lazo B"

msgid "IDS_PRSC568_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>At the beginning of August 1937, the command of the Soviet fleet presented an updated plan for the construction of combat ships, which included light cruisers of a new type. The project received the index 68, and its development was carried out by TsKB-17. A total of 26 Project 68 cruisers were planned to be built in 1938-47.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Due to the outbreak of the Great Patriotic War, none of the seven ships laid down by that time were completed. Two cruisers under construction in Nikolaev were blown up by the Germans during their retreat, and the remaining ships were completed according to the adjusted Project 68K.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC568_FULL"
msgstr "Lazo B"

msgid "IDS_PRSC568_NUMBER"
msgstr "7"

msgid "IDS_PRSC568_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSC578"
msgstr "Kn. Olga"

msgid "IDS_PRSC578_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A warship that became the embodiment of the strength, will, and power of its mistress — the Polyanitsa Olga.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Combining powerful artillery and deadly torpedo armament, \"Knyaginya Olga\" subjugates the battle to its rhythm and leaves the enemy no chance of salvation. The dark hull with golden patterns recalls the ancient princely heritage.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC578_FULL"
msgstr "Knyaginya Olga"

msgid "IDS_PRSC578_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC578_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSC606"
msgstr "Makarov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC606_DESCR"
msgstr "The German light cruiser Nürnberg was built in Kiel, participated in the Spanish Civil War and World War II. In 1945, it surrendered while in the port of Copenhagen and was then transferred to the USSR as reparations, where it received the name 'Admiral Makarov.' It served in the 32nd Cruiser Division, then in the 28th Training Ship Division."

msgid "IDS_PRSC606_FULL"
msgstr "Admiral Makarov"

msgid "IDS_PRSC606_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC606_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PRSC608"
msgstr "Latanu"

msgid "IDS_PRSC608_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The heavy relay cruiser is the latest development from Forge-11 in the creation of experimental non-lethal weaponry. According to the project, the ship's armament is purely defensive in nature; in reality, however, the cruiser possesses all the capabilities for successfully conducting offensive operations.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The \"Latanu\" is equipped with a powerful psi-generator that creates a wide-radius field to induce irrational fear in enemies and spread panic, leading to a loss of combat capability and subsequent flight. The project was refined by Neo-Mind after the collapse of the USSR: the wave frequency was altered for selective impact on specific elements of enemy psyches, amplifying the effect to a state of animal terror.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During Operation Leviathan, the ship was captured by Covenant.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC608_FULL"
msgstr "Latanu"

msgid "IDS_PRSC608_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC608_YEAR"
msgstr "1954"

msgid "IDS_PRSC609"
msgstr "Kometa"

msgid "IDS_PRSC609_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The heavy cruiser-relay is the latest development from Forge-11 within the framework of creating experimental military technology. The ship is equipped with psionic generators and a psi-wave relay, which form a directed psi-field aimed at the information systems of major cities and bases for conducting espionage. The ship's project documentation states that its armament is intended solely for defense, but in reality, it possesses significant offensive potential.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Neo-Mind engineers enhanced the cruiser's functionality after the collapse of the USSR: the psi-field can now not only read but also precisely inject disinformation into enemy communications and even their subconscious.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship was discovered and captured by the Rise faction during Operation \"Depth\".</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC609_FULL"
msgstr "Kometa"

msgid "IDS_PRSC609_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC609_YEAR"
msgstr "1954"

msgid "IDS_PRSC610"
msgstr "Smolensk"

msgid "IDS_PRSC610_DESCR"
msgstr "In 1950, three design bureaus of the 4th Special Department of the USSR Ministry of Internal Affairs developed preliminary designs for several light cruisers. The cruiser project MLK-16x130 was to be armed with four quadruple 130 mm BL-132 mounts. This variant was considered promising in terms of solving the assigned tasks and had the highest firepower per ton of displacement. However, in the early 1950s, the enterprises of the Ministry of Shipbuilding were busy building ships of other projects, and the ministry's leadership refused to include the construction of new ships, especially those with armament not yet mastered in production, in the approved plan."

msgid "IDS_PRSC610_FULL"
msgstr "Smolensk"

msgid "IDS_PRSC610_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSC610_YEAR"
msgstr "1950"

msgid "IDS_PRSC618"
msgstr "Albatross"

msgid "IDS_PRSC618_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A heavy fire support cruiser with an integrated psi-control system was developed by Forge-11 for suppressing rebellions and conducting \"cleansing\" operations. According to its design, its armament is purely defensive, but in reality, the ship possesses formidable offensive capabilities.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The \"Albatross\" is equipped with a special generator that creates a directional field, suppressing aggression and the will to resist, inducing apathy and submission. After the collapse of the USSR, a group of Neo-Mind engineers enhanced this effect to the point of inducing a state of deep psychological subjugation in enemies, resulting in the complete loss of personality.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the execution of the \"Point of No Return\" contract, the ship was captured from the \"Whisper\" faction by Mercenary forces.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC618_FULL"
msgstr "Albatross"

msgid "IDS_PRSC618_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC618_YEAR"
msgstr "1954"

msgid "IDS_PRSC708"
msgstr "Grem"

msgid "IDS_PRSC708_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In August 2025, Vladimir Gremitsky, the head of the Art Department at Lesta, passed away. He led this department for 15 years and made an invaluable contribution to the development of Universe of Warships (Мир кораблей). In total, Vladimir dedicated 24 years of his life to our studio.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>This ship was created in memory of Vladimir Gremitsky. \"Grem\"—that's how many at work addressed Vladimir. This hybrid cruiser, carrying a tactical squadron of torpedo bombers, reflects Vladimir's love not only for surface ships but also for aviation.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC708_FULL"
msgstr "Grem"

msgid "IDS_PRSC708_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC708_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PRSC710"
msgstr "Sevastopol"

msgid "IDS_PRSC710_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In May 1940, tactical games involving the Project 69 cruiser and the battleships Scharnhorst and Dunkerque were held at the Naval Academy, showing that replacing the 305 mm guns with German 380 mm guns significantly increased the cruiser's combat power.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In July 1940, the People's Commissar of the Navy N. G. Kuznetsov approved the tactical-technical assignment for the rearmament of Project 69 cruisers with German 380 mm turrets. In October, TsKB-17 developed the preliminary design 69I, which involved the installation of three turrets with German 380 mm guns and fire control systems. In November of the same year, a contract was signed with the German company Krupp, which was to supply six turrets by March 1943. They were to be installed on the cruisers 'Kronstadt' and 'Sevastopol,' which were planned to be launched in 1942. The outbreak of the Great Patriotic War canceled these plans.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC710_FULL"
msgstr "Sevastopol"

msgid "IDS_PRSC710_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC710_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PRSC809"
msgstr "Riga CLR"

msgid "IDS_PRSC809_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After acquiring the heavy cruiser Lützow from Germany, Soviet specialists studied the ship's design documentation. Soon, the idea emerged to create improved ships based on the German design. On the orders of the People's Commissar of the Navy, Admiral N.G. Kuznetsov, the Main Naval Staff prepared a preliminary operational-tactical specification for the design of such a ship, which received the project index 82.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The design bureau of the Navy's Scientific and Technical Committee developed three variants of the preliminary design. The operational-tactical specification was adjusted based on the experience of naval combat operations, and in September 1943, a second edition was issued, in which the main caliber was increased to 220 mm and universal 130 mm guns were added. Based on this specification, TsKB-17 developed eight variants of sketches by the end of May. In this form, the ship was not approved for construction, but the further development of the project led to the emergence of the \"Stalingrad\"-class heavy cruisers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC809_FULL"
msgstr "Riga CLR"

msgid "IDS_PRSC809_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC809_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PRSC810"
msgstr "Komissar"

msgid "IDS_PRSC810_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the 1930s, when active construction of the Soviet fleet began, engineers developed modern combat ship projects on their own initiative. In March 1935, engineers V. P. Rimsky-Korsakov and A. I. Maslov from TsKBS-1 presented a preliminary design for a heavy cruiser with the index X.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>It was assumed that such a ship would operate on enemy communications, and its main opponents would be 'Washington' cruisers. To perform its tasks, the cruiser was equipped with twelve 240 mm main battery guns, twelve 130 mm universal guns, and aircraft. It was planned to equip the ship with high-pressure boilers and a three-shaft power plant to achieve high speed.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC810_FULL"
msgstr "Komissar"

msgid "IDS_PRSC810_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC810_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PRSC902"
msgstr "Iron Aurora"

msgid "IDS_PRSC902_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>During the battles of 1904, the combined forces of Russia and Japan captured several units of combat equipment from the Iron Horde—a mysterious fleet that attacked both powers. A distinctive feature of the captured ships was the complete absence of a crew: control was carried out by mechanical devices located in central posts.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The hull of this specimen is assembled from heterogeneous steel sheets with rough riveted seams, in which elements of industrial equipment are visible in places. The ship is distinguished by an unusual artillery system: the guns demonstrate phenomenal rate of fire, but the shells move along a high ballistic trajectory with a low initial velocity.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC902_FULL"
msgstr "Iron Aurora"

msgid "IDS_PRSC902_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC902_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PRSC910"
msgstr "[Moskva]"

msgid "IDS_PRSC910_DESCR"
msgstr "This ship is a copy of Moskva and is suitable for Clan Battles only"

msgid "IDS_PRSC910_FULL"
msgstr "[Moskva]"

msgid "IDS_PRSC910_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSC910_YEAR"
msgstr "1953"

msgid "IDS_PRSC912"
msgstr "Iron Novik"

msgid "IDS_PRSC912_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>During the battles of 1904, the combined forces of Russia and Japan captured several units of combat equipment from the Iron Horde—a mysterious fleet that attacked both powers. A distinctive feature of the captured ships was the complete absence of a crew: control was carried out by mechanical devices located in central posts.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The most unusual of the captured specimens is striking in its performance characteristics. Despite its primitive design, the ship is capable of reaching speeds of up to 200 knots thanks to a power plant that combines steam engines with complex rotating mechanisms. Computational devices, presumably intended for calculating ramming attacks, were found in the central post.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC912_FULL"
msgstr "Iron Novik"

msgid "IDS_PRSC912_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC912_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PRSC916"
msgstr "Makarov U"

msgid "IDS_PRSC916_DESCR"
msgstr "A replica of the ship Admiral Makarov for Training Trials and Operations."

msgid "IDS_PRSC916_FULL"
msgstr "Makarov (Training)"

msgid "IDS_PRSC916_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC916_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PRSC922"
msgstr "Iron Varyag"

msgid "IDS_PRSC922_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>During the battles of 1904, the combined forces of Russia and Japan captured several units of combat equipment from the Iron Horde—a mysterious fleet that attacked both powers. A distinctive feature of the captured ships was the complete absence of a crew: control was carried out by mechanical devices located in central posts.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>This specimen is of particular interest to military engineers. Externally, it resembles an ordinary cruiser assembled from low-quality materials, but its main armament uses an unknown principle for launching shells. The guns emit a characteristic crack and produce bright electrical discharges when fired. The shells fly at incredible speed along an absolutely straight trajectory, piercing even armored targets.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSC922_FULL"
msgstr "Iron Varyag"

msgid "IDS_PRSC922_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC922_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

