msgid "IDS_PRSC999"
msgstr "Test Ship"

msgid "IDS_PRSC999_DESCR"
msgstr "A ship for testing maps. She has a high speed, but her main guns and torpedo armament have minimum damage-dealing capabilities. This ship is designed to deliver fire on islands and coastlines."

msgid "IDS_PRSC999_FULL"
msgstr "Test Ship"

msgid "IDS_PRSC999_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSC999_YEAR"
msgstr "2014"

msgid "IDS_PRSD001"
msgstr "Gremyashchy"

msgid "IDS_PRSD001_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>'Gremyashchy,' a Project 7 destroyer, was built at Plant No. 190 named after Zhdanov in Leningrad. In September 1939, the ship was transferred to the north via the White Sea-Baltic Canal.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The destroyer participated in the Great Patriotic War: as part of the Northern Fleet, it conducted convoys, hunted submarines, participated in combat patrols, and raiding operations. In March 1943, the ship was awarded the title of Guards Ship. In total, during the war years, 'Gremyashchy' conducted 90 combat sorties.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>From 1954, the destroyer served in the White Sea Flotilla. In 1957, as an experimental vessel OS-5, it participated in nuclear tests on Novaya Zemlya, where it suffered severe damage.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD001_FULL"
msgstr "Gremyashchy"

msgid "IDS_PRSD001_NUMBER"
msgstr "35"

msgid "IDS_PRSD001_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSD102"
msgstr "Storozhevoi"

msgid "IDS_PRSD102_DESCR"
msgstr "In May 1915, the Technical Bureau at the Main Shipbuilding Department received an order to design small destroyers for coastal operations. Initially, it was planned to use Project D, with a displacement of 350 tons, as its basis. However, the standard displacement increased to 750 tons and more during the development process, which brought this project closer to the Novik-class destroyers that were already under construction, both in terms of displacement and cost. As a result, the Naval General Staff refused to build the ships under this project."

msgid "IDS_PRSD102_FULL"
msgstr "Storozhevoi"

msgid "IDS_PRSD102_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSD102_YEAR"
msgstr "1916"

msgid "IDS_PRSD103"
msgstr "Derzki"

msgid "IDS_PRSD103_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1911, a competition was announced for the design of a 34-knot destroyer for the Black Sea Fleet, and the basis for the tactical-technical specifications was the modern destroyer 'Novik,' under construction in Germany. The winner was the project of the Putilov Plant, and in the fall of 1912, the construction of the first destroyers of this type began in Nikolaev.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>'Derzkiy' was laid down in October 1913, launched in March 1914, and entered service in October of the same year. The ship participated in World War I, taking part in raiding, patrol, and escort operations.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In June 1918, the ship was captured by German troops, and in December, it came under the control of the British command. The ship underwent repairs in Malta and was then transferred to Wrangel's fleet. In November 1920, 'Derzkiy' left Crimea and arrived in Bizerta, where it was interned by the French authorities.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD103_FULL"
msgstr "Derzki"

msgid "IDS_PRSD103_NUMBER"
msgstr "9"

msgid "IDS_PRSD103_YEAR"
msgstr "1914"

msgid "IDS_PRSD104"
msgstr "Izyaslav"

msgid "IDS_PRSD104_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Before the start of World War I, amid international tensions, a program of accelerated shipbuilding was adopted. It was planned to build 36 destroyers for the Baltic Sea. The third of five series consisted of the largest and most heavily armed destroyers of the 'Izyaslav' type.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of this type was built in Revel. The destroyer participated in the Battle of Moon Sound and the Ice Cruise of the Baltic Fleet. In 1922, it received a new name—'Karl Marx.'</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD104_FULL"
msgstr "Izyaslav"

msgid "IDS_PRSD104_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PRSD104_YEAR"
msgstr "1917"

msgid "IDS_PRSD105"
msgstr "Gnevny (OLD)"

msgid "IDS_PRSD105_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The naval shipbuilding program for 1933-38 included the construction of 50 destroyers. The preliminary tactical-technical assignment for the design of a new destroyer was issued in October 1929. The review of pre-draft projects continued for three years. In the summer of 1932, a delegation of the USSR Navy visited Italy, where they familiarized themselves with the new Maestrale-class destroyers. It was decided to take this type of ship as the basis for designing new destroyers for the Soviet fleet.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In October 1932, a new tactical-technical assignment was approved. The project was developed by TsKBS-1, and the technical project, numbered 7, was approved in December 1934. 'Gnevny' was built at Plant No. 190 named after Zhdanov in Leningrad. The destroyer was lost on June 23, 1941, after hitting a German minefield.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD105_FULL"
msgstr "Gnevny (< 06.03.2017)"

msgid "IDS_PRSD105_NUMBER"
msgstr "35"

msgid "IDS_PRSD105_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PRSD106"
msgstr "Ognevoi (OLD)"

msgid "IDS_PRSD106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>At the beginning of August 1937, the Defense Committee decided to abandon further construction of Project 7 ships and begin designing a new destroyer—taking into account the experience of the war in Spain. The tactical-technical assignment for the destroyer project, numbered 30, was approved in November 1937, and development was carried out by the design bureau of Plant No. 190 in Leningrad. The technical project was approved in October 1939.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship, 'Ognevoi,' was built in Nikolaev at Plant No. 200 named after the 61 Communards. With the beginning of the Great Patriotic War, the hull of the unfinished ship was towed to Sevastopol and then to Batumi, where in March 1945, the destroyer entered service. It served as part of the Black Sea Fleet. In 1956, 'Ognevoi' was withdrawn from combat service and converted into a target ship TsL-2, and in 1958, it was removed from the lists.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD106_FULL"
msgstr "Ognevoi (< 06.03.2017)"

msgid "IDS_PRSD106_NUMBER"
msgstr "30"

msgid "IDS_PRSD106_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PRSD107"
msgstr "Udaloi"

msgid "IDS_PRSD107_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the second half of the 1930s, the adopted program for the construction of a 'large fleet' envisioned the construction of large destroyers capable of operating in the open ocean. The designed Project 30 destroyers, being a development of Project 7, did not fit this definition.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>It was decided to turn to foreign experience, and in March 1939, a Soviet delegation headed by Fleet Commander I. S. Isakov and engineer V. L. Bzhezinsky arrived in the USA. During negotiations with the firm Gibbs & Cox, the Soviet side was offered a destroyer project based on the Mahan type. However, the US government did not allow sharing modern technologies with the USSR, such as universal gun mounts. Nevertheless, after the visit to the USA, an operational-tactical assignment was created for the Project 35 destroyer, approved in March 1940. The preliminary design was developed by TsKB-32.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>According to the ship construction plan for 1941, two ships were to be laid down at Plant No. 190 named after Zhdanov in Leningrad under Project 35: 'Udaly' and 'Udarny.' With the beginning of the Great Patriotic War, all work on the destroyers was stopped, and after the war, the project was not revisited.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD107_FULL"
msgstr "Udaloi"

msgid "IDS_PRSD107_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSD107_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PRSD108"
msgstr "Tashkent (OLD)"

msgid "IDS_PRSD108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the early 1930s, the Directorate of the RKKA Navy held a number of meetings with shipbuilders from different countries to find an optimal technical solution for future Soviet ships. In 1935, negotiations began with the Italian shipyard Odero Terni Orlando regarding the construction of a destroyer leader in Italy. Such a ship was to serve as a prototype for serial construction at Soviet shipyards. In February 1936, the shipyard presented the ship project, which received the index 20I.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship, named 'Tashkent', was built at the shipyard in Livorno. In early 1941, the 130 mm twin B-2LM mounts provided for by the project were installed on the ship. During the Great Patriotic War, 'Tashkent' participated in the defense of Odessa and then Sevastopol, becoming the last ship to break through to the besieged city. Upon return, the leader was heavily damaged and, while preparing for the passage to the repair site in Poti, was sunk on July 2, 1942, by a direct hit from a German aerial bomb.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD108_FULL"
msgstr "Tashkent (< 06.03.2017)"

msgid "IDS_PRSD108_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSD108_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PRSD109"
msgstr "Kiev (OLD)"

msgid "IDS_PRSD109_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In August 1938, the tactical-technical assignment for the Project 48 leader—the successor to Projects 1 and 38—was approved. The ship was to have a high degree of unification with the Project 30 destroyers. The project was developed by the design bureau of Plant No. 198. The preliminary design was approved in July 1939, and the technical design—in October of the same year. A total of 20 Project 48 leaders were planned to be built.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship, 'Kiev,' was built at Plant No. 198 named after Andre Marty in Nikolaev. After the start of the Great Patriotic War, the construction of 'Kiev' was halted, and the hull was towed first to Sevastopol and then to Batumi. After the war, it was proposed to complete the ships according to the adjusted Project 48K, but ultimately, construction was canceled.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD109_FULL"
msgstr "Kiev (< 06.03.2017)"

msgid "IDS_PRSD109_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PRSD109_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSD110"
msgstr "Khabarovsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSD110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the second half of the 1930s, the idea of an 'armored leader' was popular. In 1935, TsNII VK and TsKBS-1 developed preliminary designs on their own initiative. The TsKBS-1 project, numbered 24, was developed in five variants and presented in December 1935 to the Shipbuilding Department of the Navy Directorate of the Red Army. In February 1936, the preliminary design was ready, but then the Navy leadership opted for more traditional destroyers and leaders.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The topic of the 'armored leader' was revisited in 1939 by a joint commission of the People's Commissariat of the Navy and the People's Commissariat of Shipbuilding. In September, the technical specifications were developed, and further refinement was entrusted to TsKB-17. The project received the number 47, but due to the outbreak of the Great Patriotic War, all work on it was stopped.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD110_FULL"
msgstr "Khabarovsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSD110_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSD110_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PRSD111"
msgstr "Zorkiy"

msgid "IDS_PRSD111_DESCR"
msgstr "After the end of the Great Patriotic War, an attempt was made to return to the project of an armored leader with universal artillery. TsKB-53 worked on the project on its own initiative from the end of 1945, taking the pre-war Project 47 as a basis. However, due to the lack of technology for welding ship steel, the project was canceled."

msgid "IDS_PRSD111_FULL"
msgstr "Zorkiy"

msgid "IDS_PRSD111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSD111_YEAR"
msgstr "1953"

msgid "IDS_PRSD205"
msgstr "Podvoisky"

msgid "IDS_PRSD205_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the summer of 1927, in light of the strengthening of the Romanian fleet, the Scientific-Technical Committee of the Navy of the Red Army began the initiative design of a destroyer for the Black Sea. In May 1928, the Red Army Staff issued the Navy Directorate with a task to develop a preliminary design. The discussion of the tactical-technical specifications took place at the NTC Navy in November 1928, and within three months, a project for a 40-knot squadron destroyer was developed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The project was carried out by the design bureau of the shipbuilding section of the Scientific-Technical Committee of the Naval Department under the leadership of engineer Yu. A. Shimansky. The general arrangement drawings were approved in March 1929. However, the Navy's budget for shipbuilding was cut, and the construction of the planned ships did not begin. Later, according to the revised project, leaders of the 'Leningrad' type were laid down and built.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD205_FULL"
msgstr "Podvoisky"

msgid "IDS_PRSD205_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSD205_YEAR"
msgstr "1929"

msgid "IDS_PRSD206"
msgstr "Gnevny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD206_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The naval shipbuilding program for 1933-38 included the construction of 50 destroyers. The preliminary tactical-technical assignment for the design of a new destroyer was issued in October 1929. The review of pre-draft projects continued for three years. In the summer of 1932, a delegation of the USSR Navy visited Italy, where they familiarized themselves with the new Maestrale-class destroyers. It was decided to take this type of ship as the basis for designing new destroyers for the Soviet fleet.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In October 1932, a new tactical-technical assignment was approved. The project was developed by TsKBS-1, and the technical project, numbered 7, was approved in December 1934. 'Gnevny' was built at Plant No. 190 named after Zhdanov in Leningrad. The destroyer was lost on June 23, 1941, after hitting a German minefield.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD206_FULL"
msgstr "Gnevny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD206_NUMBER"
msgstr "35"

msgid "IDS_PRSD206_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PRSD207"
msgstr "Minsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSD207_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>During the construction of the Project 1 leaders (Leningrad class), a large number of improvements and clarifications accumulated, so the second trio of leaders was built to an improved project, designated Project 38. The main difference from the original was the more traditional stern lines and propeller shaft brackets instead of fairings. The forward superstructure was also changed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship of the project, 'Minsk', was built at Plant No. 190 named after Zhdanov in Leningrad. During the Soviet-Finnish War, it shelled coastal batteries. With the beginning of the Great Patriotic War, it laid mines in the Gulf of Finland, participated in the defense of Tallinn and the Tallinn evacuation, and in the defense of Leningrad. After the war, it was used as a training ship.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD207_FULL"
msgstr "Minsk"

msgid "IDS_PRSD207_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PRSD207_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PRSD208"
msgstr "Ognevoi"

msgid "IDS_PRSD208_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>At the beginning of August 1937, the Defense Committee decided to abandon further construction of Project 7 ships and begin designing a new destroyer—taking into account the experience of the war in Spain. The tactical-technical assignment for the destroyer project, numbered 30, was approved in November 1937, and development was carried out by the design bureau of Plant No. 190 in Leningrad. The technical project was approved in October 1939.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship, 'Ognevoi,' was built in Nikolaev at Plant No. 200 named after the 61 Communards. With the beginning of the Great Patriotic War, the hull of the unfinished ship was towed to Sevastopol and then to Batumi, where in March 1945, the destroyer entered service. It served as part of the Black Sea Fleet. In 1956, 'Ognevoi' was withdrawn from combat service and converted into a target ship TsL-2, and in 1958, it was removed from the lists.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD208_FULL"
msgstr "Ognevoi"

msgid "IDS_PRSD208_NUMBER"
msgstr "30"

msgid "IDS_PRSD208_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PRSD209"
msgstr "Osmotritelny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD209_DESCR"
msgstr "The most advanced Soviet destroyer among those designed before 1941 (Project 35). Osmotritelny was large in size and could run at a very high speed for this type of ship. In terms of the power of her main guns, she outclassed most of her foreign counterparts."

msgid "IDS_PRSD209_FULL"
msgstr "Osmotritelny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD209_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSD209_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PRSD210"
msgstr "Grozovoi"

msgid "IDS_PRSD210_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In August 1942, the People's Commissar of the Navy, Admiral N. G. Kuznetsov, approved the operational-tactical assignment for the project of a large destroyer for ocean service, which was to become the successor to Project 35. The project received the number 40. In 1943-44, specialists from the NTC Navy and TsKB-17 performed preliminary studies, and in 1944, the assignment was clarified.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>A total of 6 preliminary variants of Project 40 were completed. One of them, authored by engineer V. Nikitin, was brought to the stage of a preliminary design under the index 40N. In the summer of 1945, it was decided to build 132 large destroyers over ten years, but already in the fall, their construction was abandoned in favor of light cruisers and simple destroyers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD210_FULL"
msgstr "Grozovoi"

msgid "IDS_PRSD210_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSD210_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PRSD218"
msgstr "Opytniy"

msgid "IDS_PRSD218_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Based on German shipbuilding experience, V. Brzezinski from CDB-1 proposed a destroyer with advanced boilers, reducing turbine weight. Professor L. Ramzin designed boilers with triple the pressure of Project 7 destroyers. The saved weight allowed for twin B-31 turrets.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Built at the Zhdanov plant, the ship was renamed 'Opytniy' in 1940. Without the B-31 turrets, it was armed with B-13 guns. Commissioned urgently in 1941, the ship supported the Leningrad Front until damaged in October 1941 and repaired in 1942–44.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD218_FULL"
msgstr "Opytniy"

msgid "IDS_PRSD218_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSD218_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PRSD306"
msgstr "Soobrazitelny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD306_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Project 7 destroyers had vulnerabilities due to their linear boiler arrangement. In 1938, CDB-17 designed Project 7U with staggered boiler placement. Many ships were re-laid to this design.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Laid down in 1936 as part of Project 7, Soobrazitelny was re-laid in 1939. It participated in the raid on Constanța, the defense of Odessa, and the Kerch-Feodosiya landing operation. Awarded the Guards title, it served in the Black Sea Fleet post-war.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD306_FULL"
msgstr "Soobrazitelny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD306_NUMBER"
msgstr "18"

msgid "IDS_PRSD306_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PRSD308"
msgstr "Kiev"

msgid "IDS_PRSD308_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In August 1938, the tactical-technical assignment for the Project 48 leader—the successor to Projects 1 and 38—was approved. The ship was to have a high degree of unification with the Project 30 destroyers. The project was developed by the design bureau of Plant No. 198. The preliminary design was approved in July 1939, and the technical design—in October of the same year. A total of 20 Project 48 leaders were planned to be built.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The lead ship, 'Kiev,' was built at Plant No. 198 named after Andre Marty in Nikolaev. After the start of the Great Patriotic War, the construction of 'Kiev' was halted, and the hull was towed first to Sevastopol and then to Batumi. After the war, it was proposed to complete the ships according to the adjusted Project 48K, but ultimately, construction was canceled.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD308_FULL"
msgstr "Kiev"

msgid "IDS_PRSD308_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PRSD308_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSD310"
msgstr "Nakhodka"

msgid "IDS_PRSD310_DESCR"
msgstr "The ship was designed as a destroyer flotilla leader. No destroyer in the world could rival her in terms of speed, while her armor and the power of her artillery were comparable to those of light cruisers. Dual-purpose main guns and a battery of automatic AA guns ensured a strong AA defense."

msgid "IDS_PRSD310_FULL"
msgstr "Nakhodka"

msgid "IDS_PRSD310_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSD310_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PRSD317"
msgstr "Osmotritelny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD317_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In August 1937, the Defense Committee decided to halt Project 7 destroyer construction and design new destroyers incorporating Spanish Civil War experience. Technical specifications for Project 30 were approved November 1937, developed by Plant No. 190 in Leningrad. The design shared elements with Project 48 leaders, including artillery (leaders had three twin mounts, destroyers two).</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Osmotritelny was laid down May 5, 1940 at Plant No. 402 in Molotovsk, launched August 1944, and commissioned September 29, 1947. It served with the Northern Fleet for 11 years.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD317_FULL"
msgstr "Osmotritelny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD317_NUMBER"
msgstr "10"

msgid "IDS_PRSD317_YEAR"
msgstr "1947"

msgid "IDS_PRSD318"
msgstr "Smely"

msgid "IDS_PRSD318_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After WWII, the USSR introduced Project 30-bis destroyers, improved versions of Project 30 with better seaworthiness and anti-aircraft capabilities. This series included 70 ships, the largest class of Soviet surface combatants.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Smely was laid down in 1948 in Leningrad, launched the same year, and commissioned in 1951. It served in the Baltic and Northern Fleets.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD318_FULL"
msgstr "Smely"

msgid "IDS_PRSD318_NUMBER"
msgstr "70"

msgid "IDS_PRSD318_YEAR"
msgstr "1951"

msgid "IDS_PRSD319"
msgstr "Spokoiny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD319_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1952, the USSR halted Project 41 ships and developed the smaller Project 56 destroyer. This new design improved hull form and reduced displacement.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Spokoiny was laid down in 1953 in Leningrad, launched the same year, and commissioned in 1956. It served in the Northern Fleet, later participating in major exercises like 'Ocean-70.'</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD319_FULL"
msgstr "Spokoiny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD319_NUMBER"
msgstr "27"

msgid "IDS_PRSD319_YEAR"
msgstr "1956"

msgid "IDS_PRSD320"
msgstr "Gordelivy"

msgid "IDS_PRSD320_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Mid-1950s Project 56 destroyers, while seaworthy, had issues with speed and aluminum superstructure vibrations. Living conditions were poor, and modernization potential was low.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>To address these problems, a new destroyer design was proposed, maintaining the dimensions of Project 41 but integrating modern technology. However, by the 1960s, missile destroyers became the priority, and the USSR shifted focus to Project 57-bis.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD320_FULL"
msgstr "Gordelivy"

msgid "IDS_PRSD320_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSD320_YEAR"
msgstr "1957"

msgid "IDS_PRSD409"
msgstr "Tashkent"

msgid "IDS_PRSD409_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the early 1930s, the Directorate of the RKKA Navy held a number of meetings with shipbuilders from different countries to find an optimal technical solution for future Soviet ships. In 1935, negotiations began with the Italian shipyard Odero Terni Orlando regarding the construction of a destroyer leader in Italy. Such a ship was to serve as a prototype for serial construction at Soviet shipyards. In February 1936, the shipyard presented the ship project, which received the index 20I.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship, named 'Tashkent', was built at the shipyard in Livorno. In early 1941, the 130 mm twin B-2LM mounts provided for by the project were installed on the ship. During the Great Patriotic War, 'Tashkent' participated in the defense of Odessa and then Sevastopol, becoming the last ship to break through to the besieged city. Upon return, the leader was heavily damaged and, while preparing for the passage to the repair site in Poti, was sunk on July 2, 1942, by a direct hit from a German aerial bomb.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD409_FULL"
msgstr "Tashkent"

msgid "IDS_PRSD409_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSD409_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PRSD410"
msgstr "Delny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD410_DESCR"
msgstr "The Soviet Union planned to build a leader series to Project 20I (also known as Tashkent). Following these plans, one of the laid-down Project 20I leaders was launched prior to the German invasion of the U.S.S.R with the intention of completing it as per the modified design after the end of the war."

msgid "IDS_PRSD410_FULL"
msgstr "Delny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD410_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSD410_YEAR"
msgstr "1952"

msgid "IDS_PRSD502"
msgstr "Boykiy"

msgid "IDS_PRSD502_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The 350-ton torpedo boat project of the 'Buyny' class was ordered from the British firm Yarrow in 1897. The design was based on the series of torpedo boats of the 'Ikazuchi' type built for Japan. The ships were built in St. Petersburg in 1901–1902.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Boykiy was the lead ship in a series of ten torpedo boats, laid down in June 1901 and launched in August of the same year. It took part in the Russo-Japanese War, engaging in battles with torpedo boats and fighting in the Battle of the Yellow Sea. In December 1904, before the surrender of Port Arthur, it broke through to Chefoo, where it was interned. The ship was part of the Siberian Flotilla and underwent modernization between the wars.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>From 1914 to 1922, it was in storage in Vladivostok. During the occupation of Vladivostok by Allied forces, the ship was captured by French forces and renamed 'Quentin Roosevelt.' After the departure of the interventionist forces, the destroyer was not reactivated.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD502_FULL"
msgstr "Boykiy"

msgid "IDS_PRSD502_NUMBER"
msgstr "10"

msgid "IDS_PRSD502_YEAR"
msgstr "1902"

msgid "IDS_PRSD505"
msgstr "Okhotnik"

msgid "IDS_PRSD505_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>According to the developments of the Naval General Staff, destroyers of the 'Novik' type were to operate jointly with light cruisers of the 'Svetlana' class. However, the construction of the latter was delayed, so the commander of the Baltic Fleet, Vice Admiral A. Nepenin, appealed to the Naval General Staff with a request to install six or seven 130 mm guns on the modified 'Gogland'-type destroyers under construction.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The preliminary design of such a ship was developed by ship engineer Colonel R. Matrosov. The project was reviewed in the first half of 1917 by specialists from the Main Directorate of Shipbuilding and raised many doubts. Further development of the project ceased in connection with the October Revolution.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD505_FULL"
msgstr "Okhotnik"

msgid "IDS_PRSD505_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSD505_YEAR"
msgstr "1917"

msgid "IDS_PRSD507"
msgstr "Leningrad"

msgid "IDS_PRSD507_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In January 1930, the USSR government restored funding for naval shipbuilding, and the leadership of the Naval Forces of the Red Army planned the construction of six destroyer leaders. The 1929 project was taken as the basis, which was revised to increase speed. By the end of 1930, the technical project, numbered 1, was completed. The Central Design Bureau for Special Shipbuilding—TsKBS-1—was specially created for the design of new ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>'Leningrad' was built at Plant No. 190 named after Zhdanov in Leningrad. During the Soviet-Finnish War, the leader participated in shelling Finnish positions and supporting landing operations. With the beginning of the Great Patriotic War, 'Leningrad' engaged in mine-laying, participated in the defense of Tallinn, the Tallinn evacuation, the evacuation of the Hanko naval base, and the defense of Leningrad.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD507_FULL"
msgstr "Leningrad"

msgid "IDS_PRSD507_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PRSD507_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PRSD509"
msgstr "Sovremenny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD509_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Of the eight planned Project 48 leaders, only two were launched. From 1944 to 1950, work continued on Project 48-K, but in 1950, the USSR decided to convert these hulls into target ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The hull of 'Yerevan' was sunk in 1957 during missile tests. 'Kiev' was later returned to Nikolaev for potential conversion into a missile destroyer or test ship. If completed as a gun destroyer, it would have had modern armament like 100 mm guns and ASW rocket launchers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD509_FULL"
msgstr "Sovremenny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD509_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSD509_YEAR"
msgstr "1962"

msgid "IDS_PRSD516"
msgstr "Shaumian"

msgid "IDS_PRSD516_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Fidonisi-class destroyers were a development of the Derzkiy-class destroyers for the Black Sea. They differed from their predecessors in enhanced armament and triple torpedo tubes.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The destroyer Levkas was laid down in May 1916 in Nikolayev, and in October 1917 the ship was launched. In an incomplete state, it was captured by Austro-German occupiers, forces of the Central Rada, Greco-Anglo-French occupiers, Green Army members, and White Guards.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The destroyer, renamed Shaumian, was completed in 1923–25 and joined the Black Sea Naval Forces. During the Great Patriotic War, it participated in the defense of Odessa and Sevastopol, the Kerch-Feodosia and Sudak landing operations. In April 1942, while transitioning from Novorossiysk to Poti, Shaumian ran aground near Gelendzhik. The ship could not be refloated, and soon the destroyer was wrecked due to bad weather and German bombardments.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD516_FULL"
msgstr "Shaumian"

msgid "IDS_PRSD516_NUMBER"
msgstr "7"

msgid "IDS_PRSD516_YEAR"
msgstr "1925"

msgid "IDS_PRSD517"
msgstr "Tashkent '39"

msgid "IDS_PRSD517_DESCR"
msgstr "The destroyer leader 'Tashkent' was designed and built at the Odero Terni Orlando shipyard in Italy. Soviet armament was planned for installation—three twin B-2L turrets with 130 mm guns model 1936. However, during the development process, the B-2L and B-2M projects were merged into one—B-2LM, which delayed testing. When enlisted in the fleet in October 1939, the ship was temporarily armed with three single B-13 130 mm gun mounts, with which it met the beginning of the Great Patriotic War. Its standard armament, three B-2LM mounts, 'Tashkent' received by July 1941."

msgid "IDS_PRSD517_FULL"
msgstr "Tashkent '39"

msgid "IDS_PRSD517_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSD517_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSD519"
msgstr "Ognenny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD519_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1955–56, assignments were approved for the conversion of Project 30-bis destroyers into radio intelligence ships. These ships were to conduct radio reconnaissance, radar, and jamming in the interests of the formation. The project included the installation of ZIF-71 anti-aircraft guns with radar guidance, two RBU-2500, as well as radio reconnaissance and radar equipment. In 1958–60, eight Project 30-bis destroyers were modernized.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>'Ognenny' was built in Molotovsk at plant No. 402 as a Project 30-bis destroyer. From 1950 to 1963, it was part of the Northern Fleet. In 1958–60, it was modernized under Project 31 as a radio intelligence ship. In 1963–64, it served in the Black Sea Fleet, and from 1968 — the Baltic Fleet. In 1971, it performed combat missions, providing assistance to the Egyptian Navy.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD519_FULL"
msgstr "Ognenny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD519_NUMBER"
msgstr "8"

msgid "IDS_PRSD519_YEAR"
msgstr "1950"

msgid "IDS_PRSD709"
msgstr "Neustrashimy"

msgid "IDS_PRSD709_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>According to the ten-year military shipbuilding program of 1945, it was planned to build 226 'standard' destroyers. Some of them were to belong to the well-mastered Project 30-bis, the rest—to a new type. The development of a destroyer with universal artillery was carried out by the fleet's subordinate design bureau TsNIIVK. TsKB-53 also presented its project. In the summer of 1947, the tactical-technical assignment for the Project 41 destroyer was approved. In August 1948, the preliminary design was approved, and in September 1949—the technical design.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>'Neustrashimy' was built at Plant No. 190 named after Zhdanov in Leningrad and served as part of the Baltic Fleet until 1974. A total of 110 destroyers of this type were planned for construction, but serial production of such a complex ship was abandoned in favor of Project 56 destroyers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSD709_FULL"
msgstr "Neustrashimy"

msgid "IDS_PRSD709_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSD709_YEAR"
msgstr "1955"

msgid "IDS_PRSD810"
msgstr "DD R-10"

msgid "IDS_PRSD810_DESCR"
msgstr "Test ship."

msgid "IDS_PRSD810_FULL"
msgstr "DD R-10"

msgid "IDS_PRSD810_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSD810_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PRSD910"
msgstr "[Grozovoi]"

msgid "IDS_PRSD910_DESCR"
msgstr "This ship is a copy of Grozovoi and is suitable for Clan Battles only"

msgid "IDS_PRSD910_FULL"
msgstr "[Grozovoi]"

msgid "IDS_PRSD910_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PRSD910_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PRSD916"
msgstr "Uporny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD916_DESCR"
msgstr "Ship for Training Trials participation."

msgid "IDS_PRSD916_FULL"
msgstr "Uporny"

msgid "IDS_PRSD916_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSD916_YEAR"
msgstr "1937"

msgid "IDS_PRSS106"
msgstr "S-1"

msgid "IDS_PRSS106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A diesel-electric submarine (Series IX, Type S—'Srednyaya'), intended for operations in the open ocean. The lead submarine of the project.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The submarine was built at Shipbuilding Plant No. 189 named after Ordzhonikidze in Leningrad and entered service with the Red Banner Baltic Fleet. It participated in combat sorties during the Soviet-Finnish War. For combat successes and the crew's courage, it was awarded the Order of the Red Banner, and the commander received the title of Hero of the Soviet Union.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>At the beginning of the Great Patriotic War, S-1 required serious repairs and was not operational. The submarine was scuttled by the crew during the retreat—shells destroyed the internal instruments and mechanisms but could not breach the pressure hull. The Germans raised the submarine and towed it to Kiel. The former S-1 was used as an experimental vessel to study underwater explosions and finally sank in August 1943.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSS106_FULL"
msgstr "S-1"

msgid "IDS_PRSS106_NUMBER"
msgstr "3"

msgid "IDS_PRSS106_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PRSS108"
msgstr "L-20"

msgid "IDS_PRSS108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A Soviet diesel-electric submarine, belonging to the last series of submarine minelayers of the 'Leninets' type (Series XIII-38).</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>L-20 was laid down and launched at Plant No. 189 named after Ordzhonikidze in Leningrad. After the start of the Great Patriotic War, the submarine was transferred via the White Sea-Baltic Canal to Molotovsk, where it was completed and entered service with the Northern Fleet. During the war years, it made 14 combat sorties. In 1943, it took first place in tonnage of torpedoed targets among all submarines of the Northern Fleet.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In June 1949, the submarine was renamed B-20, after which it was used in tests. It was decommissioned from the Navy in 1958.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSS108_FULL"
msgstr "L-20"

msgid "IDS_PRSS108_NUMBER"
msgstr "6"

msgid "IDS_PRSS108_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PRSS110"
msgstr "K-1"

msgid "IDS_PRSS110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In early 1935, the head of the Submarine Shipbuilding Department of the Research Institute of Military Shipbuilding, M. A. Rudnitsky, proposed a submarine project designated KE-9 (Cruiser-Squadron). In January 1936, the preliminary design was approved.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The submarine K-1 ('Kreyserskaya'), the lead submarine of the XIV series, was built at Plant No. 194 in Leningrad and joined the Red Banner Baltic Fleet. In the summer of 1940, K-1 transited the White Sea-Baltic Canal and was assigned to the 1st Division of the Submarine Brigade of the Northern Fleet. The submarine made 16 combat patrols totaling 196 days. Five ships and two warships were lost on the mines it laid.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The submarine was lost in 1943 during a patrol near Novaya Zemlya. It is presumed that K-1 was sunk by a German submarine.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSS110_FULL"
msgstr "K-1"

msgid "IDS_PRSS110_NUMBER"
msgstr "12"

msgid "IDS_PRSS110_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PRSS111"
msgstr "B-4"

msgid "IDS_PRSS111_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Large diesel-electric submarines of Project 641 were created to replace the submarines of Project 611, being their development. The new type was intended for operations on enemy communications, reconnaissance, and mine-laying. Project 641 was one of the last to have a classic hull type—subsequent submarines already had teardrop-shaped hulls.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>B-4 was built in Leningrad and transferred via internal waterways to Severodvinsk, where it entered service. During the Cuban Missile Crisis, the submarine participated in Operation 'Kama'—the breakthrough to Cuba. B-4 became the only submarine that the American blockade forces could not force to surface. In 1963, B-4 received the honorary name 'Chelyabinsky Komsomolets.' In 1966, the submarine was in Egypt, where Soviet forces were present to prevent further escalation of the Egyptian-Israeli conflict. In the 1960s-80s, B-4 performed combat and training tasks in the Atlantic and the Mediterranean Sea.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSS111_FULL"
msgstr "B-4"

msgid "IDS_PRSS111_NUMBER"
msgstr "75"

msgid "IDS_PRSS111_YEAR"
msgstr "1961"

msgid "IDS_PRSS508"
msgstr "S-189"

msgid "IDS_PRSS508_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>At the end of 1944, the USSR Navy received materials on the German submarine U-250, which was sunk in the Gulf of Finland and then raised from the bottom. After studying the captured submarine, the tactical-technical assignment for the development of Project 613 submarines was approved.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The unfulfilled Project 608 was taken as the basis, and the development was carried out by TsKB-18. In August 1946, the tactical-technical assignment was issued, and two years later, the technical project was approved by the USSR Government. When developing the theoretical drawings, special attention was paid to ensuring high speed characteristics in the submerged position. Project 613 submarines became the most mass-produced submarines of the USSR Navy.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>S-189 was built at Plant No. 189 named after Ordzhonikidze in Leningrad and spent its entire service on the Baltic. The submarine participated in combat sorties and testing new weapons at the Ladoga Lake test site. In 1970-72, it three times received the title 'Excellent Ship.'</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSS508_FULL"
msgstr "S-189"

msgid "IDS_PRSS508_NUMBER"
msgstr "236"

msgid "IDS_PRSS508_YEAR"
msgstr "1955"

msgid "IDS_PRSS518"
msgstr "S-189 B"

msgid "IDS_PRSS518_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>At the end of 1944, the USSR Navy received materials on the German submarine U-250, which was sunk in the Gulf of Finland and then raised from the bottom. After studying the captured submarine, the tactical-technical assignment for the development of Project 613 submarines was approved.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The unfulfilled Project 608 was taken as the basis, and the development was carried out by TsKB-18. In August 1946, the tactical-technical assignment was issued, and two years later, the technical project was approved by the USSR Government. When developing the theoretical drawings, special attention was paid to ensuring high speed characteristics in the submerged position. Project 613 submarines became the most mass-produced submarines of the USSR Navy.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>S-189 was built at Plant No. 189 named after Ordzhonikidze in Leningrad and spent its entire service on the Baltic. The submarine participated in combat sorties and testing new weapons at the Ladoga Lake test site. In 1970-72, it three times received the title 'Excellent Ship.'</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSS518_FULL"
msgstr "S-189 B"

msgid "IDS_PRSS518_NUMBER"
msgstr "236"

msgid "IDS_PRSS518_YEAR"
msgstr "1955"

msgid "IDS_PRSS910"
msgstr "Leninets"

msgid "IDS_PRSS910_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The lead ship of the \"Leninets\" class of submarine minelayers (Series II). The submarine was laid down and launched at the Ordzhonikidze Plant No. 189 in Leningrad.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the Soviet-Finnish War, the submarine served on combat duty. During the Great Patriotic War, in November 1941, the \"Leninets\" was damaged by German artillery in the port of Leningrad. In December, it was discovered that the submarine had run aground.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The submarine was raised by the Baltic Fleet's salvage service in 1944, but restoration work was not carried out, and the following year the \"Leninets\" was decommissioned from the fleet.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PRSS910_FULL"
msgstr "Leninets"

msgid "IDS_PRSS910_NUMBER"
msgstr "6"

msgid "IDS_PRSS910_YEAR"
msgstr "1933"

msgid "IDS_PRSS999"
msgstr "Test Sub 1"

msgid "IDS_PRSS999_DESCR"
msgstr "A submarine intended for testing terrain."

msgid "IDS_PRSS999_FULL"
msgstr "Test Sub 1"

msgid "IDS_PRSS999_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PRSS999_YEAR"
