msgstr "1946"

msgid "IDS_PZSA518"
msgstr "Xian Deng"

msgid "IDS_PZSA518_DESCR"
msgstr "Xian Deng was a type of 'assault' ship designed for close combat that emerged during the Three Kingdoms period. In Ancient China, naval engagements were predominantly fought on rivers at close range, which made ships capable of ramming, shelling at close quarters, and boarding particularly valuable."

msgid "IDS_PZSA518_FULL"
msgstr "Xian Deng"

msgid "IDS_PZSA518_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSA518_YEAR"
msgstr "1946"

msgid "IDS_PZSB106"
msgstr "Chakri"

msgid "IDS_PZSB106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1926, British engineer J. Thurston proposed a battleship design known as Vickers Design 892. This design featured a displacement of 26,500 tons. The gun layout was similar to that of Nelson-class battleships, with two turrets armed with 305-mm guns positioned at the bow.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Further development of the design could have increased the number of main guns to seven by reducing the maximum speed. If this design had been realized, the ship might have been purchased by Thailand, which had allied relationships with Western countries in the post-war years.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSB106_FULL"
msgstr "Chakri"

msgid "IDS_PZSB106_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSB106_YEAR"
msgstr "1926"

msgid "IDS_PZSB107"
msgstr "Zulfiqar"

msgid "IDS_PZSB107_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A battleship design proposed in 1926 by British engineer J. Thurston with a displacement of 26,500 tons. The gun arrangement was reminiscent of Nelson-class battleships. If this design had been realized, the ship could have been armed with seven main guns and eight secondary gun turrets. This would have required a reduction in maximum speed and a slight decrease in armor protection.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>If built, this ship could have been purchased by Iran, which bolstered its navy with American and British ships in the post-war years.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSB107_FULL"
msgstr "Zulfiqar"

msgid "IDS_PZSB107_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSB107_YEAR"
msgstr "1926"

msgid "IDS_PZSB108"
msgstr "Hanoi"

msgid "IDS_PZSB108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A hypothetical post-war modernization of a Type A battleship of the KB-4 design from the Baltic Shipyard, proposing the replacement of anti-mine artillery and the rear main battery turret with universal gun mounts. The remaining main battery guns would be replaced with 55-caliber 305-mm guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After modernization and rearmament, according to the USSR's geopolitical considerations, the ship could have been transferred to a friendly nation in the Asia-Pacific region, such as Vietnam.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSB108_FULL"
msgstr "Hanoi"

msgid "IDS_PZSB108_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSB108_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PZSB109"
msgstr "Dongfeng"

msgid "IDS_PZSB109_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A variant of a possible modernization of the Type A battleship of the KB-4 design from the Baltic Plant. The project involves replacing the anti-destroyer artillery and the rear main battery turret with universal gun mounts, and the remaining main battery guns are replaced with 55-caliber 305 mm guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Within the framework of the mutual assistance program that existed between the USSR and the PRC, the ship could have been transferred to China after such a modernization and rearmament.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSB109_FULL"
msgstr "Dongfeng"

msgid "IDS_PZSB109_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSB109_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PZSB110"
msgstr "Tiangong"

msgid "IDS_PZSB110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A hypothetical post-war modernization of a Type A battleship of the KB-4 design from the Baltic Shipyard. In this version, the anti-mine artillery and rear main battery turret are replaced with universal gun mounts, and the remaining main battery guns are replaced with 62-caliber 305-mm guns.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After modernization and rearmament, the ship could have been transferred to China within the framework of the scientific and technical cooperation program that existed between the USSR and China.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSB110_FULL"
msgstr "Tiangong"

msgid "IDS_PZSB110_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSB110_YEAR"
msgstr "1936"

msgid "IDS_PZSB509"
msgstr "Bajie"

msgid "IDS_PZSB509_DESCR"
msgstr "Bajie is half-man, half-pig, and one of the main mythological characters in Chinese culture. A wizard who achieved immortality, but was later expelled from the heavens to walk the earth as a punishment for his human passions."

msgid "IDS_PZSB509_FULL"
msgstr "Bajie"

msgid "IDS_PZSB509_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PZSB509_YEAR"
msgstr "1935"

msgid "IDS_PZSB519"
msgstr "Wujing"

msgid "IDS_PZSB519_DESCR"
msgstr "Wujing is a former celestial General who was banished to the mortal realm and turned into a man-eating demon for breaking a precious vase in the palace. Later on, he managed to atone for his guilt, acquire a human name again, and became a monk."

msgid "IDS_PZSB519_FULL"
msgstr "Wujing"

msgid "IDS_PZSB519_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSB519_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PZSB529"
msgstr "Sun Yat-Sen"

msgid "IDS_PZSB529_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A project for a 'Soviet Union'-class battleship, assuming a significant modernization in the 1950s. Main battery mounts are replaced with twin 457 mm ones: a similar project was considered as one of the options during the development of Project 24.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Medium-caliber guns and long-range anti-aircraft artillery are replaced with universal 152 mm gun mounts, light anti-aircraft artillery is represented by ZIF-75 and ZIF-31B mounts. Such a ship could have been transferred to China within the framework of USSR assistance in fleet development.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSB529_FULL"
msgstr "Sun Yat-Sen"

msgid "IDS_PZSB529_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSB529_YEAR"
msgstr "1955"

msgid "IDS_PZSB539"
msgstr "Lou Chuan"

msgid "IDS_PZSB539_DESCR"
msgstr "Lou Chuan was a Chinese naval ship primarily used as a floating fortress during the Han dynasty. Designed as a central vessel within the fleet, Louchuan was equipped for boarding enemy ships. She also carried siege weaponry for long-range engagements."

msgid "IDS_PZSB539_FULL"
msgstr "Lou Chuan"

msgid "IDS_PZSB539_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PZSB539_YEAR"
msgstr "1938"

msgid "IDS_PZSB549"
msgstr "Julang"

msgid "IDS_PZSB549_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>A Soviet Project 21 battleship that underwent significant modernization in the 1950s.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Main battery mounts are replaced with guns from the Project 82 ship (Stalingrad-class heavy cruiser). Medium-caliber guns and long-range anti-aircraft artillery are replaced with universal 130 mm gun mounts, light anti-aircraft artillery is represented by ZIF-75 and 4M-120 mounts.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Potentially, such a ship could have been transferred to China within the framework of USSR assistance in the development of the Chinese fleet.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSB549_FULL"
msgstr "Julang"

msgid "IDS_PZSB549_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSB549_YEAR"
msgstr "1954"

msgid "IDS_PZSB610"
msgstr "Changzheng"

msgid "IDS_PZSB610_DESCR"
msgstr "If, after World War II, a Yamato-class battleship had been transferred to the Chinese fleet, such a ship would have undergone extensive modernization. As part of the scientific and technical assistance program that existed between the USSR and the PRC, Soviet specialists would have participated in the project. The battleship would have received Soviet armament and electronics."

msgid "IDS_PZSB610_FULL"
msgstr "Changzheng"

msgid "IDS_PZSB610_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSB610_YEAR"
msgstr "1949"

msgid "IDS_PZSB620"
msgstr "Huángdì"

msgid "IDS_PZSB620_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Huangdi (the Yellow Emperor) is a legendary ruler of antiquity and a cultural progenitor in Chinese tradition. He is credited with organizing the calendar and rituals, patronizing crafts, and making inventions.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the Chinese doctrine of the world's structure, Wu Xing, the Yellow Emperor is associated with the Earth element. The yellow or golden color is a symbol of imperial power and cosmic order.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSB620_FULL"
msgstr "Huángdì"

msgid "IDS_PZSB620_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSB620_YEAR"
msgstr "1949"

msgid "IDS_PZSC101"
msgstr "Chengan"

msgid "IDS_PZSC101_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Exploiting a loophole in the London Naval Treaty, which did not limit the number of ships with a displacement of up to 600 tons, Japan in the 1930s began building small ships for escort and patrol duties. These ships were classified as kaibokans: originally in Japan, this term referred to obsolete ships converted for coastal defense needs.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The project was developed by Mitsubishi. In 1938–41, the first series of four ships was built, followed by another 174 kaibokans. After World War II, some of these ships ended up in the fleets of the Allied powers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Kaibokan No. 40 was built in 1944 at the Fujinagata shipyard. In 1945–47, after Japan's surrender, the ship was used as a minesweeper. In August 1947, the former kaibokan was transferred to China, where it was named Chengan, and in 1949 it passed to the Taiwanese fleet.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC101_FULL"
msgstr "Chengan"

msgid "IDS_PZSC101_NUMBER"
msgstr "67"

msgid "IDS_PZSC101_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PZSC105"
msgstr "Chungking"

msgid "IDS_PZSC105_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>HMS Aurora, a British Arethusa-class light cruiser, was built in Portsmouth in 1935–37. During World War II, the ship participated in the Norwegian Campaign and the hunt for Bismarck, intercepted Italian convoys in the Mediterranean, covered Allied landings in Africa, Sicily, and Southern France.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In May 1948, the cruiser was transferred to the Navy of the Republic of China as compensation for ships seized by Britain in Hong Kong and lost during the war. The ship was named Chungking and became the flagship of the Chinese Navy. In February 1949, the cruiser and its entire crew defected to the Communist Party of China, but a month later it was sunk by Kuomintang aircraft.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After the civil war ended, the ship was raised. There were plans for its repair and modernization with Soviet assistance, but they were never realized.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC105_FULL"
msgstr "Chungking"

msgid "IDS_PZSC105_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PZSC105_YEAR"
msgstr "1937"

msgid "IDS_PZSC106"
msgstr "Rahmat"

msgid "IDS_PZSC106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Dido-class cruisers were built in Britain from 1937 to 1942. These were ships for squadron service and escort, whose main task was seen as the anti-aircraft defense of formations. For this purpose, 5.25-inch universal guns in twin mounts were chosen as the main battery. Such guns were used on King George V-class battleships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Eleven ships were built to the original design and another five to an improved design (also known as Bellona or Black Prince). During World War II, six of the sixteen cruisers were lost or not restored after heavy damage.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the 1940s–50s, some ships were sold to New Zealand and Pakistan. The Royal Malaysian Navy also received several foreign ships after the war, including from Britain. It is quite possible that these cruisers were considered for acquisition by Malaysia.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC106_FULL"
msgstr "Rahmat"

msgid "IDS_PZSC106_NUMBER"
msgstr "11"

msgid "IDS_PZSC106_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PZSC107"
msgstr "Chumphon"

msgid "IDS_PZSC107_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Atlanta-class cruisers were built in the USA from 1940 to 1946. These ships were designed as cruisers—leaders of destroyer flotillas—but were used primarily as anti-aircraft cruisers to escort carrier groups.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The main battery of Atlanta-class cruisers consisted of 5-inch universal guns. These guns were unified with the main battery of destroyers and were also used on battleships and heavy cruisers. A total of eleven ships of this type were built, two were lost during World War II, and the remaining were decommissioned in 1959–65.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the early 1960s, the Royal Thai Navy needed ships: both coast defense ships were lost, and two cruisers ordered in Italy were never completed. Given allied relations with the USA, Thailand could have considered acquiring decommissioned American Atlanta-class cruisers.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC107_FULL"
msgstr "Chumphon"

msgid "IDS_PZSC107_NUMBER"
msgstr "8"

msgid "IDS_PZSC107_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PZSC108"
msgstr "Harbin"

msgid "IDS_PZSC108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1939–41, Special Design Bureau No. 196 under the NKVD Directorate for the Leningrad Region, on its own initiative, designed a number of preliminary projects for a small long-range light cruiser. The tasks of such a ship would have been to fight enemy destroyers and leaders, lead light forces, mine enemy waters, operate on communications, and support landing operations.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Among the sketches was the MK-6 cruiser project, armed with 130 mm universal guns in twin mounts. If cruisers of this type had been built, it is not excluded that some of them would have been transferred to the young Navy of the People's Republic of China along with Project 7 destroyers and guard ships.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC108_FULL"
msgstr "Harbin"

msgid "IDS_PZSC108_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSC108_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PZSC109"
msgstr "Sejong"

msgid "IDS_PZSC109_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>CL-154 class cruisers were designed in the USA as a further development of the Atlanta-class cruisers. They were to differ from their predecessors by an enlarged hull and the replacement of the main battery guns with new 5-inch/54 caliber guns. The new gun had a higher rate of fire, greater range, and heavier shells. Design began with a sketch proposed in June 1942, and the last preliminary design dates from December 1944.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The construction of six cruisers was planned, and they were even assigned hull numbers, but cuts in the naval budget in March 1945 canceled these plans. If they had been completed, it is not excluded that some cruisers would have been transferred to the Navy of the Republic of Korea: in 1955–60, this country purchased 42 warships of various classes from the USA, and in the 1960s–70s, Fletcher, Gearing, and Allen M. Sumner class destroyers entered the Korean fleet.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC109_FULL"
msgstr "Sejong"

msgid "IDS_PZSC109_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSC109_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PZSC110"
msgstr "Jinan"

msgid "IDS_PZSC110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The further development of the Atlanta-class cruisers was to be the CL-154 project, which envisaged an enlarged hull, replacement of the main battery guns with 5-inch/54 caliber guns with high rate of fire, long range, and heavy shells. The first sketch was proposed in June 1942, and the final project was ready in December 1944.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Six cruisers were planned for construction and had already received hull numbers. However, due to cuts in the naval budget in March 1945, the project was never realized. If these ships had been completed, some of them could have been transferred to the Navy of the Republic of China, which actively acquired US-built destroyers of the Benson, Gleaves, Bristol, Allen M. Sumner, Gearing, and Kidd classes in the 1950s–80s.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC110_FULL"
msgstr "Jinan"

msgid "IDS_PZSC110_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSC110_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PZSC111"
msgstr "Xi'an"

msgid "IDS_PZSC111_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1950, several light cruiser designs were developed by three OKB (Design Bureaus) of the 4th Special Department of the USSR Ministry of Internal Affairs. The MLK-10x130 cruiser project was armed with five twin 130-mm BL-110 gun mounts.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>As part of a hypothetical modernization in the 1960s, it can be assumed that the BL-110 mounts would be replaced with SM-62 mounts, which were more effective in engaging air targets. After modernization and rearmament, the ship could have been transferred to China as part of the scientific and technical cooperation program between the countries.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC111_FULL"
msgstr "Xi'an"

msgid "IDS_PZSC111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSC111_YEAR"
msgstr "1950"

msgid "IDS_PZSC503"
msgstr "Ning Hai"

msgid "IDS_PZSC503_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the late 1920s, the new government of the Republic of China was looking for ways to develop—and effectively recreate—a fleet that had not been replenished with new ships since the end of the 19th century. Japanese shipbuilding firms offered their services to Beijing.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Initially, it was planned to build cruisers similar in characteristics to the Japanese Aoba type, but for economy, extremely compact ships based on the cruiser Yūbari were designed. Two mini-cruisers, Ning Hai and Ping Hai, were laid down in 1931. The lead ship, Ning Hai, was built in Japan and joined the Chinese fleet in September 1932.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Both Chinese cruisers were sunk by Japanese aircraft at the start of the Second Sino-Japanese War in 1937. In 1938–43, Ning Hai was in Sasebo, serving as a barracks ship. In 1944, the ship was repaired and converted into an escort vessel, receiving a new name. On September 19, 1944, the former Chinese cruiser was torpedoed by the submarine USS Shad and sank.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC503_FULL"
msgstr "Ning Hai"

msgid "IDS_PZSC503_NUMBER"
msgstr "2"

msgid "IDS_PZSC503_YEAR"
msgstr "1932"

msgid "IDS_PZSC506"
msgstr "Huanghe"

msgid "IDS_PZSC506_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>After the Chinese cruiser Chungking was sunk by Kuomintang aircraft in Huludao port, the government of the People's Republic of China attempted restoration. In 1951, the hull was raised and towed to Dalian, where it was inspected by a commission from the Soviet Central Design Bureau No. 17.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Following the inspection, several technical specifications were drawn up for a major overhaul with modernization. The most balanced of the proposed modernization projects involved replacing the armament with Soviet ones while maintaining the total number and caliber of artillery, but the old gun mounts would be replaced with modern ones, significantly increasing the cruiser's combat capabilities.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>While approvals and disputes over the most suitable project were ongoing, in 1959 the ship was renamed Huanghe. Some time later, all Soviet specialists were recalled from China, preventing the possible modernization of the cruiser.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC506_FULL"
msgstr "Huanghe"

msgid "IDS_PZSC506_NUMBER"
msgstr "4"

msgid "IDS_PZSC506_YEAR"
msgstr "1937"

msgid "IDS_PZSC508"
msgstr "Irian"

msgid "IDS_PZSC508_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Project 68-bis cruiser 'Ordzhonikidze' was built at Plant No. 194 in Leningrad and joined the 4th (former South Baltic) Fleet. In 1955, the Baltic Fleet was reunified, and the cruiser became part of it.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1955, the cruiser arrived in Portsmouth on an official visit by Soviet leaders to the UK. During this visit, a British underwater commando died in an incident. In 1961, the cruiser transferred to the Black Sea Fleet.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1962, 'Ordzhonikidze' was transferred to the Indonesian Navy, where it was named Irian. The cruiser served in the Indonesian fleet until 1972.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC508_FULL"
msgstr "Irian"

msgid "IDS_PZSC508_NUMBER"
msgstr "21"

msgid "IDS_PZSC508_YEAR"
msgstr "1952"

msgid "IDS_PZSC509"
msgstr "Dalian"

msgid "IDS_PZSC509_DESCR"
msgstr "A modernized version of the MK-6 cruiser project with gun mounts from the 1940s replaced with more contemporary ones—the main battery is represented by BL-109 guns instead of MK-18, and AA mounts represented by ZIF-31 and 4M-120 mounts instead of 66-K and 70-K, respectively. The project reflects a potential upgrade of the cruiser, which might have been carried out in the 1950s."

msgid "IDS_PZSC509_FULL"
msgstr "Dalian"

msgid "IDS_PZSC509_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSC509_YEAR"
msgstr "1952"

msgid "IDS_PZSC518"
msgstr "Wukong"

msgid "IDS_PZSC518_DESCR"
msgstr "Wukong is the Monkey King and one of the main mythological characters in Chinese culture. He is an immortal demon who obtained extraordinary power after long years of wandering and spiritual perfection."

msgid "IDS_PZSC518_FULL"
msgstr "Wukong"

msgid "IDS_PZSC518_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PZSC518_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PZSC519"
msgstr "Hengshui"

msgid "IDS_PZSC519_DESCR"
msgstr "The Chinese New Year in China has been celebrated since ancient times, with the first written records of the tradition dating back to the Han Dynasty. The festivities were described in the book 'Monthly Instructions for the Four Classes of People.' This treatise was authored by Cui Shi—a scholar and government official from a region that is now part of modern Hengshui city in China."

msgid "IDS_PZSC519_FULL"
msgstr "Hengshui"

msgid "IDS_PZSC519_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSC519_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PZSC528"
msgstr "Dou Jian"

msgid "IDS_PZSC528_DESCR"
msgstr "Dou Jian is a type of warship that appeared in China during the 2nd–3rd centuries AD, serving as a fast combat unit in the fleet. Its name literally means 'combat ship' in Chinese. During the Three Kingdoms era, it played an important role in covering the main forces."

msgid "IDS_PZSC528_FULL"
msgstr "Dou Jian"

msgid "IDS_PZSC528_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSC528_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PZSC529"
msgstr "Meng Chong"

msgid "IDS_PZSC529_DESCR"
msgstr "Meng Chong was a warship used in China in the second and third centuries AD. She had solid, leather-covered sides that allowed archers to fire from behind cover and protected the rowers. Meng Chong was used as a cover ship."

msgid "IDS_PZSC529_FULL"
msgstr "Meng Chong"

msgid "IDS_PZSC529_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSC529_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PZSC539"
msgstr "Chìdì"

msgid "IDS_PZSC539_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Red Emperor in Chinese mythology is the patron of the south and is associated with the element of fire. In a number of legends, the Red Emperor is identified with Shennong—a cultural hero of agriculture and medicinal herbs.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The color red in Chinese culture symbolizes joy, protection, and good fortune, and is also an important element of New Year rituals.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC539_FULL"
msgstr "Chìdì"

msgid "IDS_PZSC539_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSC539_YEAR"
msgstr "1939"

msgid "IDS_PZSC608"
msgstr "Tanifa"

msgid "IDS_PZSC608_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The heavy transport-landing cruiser was developed by Forge-11 for invasive psychological support. The ship is equipped with a system for subtle psi-correction, operating within a narrow, protected range. Its emitters are tuned to maintain combat morale, suppress fear, and improve the emotional state of the crews of supported vessels and shore units. The ship possesses all necessary resources for conducting an offensive, although, according to its design, its armament was intended to be used exclusively for defense.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After the collapse of the USSR, Neo-Mind continued work on the cruiser project: scientists altered the emitter configuration, adding a function for temporarily increasing suggestibility and aggression.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During Operation \"Maelstrom\", the ship was discovered and taken under control by the Frontier group.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSC608_FULL"
msgstr "Tanifa"

msgid "IDS_PZSC608_NUMBER"
msgstr "21"

msgid "IDS_PZSC608_YEAR"
msgstr "1952"

msgid "IDS_PZSD102"
msgstr "Longjiang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD102_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In the early 20th century, the Qing Empire actively revived its fleet, which had been defeated during the First Sino-Japanese War. Before the start of World War I, major contracts were signed with Austrian and German shipbuilding firms. With the help of intermediaries from Arnhold, Karberg & Co, a contract was signed with the AG Vulcan Stettin shipyard for the construction of six 985-ton destroyers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The six ships were laid down in 1913 and launched in 1914, but with the outbreak of war, the destroyers were requisitioned by the German government.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD102_FULL"
msgstr "Longjiang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD102_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PZSD102_YEAR"
msgstr "1913"

msgid "IDS_PZSD103"
msgstr "Phra Ruang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD103_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>HMS Radiant, an R-class destroyer, was built at the Thornycroft shipyards. During World War I, it served with the Harwich Force.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1920, the destroyer was sold to Thailand, where it was named Phra Ruang. During and after World War II, it was used primarily as a training ship.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD103_FULL"
msgstr "Phra Ruang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD103_NUMBER"
msgstr "5"

msgid "IDS_PZSD103_YEAR"
msgstr "1917"

msgid "IDS_PZSD104"
msgstr "Shenyang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD104_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Namikaze, a Minekaze-class destroyer, was built at the Maizuru Naval Arsenal. In 1942, it was in the reserve group for the Aleutian operation, later engaged in patrol duties. From December 1943, it was used for convoy escort.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1944, the destroyer was hit by a torpedo from the submarine USS Seal and was sent to Maizuru for repair and conversion into a kaiten carrier. In the summer of 1945, it was used as a minesweeper.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After the end of World War II, the ship evacuated Japanese soldiers, and in 1947 it was transferred to China as reparations, where it was named Shenyang. In 1949, it passed to the Taiwanese Navy, where it served until 1960.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD104_FULL"
msgstr "Shenyang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD104_NUMBER"
msgstr "15"

msgid "IDS_PZSD104_YEAR"
msgstr "1922"

msgid "IDS_PZSD105"
msgstr "Jianwei"

msgid "IDS_PZSD105_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The fleet development program adopted by the new Chinese government in the late 1920s included destroyers. In 1929, the Chinese approached the British firm Thornycroft, which proposed a design for a destroyer leader (internal index T.306).</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The design was generally very similar to British leaders built at the end of World War I and would have been a worthy response to Japanese destroyers built in the 1910s–20s. However, due to lack of funding, the project was never realized.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD105_FULL"
msgstr "Jianwei"

msgid "IDS_PZSD105_NUMBER"
msgstr "0"

msgid "IDS_PZSD105_YEAR"
msgstr "1929"

msgid "IDS_PZSD106"
msgstr "Fushun"

msgid "IDS_PZSD106_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Project 7 destroyer 'Rezkiy' was laid down at Plant No. 198 named after Andre Marty in Nikolaev, then transported by rail to Vladivostok and re-laid in August 1938 at the Dalzavod.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>It participated in the Soviet-Japanese War at the final stage of World War II. In 1954, together with the destroyer 'Rekordny', it was transferred to the People's Republic of China and entered the PLA Navy as Fushun.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD106_FULL"
msgstr "Fushun"

msgid "IDS_PZSD106_NUMBER"
msgstr "35"

msgid "IDS_PZSD106_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PZSD107"
msgstr "Gadjah Mada"

msgid "IDS_PZSD107_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The destroyer HMS Nonpareil, an N-class ship, was built in Dumbarton, and in 1942 was transferred to the Dutch Navy under the name Tjerk Hiddes. It participated in the occupation of Madagascar and escorted convoys in the Indian Ocean.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In March 1951, the ship was transferred to the Navy of independent Indonesia, where it was named Gadjah Mada and became the fleet flagship. The destroyer served in the Indonesian Navy for ten years.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD107_FULL"
msgstr "Gadjah Mada"

msgid "IDS_PZSD107_NUMBER"
msgstr "8"

msgid "IDS_PZSD107_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PZSD108"
msgstr "Hsienyang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD108_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>USS Rodman, a Gleaves-class destroyer, was built at Federal Shipbuilding and Drydock Company. During World War II, the ship was involved in patrol and convoy operations in the North Atlantic, supported the landing in North Africa, escorted President F. Roosevelt to the Tehran Conference. In 1944, it covered the Allied landing in Normandy, then operated in the Mediterranean and the Pacific.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1955, the destroyer was sold to the Navy of the Republic of China and received the name Hsienyang. In 1956, it collided with other ships twice, earning it the nickname Ram.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD108_FULL"
msgstr "Hsienyang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD108_NUMBER"
msgstr "66"

msgid "IDS_PZSD108_YEAR"
msgstr "1942"

msgid "IDS_PZSD109"
msgstr "Chung Mu"

msgid "IDS_PZSD109_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>USS Erben, a Fletcher-class destroyer, was built at Bath Iron Works. Participated in the Pacific War: covered the landing of American troops on Tarawa, the Marshall Islands, New Guinea, and the Mariana Islands, guarded carriers in the Battle of the Philippine Sea. In 1945, participated in the landing on Okinawa. Received six battle stars during the war.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1946–51, it was in reserve, re-entered service during the Korean War. In the 1950s, it was part of the US 7th Fleet. In May 1963, it was sold to the Republic of Korea and entered its fleet as Chung Mu.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD109_FULL"
msgstr "Chung Mu"

msgid "IDS_PZSD109_NUMBER"
msgstr "175"

msgid "IDS_PZSD109_YEAR"
msgstr "1943"

msgid "IDS_PZSD110"
msgstr "Yueyang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD110_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>USS Haynsworth, an Allen M. Sumner-class destroyer, was built at Federal Shipbuilding and Drydock Company. Participated in the Pacific War as part of Admiral J. McCain's Task Force 58. On April 6, 1945, it was damaged by a kamikaze attack.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the 1950s–60s, the destroyer served with the US 6th Fleet in the Mediterranean, participated in the Suez and Cuban Crises. From 1964, it was a Naval Reserve training ship.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1970, the destroyer was sold to the Taiwanese Navy, receiving the name Yueyang. The ship served in the Taiwanese fleet for 28 years. In 1999, the ship was decommissioned, and in 2001 its hull was used to create an artificial reef.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD110_FULL"
msgstr "Yueyang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD110_NUMBER"
msgstr "58"

msgid "IDS_PZSD110_YEAR"
msgstr "1944"

msgid "IDS_PZSD111"
msgstr "Kunming"

msgid "IDS_PZSD111_DESCR"
msgstr "A fast and well-armed destroyer, she is essentially an enlarged variant of the U.S. Somers-class ships with enhanced torpedo and anti-aircraft armament."

msgid "IDS_PZSD111_FULL"
msgstr "Kunming"

msgid "IDS_PZSD111_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSD111_YEAR"
msgstr "1947"

msgid "IDS_PZSD208"
msgstr "Siliwangi"

msgid "IDS_PZSD208_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>The Project 30-bis destroyer 'Volevoy' was built at Plant No. 199 named after the Leninist Komsomol in Komsomolsk-on-Amur. It was laid down in March 1951, launched in September, and commissioned in December of the same year.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In February 1959, together with three other Project 30-bis destroyers, it was transferred to the Indonesian Navy, receiving the name Siliwangi. A total of eight ships of this type were transferred to the Indonesian Navy.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD208_FULL"
msgstr "Siliwangi"

msgid "IDS_PZSD208_NUMBER"
msgstr "70"

msgid "IDS_PZSD208_YEAR"
msgstr "1951"

msgid "IDS_PZSD506"
msgstr "Anshan"

msgid "IDS_PZSD506_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>'Rekordny', a Project 7 destroyer, was laid down in September 1936 at Plant No. 198 named after Andre Marty in Nikolaev, then sent to Vladivostok, where it was re-laid in July 1937 at the Dalzavod. Launched in April 1939 and entered service in January 1941. In 1945, the ship participated in the Soviet-Japanese War.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1954, together with the destroyer 'Rezkiy', it was transferred to the People's Republic of China and entered the PLA Navy under the name Anshan. Decommissioned in 1992, after which it was placed in Qingdao port as a museum ship.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD506_FULL"
msgstr "Anshan"

msgid "IDS_PZSD506_NUMBER"
msgstr "35"

msgid "IDS_PZSD506_YEAR"
msgstr "1941"

msgid "IDS_PZSD508"
msgstr "Loyang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD508_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>USS Benson, the lead ship of its class, was built at the Fore River Shipyard. In 1940–41, it was part of the Neutrality Patrol. From the start of US involvement in World War II, it escorted convoys in the North Atlantic and Mediterranean, supported the Allied landing in Italy.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1945, it was transferred to the Pacific, escorting American carriers. Awarded four battle stars during the war. In 1954, the ship was transferred to Taiwan, where it was named Loyang.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD508_FULL"
msgstr "Loyang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD508_NUMBER"
msgstr "96"

msgid "IDS_PZSD508_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PZSD510"
msgstr "Lüshun"

msgid "IDS_PZSD510_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>In 1949–56, a program of scientific and technical assistance operated between the USSR and the PRC, within which the Soviet side transferred, among other things, blueprints for prospective warships to China.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In particular, China was given the technical documentation for the Project 41 destroyer. In their original form, ships of this project were not built in China, but in 1968–87, 15 Type 051 (Luda-class) missile destroyers were built, which were based on the Soviet Project 41.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD510_FULL"
msgstr "Lüshun"

msgid "IDS_PZSD510_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSD510_YEAR"
msgstr "1950"

msgid "IDS_PZSD518"
msgstr "Fenyang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD518_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>Yoizuki, an Akizuki-class destroyer, was built at Uraga Docks. Participated in the Pacific War. In June 1945, it was damaged by a mine and spent the remainder of the war under repair. After the war, it was used to repatriate Japanese soldiers.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In August 1947, it was transferred to China as reparations. In the Chinese fleet, it was named Fenyang, and in 1949 it passed to the Taiwanese fleet.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD518_FULL"
msgstr "Fenyang"

msgid "IDS_PZSD518_NUMBER"
msgstr "13"

msgid "IDS_PZSD518_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PZSD529"
msgstr "Ben Ma"

msgid "IDS_PZSD529_DESCR"
msgstr "Ben Ma is a type of warship used for escort purposes in China during the 2nd–3rd centuries. This ship was said to be as fast as a galloping horse, which is how it got its name."

msgid "IDS_PZSD529_FULL"
msgstr "Ben Ma"

msgid "IDS_PZSD529_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSD529_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PZSD598"
msgstr "Loyang B"

msgid "IDS_PZSD598_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>USS Benson, the lead ship of its class, was built at the Fore River Shipyard. In 1940–41, it was part of the Neutrality Patrol. From the start of US involvement in World War II, it escorted convoys in the North Atlantic and Mediterranean, supported the Allied landing in Italy.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1945, it was transferred to the Pacific, escorting American carriers. Awarded four battle stars during the war. In 1954, the ship was transferred to Taiwan, where it was named Loyang.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSD598_FULL"
msgstr "Loyang B"

msgid "IDS_PZSD598_NUMBER"
msgstr "96"

msgid "IDS_PZSD598_YEAR"
msgstr "1940"

msgid "IDS_PZSD610"
msgstr "Tuntou"

msgid "IDS_PZSD610_DESCR"
msgstr "A ship for testing."

msgid "IDS_PZSD610_FULL"
msgstr "Tuntou"

msgid "IDS_PZSD610_NUMBER"
msgstr "1"

msgid "IDS_PZSD610_YEAR"
msgstr "1945"

msgid "IDS_PZSD718"
msgstr "Ship Smasha"

msgid "IDS_PZSD718_DESCR"
msgstr "Orks do not navigate the Warp. They need no Astronomican like pesky Umiez. Gork and Mork guide them to WAAAGH! They jump into Warp and then jump out of it, Gunz blazing. But not with this sleek little ship. Where did the Boyz get this one? Was it assembled from parts of some primordial space hulk? Or just found by chance in some forgotten nooks and crannies of the Galaxy? They don’t remember and they don’t care. It has always been there, the only ship you can talk to. You tell it where to go, and you go there. But you must be smart to do so. Not Mekboy smart. Not Weirdboy smart. Real smart."

msgid "IDS_PZSD718_FULL"
msgstr "Ship Smasha"

msgid "IDS_PZSD718_NUMBER"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PZSD718_YEAR"
msgstr " "

msgid "IDS_PZSS508"
msgstr "Pasopati"

msgid "IDS_PZSS508_DESCR"
msgstr "<p>S-79 is a Soviet Project 613 submarine, built at Plant No. 444 named after Andre Marty in Nikolaev. In the summer of 1954, it was transferred to the Northern Fleet, and in 1955, via the Northern Sea Route, it moved from Polyarny to the Far East. In the late 1950s – early 1960s, the submarine supported the combat training of surface ships of the Pacific Fleet.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In 1959, it sailed to Indonesia, where it was transferred to the Indonesian Navy and renamed Pasopati. In 1961–62, the submarine participated in the Indonesian Operation Trikora to annex the western part of the island of New Guinea. In 1994, Pasopati was decommissioned and installed as a museum ship in Surabaya.</p>"

msgid "IDS_PZSS508_FULL"
msgstr "Pasopati"

msgid "IDS_PZSS508_NUMBER"
msgstr "236"

msgid "IDS_PZSS508_YEAR"
msgstr "1954"

