msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_GLOBAL_0"
msgstr "<p>By June 1941, the Northern Fleet consisted of 18 surface ships, 15 submarines, and 116 naval aircraft. Most of the ships were based in Polyarny, not far from Murmansk.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Even before the start of the war, Luftwaffe aircraft conducted reconnaissance over Soviet territory, so the enemy was aware of the location of many strategically important facilities and naval bases.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>On June 22, German aviation carried out a bombing raid on the Polyarny area. Later, the Northern Fleet's sea lines of communication and airfields were attacked. On June 29, enemy troops launched an offensive in the Murmansk direction.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_GLOBAL_1"
msgstr "<p>The Northern Fleet played a crucial role in the battle for the Arctic: it supported troops with amphibious landings and ensured maritime supply. The fleet's forces protected strategically important convoys and struck enemy ships, vessels, and bases. After the liberation of the Arctic, the Northern Fleet continued to protect Soviet and allied vessels from attacks by the latest German submarines.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Northern Fleet celebrated the victory in the Great Patriotic War with a large-scale parade in the Kola Bay.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP01_0"
msgstr "<p>In 1942, a squadron of heavy German ships was formed in Trondheim, Norway, to intercept communications between the Soviet army and the Allies in the Barents Sea.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>On July 5th, the German squadron, consisting of a battleship, two heavy cruisers, and several destroyers, left port to attack an Arctic convoy.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>That same day, the enemy ship formation was detected by the crew of the Soviet submarine K-21. Approaching the squadron, the submarine attacked the battleship Tirpitz with four torpedoes.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP01_1"
msgstr "<p>On July 4, while en route to Arkhangelsk, the Allied convoy PQ-17 received an order from British command to scatter. This directive was prompted by inaccurate intelligence and fear of an attack by the German battleship Tirpitz.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Under the conditions of the polar day, the scattered ships became easy prey for German submarines and aircraft. The majority of the convoy's vessels were destroyed.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The remaining ships, hiding from enemy attacks amidst the ice, continued their journey to Soviet ports. On July 8, six transports, three corvettes, two anti-aircraft ships, three minesweepers, and a rescue vessel carrying the crews of six sunken ships entered the Matochkin Shar Strait. Later, the tanker \"Donbass\" was brought to the Yokanga base. Two more transports were met by ships of the Northern Fleet in Svyatonossky Bay.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The last vessel of the convoy reached port only on July 28.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP01_2"
msgstr "<p>In August 1942, Germany launched Operation \"Wunderland\" with the aim of blocking the Northern Sea Route to Allied convoys. For this purpose, the heavy German cruiser Admiral Scheer was dispatched to the Kara Sea.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>On August 25, the icebreaker steamship \"Alexander Sibiryakov\" spotted the enemy vessel near the Nordenskiöld Archipelago. Despite the American flag being raised on the cruiser, the icebreaker's crew grew suspicious and reported the ship over the radio. After this, communication with the \"Sibiryakov\" was lost.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Thanks to the icebreaker's warning, the nearest convoy to the battle site changed course, and the port of Dikson, which the German cruiser intended to destroy, managed to prepare for the attack and put up unexpected resistance.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP01_3"
msgstr "<p>In 1942, the enemy's primary objective was to restrict the movement of Arctic convoys along the Northern Sea Route. To achieve this, the German army increased its air and naval forces based in Northern Norway. However, despite the losses, the supply of equipment and strategic resources to the Soviet Union continued.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The forces of the Northern Fleet participated in battles not only at sea but also on land. For instance, to support the 14th Army during the Murmansk offensive operation and to conduct sabotage behind enemy lines, the Northern Fleet landed several amphibious assaults in the area of Cape Pikshuev.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP02_0"
msgstr "<p>In 1943, the Soviet command decided to reinforce the Northern Fleet with submarines from the non-combatant Pacific Fleet and transfer six submarines to Polyarny: L-15, L-16, S-51, S-54, S-55, and S-56. During the transit, the submarines had to navigate through the waters of three oceans and nine seas.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>On September 25, the first Leninets-class submarines — L-15 and L-16 — departed from Petropavlovsk-Kamchatsky, but a few days later, L-16 was sunk by an unknown submarine in the Pacific Ocean. The remaining submarines began their voyage in early October.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In January 1944, the first submarine arrived in the Kola Bay: S-51 significantly outpaced its comrades, completing the entire journey in less than four months.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP02_1"
msgstr "<p>On January 20, the destroyer leader Baku and the destroyer Razumny left the Kola Bay to intercept an enemy convoy traveling east from Tromsø.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>On the night of January 21, the Soviet ships discovered two transports escorted by two vessels near Botsfjord and began their attack. They had to operate almost blindly, and furthermore, the destroyers came under fire from coastal batteries and enemy escort ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After several minutes, the firing ceased. Baku and Razumny returned to base undamaged, reporting hits on a German warship and a transport vessel.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP02_2"
msgstr "<p>Soviet submarines made a tremendous contribution to the activities of the Northern Fleet. They laid minefields, attacked German transports, and destroyed enemy submarines.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>On August 28th, near Cape Zhelaniya, the Soviet submarine S-101 detected the German submarine U-639 and attacked it, firing three torpedoes from its bow tubes. The salvo was accurate, and within seconds the enemy submarine was sunk.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP02_3"
msgstr "<p>In 1943, the Northern Fleet was significantly reinforced. At the same time, the enemy's Norwegian grouping of ships and aircraft also increased. The situation required the improvement of naval combat tactics.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The Northern Fleet continued to support the coastal flank of the 14th Army and supplied the troops by sea. Surface ships, aircraft, and submarines protected Soviet sea communications and operated on enemy supply lines.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP03_0"
msgstr "<p>The Northern Fleet was assigned the task of conducting Operation RV-1 from January 16 to 26 with the aim of destroying one or more enemy convoys in the Hammerfest — Kirkenes — Petsamo route.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The operation involved naval aviation, submarines, destroyers, torpedo boats, and coastal batteries. All these forces were to act in concert within their pre-assigned zones, guide each other toward the enemy, and deliver coordinated strikes against the convoys along their entire route.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP03_1"
msgstr "<p>On September 29, the 14th Army of the Karelian Front was tasked with launching an offensive and clearing the Petsamo region of enemy forces.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>To support the offensive, the command of the Northern Fleet planned Operation \"West,\" which involved actions in maritime and coastal directions.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>In the coastal direction, the fleet's forces were to breach and destroy the enemy's defenses between the mainland and the Sredny Peninsula and support the ground forces by landing troops on the flanks and rear.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP03_2"
msgstr "<p>The main objective of the naval component of Operation \"West\" was to prevent the evacuation of German troops from Northern Finland to Norway through the ports of the Varangerfjord. The execution of this plan again involved the joint actions of various naval forces in predetermined sectors.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>Naval aviation operated along the line Kirkenes — Hammerfest; torpedo boats — in the area of the Varanger Fjord; submarines — from Vardø to the 20°00' meridian. Destroyers lay in wait for enemy convoys between Vardø and Nordkyn Cape.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP03_3"
msgstr "<p>In 1944, through the joint efforts of the Northern Fleet and the Karelian Front, the Soviet Arctic was liberated. Germany's heavy industry ceased to receive valuable nickel and iron ore from the Petsamo region, and the German navy lost its bases in the Barents Sea.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>After the defeat of the German army in the Arctic, the Northern Fleet continued to protect external and internal sea communications, as the enemy still penetrated Soviet waters.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The ship composition of the Northern Fleet increased. Its ranks were temporarily supplemented by the battleship Royal Sovereign and the cruiser Milwaukee, transferred by the Allies, which were renamed \"Arkhangelsk\" and \"Murmansk.\"</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP0_0"
msgstr "<p>On July 5, the commander of the 52nd Rifle Division requested the commander of the Northern Fleet to assist the flank of the ground forces by landing troops on the southern shore of Bolshaya Zapadnaya Litsa Bay.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The operation began on the night of July 6. The ships carrying the landing force proceeded using a \"flow\" method—one by one—which ensured better concealment under the conditions of the polar day. Motorboats were used as landing craft, and the landing itself was conducted on an unprepared shore to avoid shelling at the pier.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The main objective of the operation was achieved: the landing force destroyed enemy groups and linked up with units of the rifle division.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP0_1"
msgstr "<p>The enemy's nearest seaport to the front line became the harbor of Liinakhamari. It was there that the supply transports for the German army were concentrated.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>On August 21, 1941, despite the approaches to the port being covered by batteries and anti-submarine patrols, the submarine M-172 broke through into the Petsamonvuono fjord, after which it entered the harbor and attacked an enemy transport ship anchored in the roadstead.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP0_2"
msgstr "<p>On November 24 and 25, a squadron of ships, consisting of the British light cruiser Kenya, the destroyers Bedouin and Intrepid, as well as the Soviet destroyers Gremyashchiy and Gromkiy, patrolled the northeastern coast of Norway in search of enemy ships.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>During the return voyage, the allied ships jointly attacked the fortress, port, and coastal batteries of Vardø Island, which served as a naval base for the German fleet.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The standard ammunition expenditure was 120 main caliber shells per ship.</p>"

msgid "IDS_TERRITORY_ARCHIVE_TEXT_MAP0_3"
msgstr "<p>By the autumn of 1941, the advance of the German-Finnish troops in the Arctic had been thwarted by the forces of the 14th Army of the Karelian Front and the Northern Fleet. The invasion of the Murmansk region was halted, but combat operations in the north continued.</p><p class=\"p_spacing\"> </p><p>The most important task for the Soviet army was to prevent the enemy from breaking through to the sea: due to the weak development of the highway and railway network in the Arctic, sea routes were of key importance for communications. The forces of the Northern Fleet impeded the supply of enemy troops, as well as the export of nickel and iron ore to Germany.</p>"

